/* Machine translations of Logik Dohna-Wundlacken (uncorrected OCR), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['dohna/kemp'] = {"dohna-s02-001":["[First hour, 23rd April, from 7 — 8]",[]],"dohna-s02-002":["Prolegomena",[]],"dohna-s02-003":["[All things between heaven and earth happen and act according to rules, e.g. bodies according to the laws of gravity.]",["OCR 'zAvischen' corrected to 'zwischen'."]],"dohna-s02-004":["We must reflect upon our thinking, that is, do so according to rules. Every language is bound to certain particular rules. This holds especially of dead languages, where the rules can properly be specified. But the rules can also be used without our actually naming them. These rules we learn [*exercitat*] gradually, by trial. The first attempts are faulty; in the end we arrive at proficiency. Among the rules of thinking there are general rules, which apply without distinction to particular objects. So too there are general rules of language. A grammar of this kind contains no words, no *copia vocabularum*, but only the form of language. We shall be able to conceive a general doctrine [of thinking]. This general doctrine of thinking is entitled logic, the doctrine of the understanding. It is a preparation for thinking about objects.",["OCR 'Regem' corrected to 'Regeln'.","'(des Denkens)' is rendered in brackets as the sense requires; the German has parentheses. The sentence 'Wir werden uns vorstellen können eine allgemeine Lehre' is kept close to the German word order loosely, as the transcript is rough.","'Verstandeslehre' rendered 'doctrine of the understanding'."]],"dohna-s02-005":["Understanding is the faculty of thinking (of understanding something), [the faculty] of rules; judgment [*Urteilskraft*] is the faculty of subsuming; reason, that of inferring (of having insight into something) what belongs thereto. A rule is a concept under which a manifold, a multiplicity of representations, is contained.",["The OCR brace '{etwas' is read as an opening parenthesis. The construction is elliptical; I supplied '[the faculty]' and left 'what belongs thereto' (was dahin gehört) as ambiguous in reference, probably what falls under the rule.","Urteilskraft rendered 'judgment' (faculty of judgment)."]],"dohna-s02-006":["We can distinguish:",[]],"dohna-s02-007":["1. the necessary [*necess.*] general rules of thinking, without distinction as to what the thinking is directed upon. If they were not general, they would not be necessary.",[]],"dohna-s02-008":["[2. the contingent rules.]",["'die zufälligen' is rendered 'contingent rules', the noun being understood from the preceding item."]],"dohna-s02-009":["These rules concern only the form of all rules of thinking.",[]],"dohna-s02-010":["[E.g. If men are mortal, then they have also (at one time) been well [?]. If the one proposition is true, so is the other. Such a judgment is a hypothetical, conditioned judgment.]",["The OCR 'wohl t gewesen' is garbled; the example as it stands is unintelligible, and I translate only what can be made out. Perhaps 'wohl' belongs to a word such as 'geboren' or the like; I do not conjecture."]],"dohna-s02-011":["The content of a mode of knowledge [*Erkenntnis*] is the object or the matter, and is distinguished from the form. A general logic must abstract from all objects of thinking.",["'Erkenntnis' in the singular is rendered 'a mode of knowledge', as the sense is a particular cognition."]],"dohna-s02-012":["The necessary general rules of thinking must further be derived *a priori*, not from experience. Empirical is entitled whatever comes from experience (*ex cognitione oriuntur*), and is opposed to the (transcendental) knowledge *a priori*. It is true that empirical acquaintance is called in to assist the latter. But it is never good; it is always better if one knows everything from reason.",["'ex cognitione oriuntur' kept as in the text; one would expect 'ex experientia', but I do not emend. Possibly a transcriber's slip.","'Kenntnis' (acquaintance) is distinguished from 'Erkenntnis' (knowledge). 'dem letztern' taken to refer to knowledge *a priori*."]],"dohna-s02-013":["The logical rules are not those according to which we think, but those according to which we ought to think. [Logic contains no matter at all, only the form of thinking.] Logic must contain principles *a priori*. [Hence logic is a science, and grammar is not, because its rules are contingent.] Consequently any logic in which rules drawn from experience occur is no longer pure. [The rules concerning what is sensibly perfect are contained in aesthetics.]",["'Ästhetik' rendered 'aesthetics'; 'das sinnlich Vollkommene' as 'what is sensibly perfect'."]],"dohna-s02-014":["Universal is what is general according to reason; empirically general only in so far as it is always so in experience. *Paedeutica* (training of children) [TrouSsuTiKri]. A doctrine of practice [*Übungslehre*] is no longer pure logic, but only the finer exercise and application of it. [The distinct concept is that whose marks I am conscious of. Without these general rules (even if one is not conscious of them) thinking in general is not possible; hence logic is the propaedeutic of all the sciences, the supreme touchstone that something accords with the laws of the understanding.] All psychological observations must be excluded from (pure) logic. All rules that are demonstrable in logic at all are still always in need of a ground from which they are derived. Many propositions (e.g. that of contradiction) cannot be proved at all, either *a priori* or empirically.",["'TrouSsuTiKri' is garbled OCR, presumably Greek (παιδευτική); left as it stands.","'Probierstein' rendered 'touchstone'; 'Propädeutik' as 'propaedeutic'.","'lassen sich gar nicht weder ... noch' is a double negative in the German; rendered as a simple negation."]],"dohna-s02-015":["[Is there a natural logic? *Logica est scientia. Omnis scientia vero est artificialis. Ergo —*]",["The argument breaks off at 'Ergo —' in the source."]],"dohna-s02-016":["A proof that is made wholly *a priori* [at the same time intuitively, with insight into the necessity] is called demonstration. What, then, is not capable <(*dignum*) of demonstration is empirical.",["The '<' before '(dignum)' is apparatus or OCR residue; the closing bracket is missing. I carry it over and the reading remains uncertain: probably 'keiner Demonstration fähig (dignum) ist'."]],"dohna-s02-017":["We can consider this science as to whether it is doctrine or criticism [— concerned with use —] or both together.",["'Kritik' rendered 'criticism' (the judging of what is given), not in the sense of the Critical Philosophy."]],"dohna-s02-018":["[Logic as doctrine may be entitled a canon of the understanding and of reason. A canon is demonstrated criticism (in Greek it signifies: rule).]",[]],"dohna-s02-019":["Logic is doctrine; it puts rules into our hands (*praebet*); it is demonstrated theory.",["OCR '«(praebet)' read as '(praebet)'."]],"dohna-s02-020":["Doctrine [is a sum-total of rules in which the rules must precede the product (criticism is the use of judgment, where the product precedes the rule)] contains the ground of judging whether something is true or false. It can have a twofold use: first merely as criticism, secondly as organon. In the first case, e.g. the doctrine of taste is merely criticism [serves to judge something, e.g. a poem]; it does not teach us to make anything ourselves with taste. [Criticism requires that the product should already be there, even if the technical rules can be given beforehand.]",["'Urteilskraft' rendered 'judgment'. 'Organon' left in its Greek-Latin form. 'Doktrin ... enthält' is syntactically broken by the long bracketed insertion; I kept the order and did not smooth it, to keep the bracket as Academy apparatus.","'Das erstemal' rendered 'in the first case'."]],"dohna-s02-021":["Mathematics is doctrine, but at the same time an organon [through which a mode of knowledge becomes possible as regards its content] of great extent, namely, e.g. arithmetic. Is logic also an organon? No; it serves only for criticism, like grammar (which has much in common with logic), from which one cannot properly learn the language; for that a wealth of words is also required.",["OCR 'nänilich' corrected to 'nämlich'.","'Erkenntnis' rendered 'a mode of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s02-022":["Doctrine is demonstrated discipline [institution, principle of production]. Logic does not extend to an organon; it has no objects. If, however, a logic is nevertheless misused as an organon, it is called dialectic. [The art of speaking for and against (*pro et contra*) on every object. Unintentionally reason can deceive itself when it transgresses the laws of logic.] But if it is to serve merely for judging, it is entitled analytic, or logic of truth. Dialectic, on the other hand, is only the logic of illusion.",["OCR 'Un vorsätzlich' read as 'Unvorsätzlich' (unintentionally); the sense fits the context, but the printed reading is uncertain.","'Schein' rendered 'illusion'."]],"dohna-s02-023":["[The use of logic is analytic if it is used only as canon, dialectical if it is also used as organon; it is then the logic of illusion and deceives us. Logic as canon is entitled analytic (logic of truth). Zeno of Elea, a great dialectician.]",[]],"dohna-s03-001":["[2nd hour]",[]],"dohna-s03-002":["To think is entitled to represent something to oneself in a concept. Logic may be entitled doctrine of reason [*Vernunftlehre*], and not merely rational doctrine [*vernünftige Lehre*] [a science of reason not according to matter, because its object is human reason alone. Mathematics, physics, morals are indeed also sciences of reason, but only according to form, not according to matter.] Logic occupies itself only with the formal rules of thinking. [The object is the matter, the subject the form.]",["The German distinction 'Vernunftlehre / vernünftige Lehre' is kept in brackets as the sense turns on it.","The closing bracket after 'vernünftige Lehre' is absent in the source before the opening bracket; carried over as it stands."]],"dohna-s03-003":["Can instruction in the fine arts be doctrine? Their rules can serve only for judging. Aesthetics permits no doctrine, only a criticism. Sciences, however, permit doctrine. As touchstone of the correctness of the judgment of taste the fine art has no rules, whereas doctrine has rules.",["'Kritik' rendered 'criticism'; 'Ästhetik' as 'aesthetics'."]],"dohna-s03-004":["By barbarism is understood not only the onset of complete ignorance, but also, above all, the corruption of taste. This we should have to fear, if we did not still possess such splendid models from antiquity. [Namely poets and fine minds, e.g. Horace, etc. Taste is to be formed and maintained only by examples, not by rules. Remark: Polyclitus, a famous sculptor of antiquity, made a statue that was afterwards always used as a model. The archetype (*Archetypon*, original image) lies in every one's head, and what is produced must approximate to it.]",["'Archetypon, Urbild' kept in Greek form with the German in apposition. 'was sich ihm nähern muß' is ambiguous in its antecedent; I supplied 'what is produced' by judgment."]],"dohna-s03-005":["Fine art, then, requires models (*exemplar*); not so science, which requires rules. A doctrine, in so far as it can be demonstrated, is entitled a canon. Logic is a canon, not merely criticism; it is also only a means of judging. [A canon is an inviolable law. Hence it could not be applied to taste, where everything depends on custom and on the pleasure of others.]",["'nicht allein Kritik, sie ist auch nur Mittel der Beurteilung' is awkward in the German and possibly a transcription slip; I translate it as it stands.","'Gewohnheit' rendered 'custom'."]],"dohna-s03-006":["Is logic as canon also a means to the acquisition of science? No. Logic abstracts from all content, and so also from all knowledge, [it only brings it about that our thinking is correct] and it is no organon. But mathematics is not only a canon but also a splendid organon. [Yes, certainly the greatest, for it is concerned with matter, with the measurement of time and space.] Mathematics belongs also to natural science.",["Wissenschaft rendered 'science', as the context (mathematics, natural science) suits."]],"dohna-s03-007":["Logic is now divided:",[]],"dohna-s03-008":["1. into *logica naturalis* (natural) and (only a complement),",["The parenthesis 'and (only a complement)' is left as it stands in the source; its reference is unclear."]],"dohna-s03-009":["2. into *logica artificialis* (artificial). This is the logic which is an actually explicit, not implicit, use of our understanding. [At bottom there is only an artificial logic, for a natural logic at bottom does not exist — natural grammar.]",["The final dash and 'natural grammar' are kept as in the source; the sense is elliptical."]],"dohna-s03-010":["It is expounded 1. scientifically, as a science is always expounded; 2. scholastically-popularly, in school fashion [schulgerecht], adapted to the subjective ground of each person's faculty of knowledge. [Popularity detracts from perfection.] All science must be expounded in school fashion. This is a consequence of perfection.",["'Erkenntnisvermögen' rendered 'faculty of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s03-011":["Now we can divide logic:",[]],"dohna-s03-012":["1. into the logic of sound understanding, *sensus communis*,",[]],"dohna-s03-013":["2. into the logic of speculative understanding, which gives rules *in abstracto*.",[]],"dohna-s03-014":["Common understanding is the faculty of being able to judge according to rules *in concreto*. [Not the 'plain' understanding. This word is absurd.] Common understanding is entitled sound when it is correct. Morals must be known wholly *in concreto*, but physics has also *abstracta*.",["'schlichte' rendered 'plain'; the antecedent of 'Nicht der schlichte' is supplied by me as 'understanding'."]],"dohna-s03-015":["A logic is to be expounded *in abstracto*. As regards its content, it is a propaedeutic [preparatory exercise for sciences] of all sciences, e.g. in respect of physics and morals.",["The sentence is kept as it stands, though 'was ihren Inhalt anbetrifft' is odd for a formal logic; possibly a transcript slip."]],"dohna-s04-001":["[Third hour, 26 April 1792]",[]],"dohna-s04-002":["Can it also be (regarded as) a propaedeutic of philosophy? No. For it is itself a part of philosophy [and indeed the part with which we ought to begin], as we shall soon hear.",[]],"dohna-s04-003":["All our knowledge is either (according to its mode of acquisition)",[]],"dohna-s04-004":["1. rational, in so far as it is derived *a priori* from reason (e.g. pure mathematics, metaphysics),",[]],"dohna-s04-005":["2. historical; as such it is:",["Restated 'als solches ist es' loosely, as a clause."]],"dohna-s04-006":["a) subjectively historical, when the knowledge in the subject rests on empirical grounds;",[]],"dohna-s04-007":["b) objectively historical, when the ground of knowledge can never be other than historical. Modes of knowledge that are <(objectively) rational can nevertheless be subjectively historical. [Logic itself is a system. The modes of knowledge conforming to it are partly systematic, partly fragmentary. — Opposed to methodical knowledge is the tumultuary.] In mathematics there can be a subjectively historical knowledge, even if it is certainly objectively rational; this occurs especially in land-surveying. Also, unfortunately, in natural religion [which many believe merely from lazy trust in the reason of others, so as not to rack their brains. If they then forget the words, the thing too is forgotten.]",["The unclosed '<(' before 'objectively' is carried over as printed (OCR/apparatus); the sense is clear.","'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'; 'Erkenntnisgrund' 'ground of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s04-008":["[What is philosophy?] All rational knowledge is either 1. one through concepts, or 2. one through construction of concepts. The latter is called presenting a concept, *exhibere*. This is rational knowledge only in so far as the concepts correspond with one another {in intuition} *a priori*. A rational knowledge through concepts is entitled philosophical. Thus a system through concepts is philosophy.",["The curly brace '{' before 'in der Anschauung' is OCR for '(' ; I keep the sense with braces as printed."]],"dohna-s04-009":["[To construct a concept is to give it a corresponding intuition *a priori*.] A rational knowledge through construction of concepts is entitled mathematical. Thus a system [of such knowledge is] mathematics.",["'ihni' read as 'ihm' (OCR). 'of such knowledge is' is supplied for the elliptical 'Also ein System Mathematik'."]],"dohna-s04-010":["A system must always be thought through: a manifold in connection, a combination of modes of knowledge from a common principle. [It must be that.] Hence we can learn much geography, but not in a system, only as an aggregate. The philosopher can be regarded as an artist of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*] and as a teacher of wisdom. Philosophy (in the practical sense) is the science of the ultimate ends of human reason. Philosophy as art of reason is called speculative, as doctrine of wisdom practical. The ancients always called philosophy the doctrine of wisdom.",["Source has 'durchdacht ... [sein]', with 'sein' supplied by the editor; I restated the sentence so that 'must be' governs the whole list, and the bracketed 'It must be that' reflects the editor's supplied 'sein'.","'<lm praktischen Sinn)' read as '(im praktischen Sinn)'."]],"dohna-s05-001":["[Fourth hour, the 27th]",[]],"dohna-s05-002":["A distinction can be made between the two expressions 'learning philosophy' and 'learning to philosophize'. Learning means imitating the judgments of others, and is thus very different from one's own reflection. [Not to learn philosophy but to learn to philosophize, otherwise it remains mere imitation — but to arrive at it oneself through exercise of the understanding, that is what matters. Only he who is capable of the independent use of his reason is entitled a philosopher. Philosophy is scientific knowledge *in abstracto* — it is itself a science.] Learning to philosophize means that one must learn to use one's reason oneself; and here a use of both sound and speculative reason takes place. We must exercise it in concepts. Practical knowledge [is knowledge] in so far as our understanding aims at cultivation and end; and practical reason is the ultimate end of human | wisdom]. Philosophy as doctrine of wisdom presupposes a teacher of wisdom. The ancients required of a teacher of wisdom that he be also practically a wise man. [Wisdom is agreement with the final ends of all things. All other modes of knowledge have their utility only as means — only philosophy leads to the ultimate final end, wisdom. (The doctrine of the highest good.) Philosophy — love of wisdom.] The Cynics [the name derives from Kynosarges, a dwelling-place where they usually assembled] took, as they called it, the short way to happiness, by exercising themselves to be able to do without everything.",["The sentence 'Praktische Erkenntnis, sofern ...' is elliptical; I supplied 'is knowledge' in brackets. Bildung rendered 'cultivation'.","The passage 'menschlicher | Weisheit]' has a stray closing bracket and a page-break sign; carried over as printed, and the text may be corrupt here.","'Hausstätte' translated as 'dwelling-place'; the reading is uncertain (possibly an OCR or transcript slip)."]],"dohna-s05-003":["Now we shall say something of philosophy as the sum total of items of information [*Kenntnisse*] and of its history.",["'Kenntnisse' is distinguished from 'Erkenntnis' (knowledge) by rendering it 'items of information'."]],"dohna-s05-004":["The first philosophers were poets. For time was needed to find words for abstract concepts; hence at first supersensible thoughts were represented under sensible images. Pherecydes was the first to write philosophy in prose. [The founder of the Ionian school is Thales. To the Eleatic belong Parmenides and Xenophanes. Because of the poverty of language one could at that time philosophize only in poetry.]",["'WTorte' read as 'Worte' (OCR)."]],"dohna-s05-005":["The most famous sect is the Platonic, then the Aristotelian. The latter were called Peripatetici [they stayed in the Lyceum, which was a colonnade where teaching was done *ambulando*], the first Academics. The Stoics (under Zeno of Citium) had their name from στοά, *porticus*. The Epicureans — (*horti*) [Epicurus taught in them].",["'Diese' resolved to the Aristotelians, as the sense requires. 'die erstem Akademiker' read as 'die ersten Akademiker'; its connection with the Peripatetics is doubtful (perhaps 'the former' is meant, or a transcript slip).","'crroa' read as στοά (OCR)."]],"dohna-s05-006":["The so-called Academy (that is, the school of Plato) was at first wholly dogmatic, but afterwards degenerated into a mania for doubt, scepticism. Carneades cannot be regarded as the founder of a new school, but only as an extraordinary orator. Arcesilas, however, founded a new one.",[]],"dohna-s05-007":["[Anaxagoras was the first philosopher who also taught theology.] The ancients divided the whole of philosophy into three parts, namely logic, physics and ethics. It remains only to consider whether a dichotomy would not here be better than a trichotomy. [Practical | philosophy |: 1. ethical — Socrates never gave himself out as a philosopher; his fragmentary teachings Plato brought into a system. 2. political, rules of prudence — Pythagoras. In regard to him everything still lies in darkness. A member of the subdivision comes into the superior division.]",["'Trichometrie' read as 'Trichotomie' (apparently a slip in the source or OCR).","The bracketed note is fragmentary; the placement of the names after 'ethische' and 'politisch' is as in the source, and the dash is my punctuation of an unclear connection."]],"dohna-s05-008":["We have now divided philosophy, strictly speaking, as follows:",[]],"dohna-s05-009":["1. into the formal, which is logic.",[]],"dohna-s05-010":["2. into the material. This can be divided into the theoretical and the practical.",[]],"dohna-s05-011":["Ethics has in more recent times been called the doctrine of virtue. Physics too can be considered in two ways.",[]],"dohna-s05-012":["1. as *physica rationalis*, pure doctrine of nature [pure is the opposite of empirical]; this has been entitled metaphysics since Aristotle.",[]],"dohna-s05-013":["2. as *physica materialis* or as metaphysics and empirical physics. All knowledge is pure if no influence of empirical information [*Kenntnisse*] is present in it.",[]],"dohna-s05-014":["The *physica empirica* can again be divided",[]],"dohna-s05-015":["1. into the doctrine of bodies, *physica specialis* †, and",["The 'f' after 'specialis' is an OCR mark, probably a footnote sign or stray character; rendered as †, doubtful."]],"dohna-s05-016":["2. into the doctrine of the soul, *psychologia specialis* †.",["'ps}rchologia' read as 'psychologia' (OCR); the trailing 'f' as in the preceding passage."]],"dohna-s05-017":["If we regard philosophy as the sum-total of several sciences, we must first look at the so-called seven liberal arts: 1. grammar, 2. rhetoric, 3. dialectic, 4. arithmetic, 5. music, 6. geometry, 7. astronomy. [This division was made by Hrabanus Maurus (in the time of Charlemagne) for the sake of theology. — At that time all the sciences were divided into a) the higher faculty: 1. the preservation of salvation, 2. of freedom and property, 3. life and health, in general the *esse*; b) the *melius esse*, the lower faculty.]",["'Wenn Mir' is an OCR slip for 'Wenn wir'; corrected silently.","The sentence on the faculties is compressed and its construction is uncertain; I have kept its enumeration and supplied nothing beyond 'the preservation of' repeated by sense. 'a)' and 'b)' are set apart by me from the OCR 'a)' and 'e;b)'.","*esse* and *melius esse* left in Latin as technical terms."]],"dohna-s06-001":["[Fifth hour, 30th April] *Tractatio ipsa*",["Latin left in italics: 'the treatment itself'."]],"dohna-s06-002":["Logic is the science which contains the formal rules and principles of thinking. It is usually divided:",[]],"dohna-s06-003":["1. into the theoretical,",[]],"dohna-s06-004":["2. into the practical. But there is no such logic, for in that case it would have to be applied to objects, which does not take place at all, since it contains only the formal rules of thinking.",["The German sentence is run together; I have divided it for clarity."]],"dohna-s06-005":["It can therefore rather be | divided | as follows:",["The signs | are the Academy apparatus, carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s06-006":["1. into the doctrine of elements, which contains the rules in general,",[]],"dohna-s06-007":["2. into the doctrine of method, which contains the principles of science.",[]],"dohna-s06-008":["The doctrine of method is the sum-total of items of cognition [Kenntnisse], in so far as they are made into a system [— it contains a guide to the way in which a system of knowledge [Erkenntnis] is to be obtained]. It constitutes the so-called practical part.",["Kenntnisse is distinguished from Erkenntnis; 'items of cognition' is my choice for the former."]],"dohna-s06-009":["The most notable phenomenon in the history of logic is that it was at strife with itself as to whether any knowledge whatever is certain. Those who believed that it could be completely proved were entitled dogmatists; the doubters, however, sceptics. [The sceptics wished to prove that certainty can be obtained about nothing at all, and so fell into contradiction with themselves — a *Kathartikon* [purgative] which cancels itself. — The acuteness with which they attacked the Scholastics is to be admired.] In the end they were a useful sect. [Pyrrho was the oldest of all sceptics — afterwards Arcesilas, Carneades.]",["'Kathartikon' kept in Greek-derived form; the gloss 'purgative' in brackets is mine.","'Logik' as subject of 'stritt' is odd; I have kept 'it' referring to logic as the text has it."]],"dohna-s06-010":["We have no one who has surpassed Aristotle or has added to his <pure> logic (which is, in fact, intrinsically impossible), just as no mathematician has surpassed Euclid.",["Angle brackets are the editors' and are kept."]],"dohna-s06-011":["| [Something on the history of logic. Of all the peoples of the East [orientalischen Völker], the Greeks have the greatest merit in the cultivation of philosophy. Their study afterwards almost wholly declined in the Middle Ages, until in the twelfth century the Scholastics arose in Paris. All their distinctions really concerned theology only, whence also the many useless rules and barbarous expressions. In the end relief was found by drawing forth the old classics from the scholastic rubbish and the corruption of language [Sprach-Verderbnis] that had (resulted) from it. — Locke became famous through his *Essay concerning Human Understanding*; he speaks in it of the origin of concepts, which, however, properly belongs not to logic but to metaphysics. The result of his investigations: everything derives from experience. But it does not at all follow from this that concepts can (only) be exhibited in experience. — Leibniz now entered the lists. Although he wrote no logic properly so called, he did much to clarify concepts (he wrote in defence of his countrymen against the Englishman Locke). He expressed in his works ideas which subsequently moved Wolff to his systematic logic. A detailed account of this is found in Reusch, *Systema logicae*, and in Corvinus. Crusius gives the understanding much nourishment.] |",["'orientalischen Völker' is as printed; the sense (Greeks among the 'oriental' peoples) is odd but kept.","OCR 'eigentHch' corrected to 'eigentlich'.","Locke's title given in English; the German has 'Versuch über den menschlichen Verstand'."]],"dohna-s06-012":["A principal point in logic will rest on the criterion of truth. Now we begin.",[]],"dohna-s07-001":["I. The Doctrine of Elements",[]],"dohna-s07-002":["[It deals with the concepts of the understanding in general. The division of all thinking — which has various forms that can be exhausted and brought into a system. The general element which underlies all knowledge is representation — an indefinable fundamental concept. Knowledge is the relation of representation to an object — combined with an *actio* in the mind — consciousness (representation of our representation), which obscure representations lack.]",["OCR 'Hegt' read as 'liegt'.","'actio' left in Latin."]],"dohna-s07-003":["The ancients divided it into 1. *apprehensio simplex*, 2. *judicium* and 3. *ratiocinium*. Two elements belong to our knowledge, intuition and concept. Representations can also be referred to something other than knowledge, namely to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure (the way in which we are affected by things). All our intuition belongs to sensibility, every concept to the understanding. Sensibility contains two faculties, sense and imagination [this is the magic power of the human mind], or the faculty of intuition of an object in so far as it is not present; sense, however, is that faculty in so far as the object is present.",["'sie' in 'teilten sie' has no clear antecedent; I take it to mean thinking, as in the preceding entry, and say 'it'.","The final clause is elliptical; I have supplied 'faculty' and 'is that faculty' to complete the sense."]],"dohna-s07-004":["[All our representation is: 1. sensation [Empfindung] — its relation to the subject, 2. knowledge — relation to the object. For example, 1. pleasure — lies merely in me — not in the object.]",["'Beziehung derselben aufs Subjekt' with 'derselben' referring to representation."]],"dohna-s08-001":["On the Consciousness of Representation, or Apperception",[]],"dohna-s08-002":["This is the representative faculty [Vorstellungsvermögen] in relation to an object [which belongs to our state], which at apprehension is not yet there at all, but is, as it were, produced (grasped). In the thought that man can say 'I am' there lies an extraordinary amount. Consciousness of our concepts is always difficult. [It is especially required for this. For lack of consciousness animals too are incapable of concepts — they have intuition. — Consciousness is a quite separate dimension of the faculty of knowledge (hence there is no gradation from animals to man).] The concept contains that which is common to many (different) representations. A concept is entitled:",["'Es wird vorzüglich dazu erfordert' is elliptical ('dazu' unspecified, presumably for concepts); translated literally.","OCR '{(verschiedenen))' read as '(verschiedenen)'."]],"dohna-s08-003":["clear, if one is conscious of it. (The savage would ask: What is a house?)",[]],"dohna-s08-004":["distinct, if one is conscious not only of the partial concepts but also of the total concepts. We need distinctness in all concepts which we wish to communicate to others. Distinctness cannot be demanded of any concept which contains no manifold (partial representations) in itself. For example, time. [In the case of this concept only clearness can be demanded — it is only a simple representation.]",["'Totalbegriffe' rendered literally; the sense (perhaps the whole concept) is uncertain."]],"dohna-s08-005":["If a representation which is clear as a whole is obscure *particulariter*, it is indistinct. [Indistinct representations are called confused. They come, however, properly from the weakness of the representation. But if there is order in the | representations |, it comes from confusion. Only if the representation is composite can it be confused.] Concepts of which one is not conscious are obscure. This holds, however, only of obscure representations. These are either:",["OCR 'ScliAväche' read as 'Schwäche'; '| Vorstellungen j,' read as '| Vorstellungen |,'.","The bracketed sentence on order and confusion is obscure and possibly corrupt in the transcript; translated as it stands.","'Es gilt aber nur von dunkeln Vorstellungen' is puzzling after 'Begriffe'; kept literally."]],"dohna-s08-006":["1. merely clear, and indeed either",[]],"dohna-s08-007":["a) simple or",[]],"dohna-s08-008":["b) composite, or",[]],"dohna-s08-009":["2. merely distinct. This distinctness is:",[]],"dohna-s08-010":["a) distinctness of intuition,",[]],"dohna-s08-011":["b) distinctness of concepts. (In the author only this occurs, but the corresponding intuition, presentation, *exhibitio*, is equally necessary.)",["'Autor' is Meier; *exhibitio* left in Latin."]],"dohna-s08-012":["[Order in representations and concepts is of the greatest importance. There are three relations:",[]],"dohna-s08-013":["1. of subject to predicate,",[]],"dohna-s08-014":["2. of the dividing members to the concept divided,",[]],"dohna-s08-015":["3. the relation of ground to consequence.]",[]],"dohna-s08-016":["Even philosophers have not succeeded in fully analysing the concept of virtue. One has it in oneself, but cannot give it out. —",[]],"dohna-s08-017":["[Sixth hour, 1st May]",[]],"dohna-s08-018":["§ 15 and § 16 in our author really do not belong to logic at all. [K 11. 12]",["'[K 11. 12]' is the editors' reference, kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s08-019":["§ 17. Rational knowledge [*das vernünftige Erkenntnis*] is a distinct way of knowing something from grounds. Strictly, knowledge from concepts is knowledge of the understanding. To think is to represent something to oneself discursively, as distinguished from intuition, which is to represent something to oneself intuitively. Reason is the faculty of inferring; this requires judgments, and these cannot take place without concepts. Receptivity is distinguished from spontaneity [self-activity]. Understanding is the higher faculty of knowledge. [For it is the faculty of rules, and rules are already concepts.] Thus we proceed from representations to concepts (*facultas concept . . .*), then to judgments, then to inferences.",["The sentence 'dazu gehören Urteile, und diese können nicht ohne Begriffe geschehen f.' ends in a stray 'f.' (OCR, or an abbreviation for 'etc.'); omitted as unreadable.","*Obererkenntnisvermögen* rendered 'higher faculty of knowledge' (Kant's *facultas cognoscitiva superior*).","*Verstandeserkenntnis* rendered 'knowledge of the understanding'; *Erkenntnis* = knowledge throughout.","'*facultas concept . . .*' is broken off in the source; left as it stands."]],"dohna-s08-020":["[The higher faculty of knowledge contains:",["The opening bracket is the editors' and is carried over unclosed, since the bracket closes in the passage dohna-s08-023."]],"dohna-s08-021":["1. Understanding,",[]],"dohna-s08-022":["2. Judgment [*Urteilskraft*],",["*Urteilskraft* rendered 'judgment' (faculty of judgment), as the context of a faculty requires; German given in brackets to distinguish it from *Urteil*."]],"dohna-s08-023":["3. Reason.]",[]],"dohna-s08-024":["The understanding is the faculty of representing the universal as such. [E.g. the definition of man in general.]",[]],"dohna-s08-025":["Judgment [*Urteilskraft*] is the faculty of representing the particular as contained under the universal [Caius is a man], or the faculty of subsumption.",[]],"dohna-s08-026":["Reason is the faculty of deriving the particular from the universal, or knowledge *a priori*. [All men are mortal. Sempronius too is a man. Sempronius is mortal.",["The bracket opened here is closed only in the next passage (dohna-s08-027)."]],"dohna-s08-027":["In the first two, understanding and judgment, one can teach; in the last, one can only practise.]",["'In letzterer' is taken as referring to judgment [*Urteilskraft*] (the second of the 'two', then 'latter'), but the sense is doubtful: the preceding list is understanding, judgment, reason, and the context suggests 'the last' means reason, or that judgment is meant. I have rendered 'the last', i.e. reason, leaving the doubt open. The German as it stands says 'in the latter', and the transcript may be confused."]],"dohna-s08-028":["The definition of logic as *cognitio rationalis* is not the best; it could hold only if logic had as its object the use of the rules of understanding and of reason.",["*Objekt* rendered 'object'; the sentence is elliptical ('wenn sie ... hat') and I have supplied 'logic' for 'sie'."]],"dohna-s08-029":["All knowledge must be rational. The author should therefore have said knowledge of reason [*Vernunfterkenntnis*]. What is not knowledge of reason is historical knowledge, and rests on empirical grounds. [Scientific knowledge is that of which rules *in abstracto* are possible. Knowledge is common in so far as it is not scientific.]",["'Seientifisches' is an OCR slip for 'Scientifisches'; I render 'scientific'.","*Gemein* rendered 'common'."]],"dohna-s08-030":["We must strive to philosophise, not merely to learn philosophy. For were we to do the latter, our knowledge would be merely historical and not drawn from ourselves. An aggregate of items of cognition [*Kenntnisse*] as a system is science [*Wissenschaft*]; at its basis lies a chief principle from which all the rest is derived. A mode of knowledge is entitled common in so far as it is not science. It may at times be very extensive, but it has no system and no unity of the whole. In respect of matter, one who possesses such knowledge is not opposed to one who has science, but only in respect of form. Indeed he may even be richer in respect of matter, but everything is thrown together in confusion. We may reckon philosophical knowledge as part of erudition [*Gelehrsamkeit*], in so far as we can give a philosophical history of what all philosophers have said from the beginning. But one who can do this is not on that account a philosopher. One is that only when one can philosophise. Historical knowledge as science is erudition. A teacher of reason [*Vernunftlehrer*] may be both philosopher and mathematician. One who possesses all historical knowledge as science is entitled a polyhistor.",["'Heget' is an OCR slip for 'liegt'; 'vast' is probably for 'vast' (Latin-derived, 'extensive'), rendered 'very extensive'.","*Kenntnisse* rendered 'items of cognition' to keep it distinct from *Erkenntnis*, 'knowledge'.","'Der Materie nach ist der ... entgegengesetzt': 'der' taken as the man who has common knowledge; I have restated the sentence for clarity."]],"dohna-s08-031":["In the concept of the philosopher one can still think a twofold distinction:",[]],"dohna-s08-032":["1. as artist of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*], 2. as sage or legislator.",[]],"dohna-s08-033":["To philosophy proper (which is not to be merely an art of reason) nothing belongs but metaphysics and morals. These serve as means of knowing our existence as rational beings, and of knowing the final end, our goal [— the highest good —], the most sublime that we have. From this it is evident that the physicist has no science at all, although physics is always reckoned among the sciences.",["*Vernunftkunst* rendered 'art of reason'."]],"dohna-s08-034":["Our author now deals",["The sentence is continued by the heading in dohna-s08-035."]],"dohna-s08-035":["With beautiful knowledge",["Heading, *Vom schönen Erkenntnis*; rendered literally."]],"dohna-s08-036":["[Beautiful knowledge in fact does not exist at all. In knowledge all that matters is the relation to the object; if that is hit, the knowledge is true; but only the presentation [*Darstellung*] can be beautiful.",["Bracket opened here is closed in dohna-s08-039."]],"dohna-s08-037":["Sensibility is the faculty of intuition:",[]],"dohna-s08-038":["a) Sense, the faculty of intuition in the presence [of the object],",["'[of the object]' supplied from the parallel clause in the next item."]],"dohna-s08-039":["b) Imagination, the faculty of intuition in the absence of the object.]",["*Einbildungskraft* rendered 'imagination'."]],"dohna-s08-040":["When I call something beautiful, I thereby express my liking [*Wohlgefallen*], my pleasure [*Lust*] in the object (the relation of the object to the subject, which produces an agreeable representation which determines the mind itself to its own preservation). The basis of the definition. In everything beautiful we understand only the relation of knowledge to the subject, not at all the object itself. E.g. in describing beautiful regions one describes only how one has been affected by them. It is impossible to depict the object itself. There is therefore no such thing as beautiful knowledge.",["'Die Basis der Definition' is a fragment, left as it stands.","'Beziehung der Erkenntnis auf das Subjekt' rendered 'relation of knowledge to the subject'."]],"dohna-s09-001":["[7th hour, 3rd May]",["Editors' heading, bracketed in the source."]],"dohna-s09-002":["Perfection is divided",["The sentence continues in the next two passages."]],"dohna-s09-003":["1. into logical perfection, in the agreement of the faculty of knowledge with the object,",[]],"dohna-s09-004":["2. into aesthetic perfection, | it | consists in the agreement of the object with the faculty of knowledge of the subject.",["The signs | are the Academy apparatus and are carried over."]],"dohna-s09-005":["[Aesthetic perfection is the subjective agreement of the understanding with sensibility — which enlivens the representation of an object. Because the agreement is only subjective, it will also be possible only through sensation. A feeling of pleasure arises from it, just as a feeling of displeasure arises from the sensation of conflict.]",["*Gefühl der Lust/Unlust* rendered 'feeling of pleasure/displeasure'."]],"dohna-s09-006":["Imagination and understanding are the only two active faculties of knowledge of the human mind [*Gemüt*]. But the senses are wholly passive; they necessarily require an object, whereas imagination creates objects for itself.",[]],"dohna-s09-007":["[A stray 'I'] To all our modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] belong the following two items [two elements, of which one without the other yields no knowledge]:",["The initial 'I' is an OCR stray (probably a page or line mark); not translated, and shown above only to make the omission visible."]],"dohna-s09-008":["1. Intuition (the interpretation of the concept, of thinking);",["'Interpretation' is the transcript's word (*Interpretation*); the sense is obscure, perhaps 'illustration' (cf. *Versinnlichung*), and is left literal."]],"dohna-s09-009":["2. Concept. The pure concept, like the pure rule, does not yet give distinctness of knowledge; intuition must belong to it as well. Conversely, intuition without concept is likewise nothing. For without [a concept] it would be just as if one had seen nothing. E.g. tasteful poetic descriptions of regions, which produce only intuition, serve not at all for knowledge, and are fit only for dressing-tables.",["'Ohne' left without object in the German; '[a concept]' is supplied. 'Toiletten' rendered 'dressing-tables' (literal sense, perhaps 'toilettes'); doubtful."]],"dohna-s09-010":["But if I look merely and solely to beauty, I do not demand instruction, but only pleasing entertainment. The mind can excite this liking in itself, and in doing so remains in spontaneity. Taste is to be the faculty of having liking in the object from the subjective agreement of the powers of knowledge.",["*Erkenntniskräfte* rendered 'powers of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s09-011":["[Since beauty can never be known through concepts, but only through feeling, and this is not so communicable as concepts, which, since they relate to the object, are the same in all subjects — no objective rules can be given for it. Yet the beautiful is distinguished from the agreeable in that the former holds subjectively universally for all, the latter extends merely to individual subjects. In matters of the agreeable one will not require anyone to pass the same judgment — as one does with objects of the beautiful, where the judgment of taste is universally valid. — The aesthetically beautiful is to be elucidated not by rules but merely by examples — hence the worth of the classics.]",["The first sentence is irregular (the 'da' clause and the 'so' main clause); I have kept it close but smoothed the dashes."]],"dohna-s09-012":["In regard to liking in objects, knowledge is twofold.",[]],"dohna-s09-013":["1. The beautiful; the faculty of discriminating the beautiful is called taste.",[]],"dohna-s09-014":["2. The sublime; the faculty of discriminating | the sublime | is called feeling.",["The signs | are the Academy apparatus."]],"dohna-s09-015":["Taste belongs to judgment [*Urteilskraft*]. For it is not | a | means of producing objects, but only of judging them.",[]],"dohna-s09-016":["Now judgment is also twofold:",[]],"dohna-s09-017":["1. logical,",["The enumeration breaks off here; the second member is in the following passage."]],"dohna-s09-018":["2. Aesthetic [judgment]. This is taste [understanding in union with the imagination]. An empirical judgment is brought about by the immediate impression which the (representation of the) object makes upon our mind [with regard to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure]. Logical judgment has always fixed rules; but the rules of aesthetic judgment rest only on empirical grounds. To quarrel over the pleasure that arises from the senses (for example, to be surprised that sauerkraut does not please another's palate) would be very foolish. Hence the proverb *Chacun à son goût* would, in this case, if it had this meaning, be correct. But it does not have this meaning [for by taste we here understand not the stimulation of the palate but aesthetic judgment], and so in every other respect it is false. For taste is aesthetic judgment, and it is universally valid. This may put us on the track of what taste properly consists in. [Everything that belongs to sensation is subjective; everything that belongs to intuition is objective.] Everything that rests not upon the relation of our faculty of representation to the object but upon the subject, upon the representing faculty, is aesthetic. In so far as we find imagination and understanding harmoniously animated by a certain feeling, we have taste. [The freedom of the imagination in agreement with the concept of the understanding. The understanding, without the imagination, which gives it intuition, would accomplish nothing; the object would vanish for it. The understanding comes to the aid of the imagination and brings unity into its products.]",["'2. ästhetische' follows a heading lost before it; I supply '[judgment]' (Urteilskraft is the evident sense from context). The word is outside the passage proper, so treat it as an inference.","OCR 'Aväre' corrected to 'wäre'; 'ä son gout' to 'à son goût'.","'wenn es die Bedeutung hätte': the antecedent is the sensory sense of 'taste' (palate); I make this explicit in 'this meaning'.","Urteilskraft rendered 'judgment' (faculty of judgment); Einbildungskraft 'imagination'; Geschmack 'taste'."]],"dohna-s09-019":["|[Taste is an art. The understanding and the imagination, which must unite for it, are like two friends who cannot endure each other and yet cannot part, for they live in constant strife and are nevertheless indispensable to one another. As follows from the above, there are no beautiful sciences, but only an art of the beautiful. To produce it, genius is required. For example, a good poem cannot be ordered to measure; it succeeds only when the poet has the mood [*Laune*], a fortunate disposition of mind, for it; rules are not sufficient for this. There were poets before there were rules of poetics.] |",["*Laune* (mood, humour) is glossed in the text by 'glückliche Gemütsdisposition'; I kept the gloss.","Apparatus signs | carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s09-020":["[8th hour]",[]],"dohna-s09-021":["The logical perfection of representations consists in this, that they represent particular representations as in the universal [or formal completeness (just as the aesthetic is material) is the requisite of logical perfection; it seeks the particular under the universal]. In the aesthetic, however, the case is precisely the reverse: there universal representations are represented in the particular. In a logical judgment, then, I look only to this, that particular representations are represented in the universal. [With the aesthetic judgment it is again precisely the reverse.] Logical perfection requires that intuition be brought to concepts; aesthetic perfection, that concepts be brought to intuition. Logical perfection consists in the agreement of knowledge with the object; aesthetic perfection, in its agreement with the subject and its faculty of knowledge.",["OCR 'unigekehrt' corrected to 'umgekehrt'; 'demio Objekt' read as 'dem Objekt'.","The bracketed gloss at the opening is syntactically loose in the German ('oder formale … Vollständigkeit'); I kept it close to the text and bracketed as the editors do.","Erkenntnis = knowledge; Erkenntnisvermögen = faculty of knowledge."]],"dohna-s09-022":["Our author [Meier] opposes ugliness (*deformitas*) to beauty [ugliness is just as positive as beauty: an object that I hate, the maximum of disgust]; but he holds that there is still an intermediate term, dryness (*jejunum*) [it is a degree of perfection which mathematics possesses, because it has dryness]; and dryness and beauty can indeed subsist together [for example, in a sermon that begins dryly but afterwards contains many beauties].",["'Unser Autor' is Meier, whose text Kant uses as the textbook; the name is not in the passage, so it is bracketed.","The angle bracket in '<(Jejunum>' is an apparatus mark; I have not reproduced it and read *jejunum* as the Latin term.","Final bracket is closed in the German only by the full stop; I closed it."]],"dohna-s09-023":["Beauty can concern only the presentation [*Darstellung*], the intuition, not, strictly speaking, the concept. The great business of propagation is spread throughout the whole of nature. The expression 'beautiful knowledge' is not at all fitting.",["The sentence on propagation (*Fortpflanzung*) is abrupt and its connexion with the context is unclear; it may be a transcriber's slip or an illustration lost in the notes. I translated it as it stands.","'ganz eigentlich' rendered 'strictly speaking'."]],"dohna-s09-024":["The faculties of our mind can be brought under the following three classes:",[]],"dohna-s09-025":["1. Faculty of knowledge,",[]],"dohna-s09-026":["2. Feeling of pleasure and displeasure,",[]],"dohna-s09-027":["3. Faculty of desire.",[]],"dohna-s09-028":["A representation is our knowledge, and indeed",["Sentence continues in the following items."]],"dohna-s09-029":["1. In relation to the faculty of knowledge it is entitled logical perfection [in relation to the understanding; truth is a judgment, not a feeling].",[]],"dohna-s09-030":["2. In relation to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, aesthetic perfection [in relation to the faculty of judgment].",[]],"dohna-s09-031":["3. In so far as it agrees with our faculty of desire, practical perfection [in relation to reason].",["The German has no verb governing 'praktische Vollkommenheit'; I supplied 'it is entitled' by parallel with items 1 and 2 only implicitly, and have not bracketed it since it is carried by the structure."]],"dohna-s09-032":["On the perfections of knowledge",[]],"dohna-s09-033":["We can have perfection in knowledge:",[]],"dohna-s09-034":["1. in quantity: universality;",[]],"dohna-s09-035":["2. in respect of quality: distinctness through concepts [that is, through consciousness of the marks];",["Merkmale = marks."]],"dohna-s09-036":["3. in respect of relation: relation (of the representation) to an object. Truth: agreement of the judgment with the mode of thinking. [Truth is here the logical perfection.]",["'Übereinstimmung des Urteils mit der Denkart' stands thus in the text; one expects 'mit dem Objekt', and the transcript may be at fault. Translated as it stands."]],"dohna-s09-037":["4. in respect of modality (this rests on whether it is contingent or necessary). [Here it is the necessity known *a priori*.]",[]],"dohna-s09-038":["Logical perfection (as to quantity) consists in the universality of representations, in that concepts become distinct. This universality was objective [and consisted in representing the particular in the universal *in abstracto*]. But aesthetically it is subjective, and this subjective universality is entitled popularity. [Aesthetic perfection, on the contrary, consists in representing the universal *in concreto*; this is popularity: to make concepts of the understanding conformable to the *sensus communis*, that is, to sound common understanding.]",["The angle bracket before '(der Quantität nach)' is an apparatus mark and is omitted.","'Deutlich werden' rendered 'become distinct'; the sense of 'in that' (daß) is explanatory and somewhat loose in the German."]],"dohna-s09-039":["The second logical perfection is distinctness through concepts. This is effected through few marks [in which, however, I am also conscious of the partial representations]; the aesthetic through many marks. Logical distinctness arises from my representing little in many things; aesthetic, from my representing much in (few) things. Aesthetic distinctness is entitled liveliness in the combination of many representations. For logical distinctness abstraction is required; aesthetic distinctness requires a swarm of accessory representations.",["OCR 'D e u 1 1 i c h k e i t' read as 'Deutlichkeit'; 'Neben Vorstellungen' as one word.","'Deutlichkeit' rendered 'distinctness' (the usual Kantian logical sense)."]],"dohna-s09-040":["The third perfection is relation to the object: truth [agreement with the object]. That is aesthetically true which holds according to appearance [*Erscheinung*], the agreement of a judgment with the mode of thinking of the subject. Thus the poet goes by universal semblance, by subjective truth. What is poetic is always aesthetically, seldom logically, true. For example: the thunder rumbles. [Truth is agreement of the judgment with the mode of thinking.]",["'Denkungsart' rendered 'mode of thinking'; the bracketed final sentence repeats the transcript's formula, possibly a slip for 'with the object'."]],"dohna-s09-041":["Now the fourth [perfection]. Modality rests on whether it is a necessary or a merely contingent judgment. There is an aesthetic necessity when it is necessary, according to laws of the imagination and not according to laws of reason, to represent the thing to oneself. Aesthetic necessity is only subjective necessity. Aesthetic perfection is a condescension of the understanding to sensibility.",["OCR 'istnur' corrected to 'ist nur'. 'Perfection' after 'fourth' is supplied from the context and bracketed."]],"dohna-s09-042":["[9th hour]",[]],"dohna-s09-043":["Through intuition an object is given; through the concept it is thought. When intuition and concept accord for the enlivening of knowledge itself, they produce in us a satisfaction, and one then calls it beautiful knowledge. One must endeavour to make understanding and imagination accord in one business. This, however, is no longer play. In a beautiful product no new knowledge is required, but only the enlivening of the powers of knowledge. In general, the production of the beautiful is not science but free play of the imagination. Imagination and understanding are two friends who cannot do without one another but also cannot endure one another, for one is always doing the other harm. The more universal the understanding is in its rules, the more perfect it is; but if it wishes to consider things *in concreto*, it can do absolutely nothing without the imagination.",["The bars around 'er' (| er |) are apparatus marks (page/line breaks) and are kept in the English? Omitted here only as a transcription artefact; the German reads 'kann er schlechterdings'.","'Wohlgefallen' rendered 'satisfaction'; 'Erkenntniskräfte' 'powers of knowledge'.","'Dies aber ist nicht mehr Spiel' seems to be in tension with 'freies Spiel' below; translated as it stands."]],"dohna-s09-044":["The imperfections of knowledge are either 1. imperfections of lack (*defectus*), or 2. imperfections of privation (*vitium*), better, of transgression (*reatus*, *transgressio legis*, 'eristreus').",["'eristreus' is doubtful, apparently an OCR or transcriber's corruption of some Latin or Greek term; left in quotation marks and not interpreted.","The German 'Beraubung' paired with *vitium* is as in the transcript."]],"dohna-s09-045":["[Ignorance is like a *tabula rasa*; error, like a scribbled tablet. Here one has, over and above, the trouble of wiping out the false in order first to get back to ignorance.",["The bracket is opened but not closed in the passage; the text may continue in the next passage."]],"dohna-s09-046":["Truth, ignorance, error",["Probably a diagram of three terms, whose arrangement is lost in the OCR; translated as a plain list."]],"dohna-s09-047":["+ a a — a]",["A string of marks and letters, apparently a fragment of the editors' apparatus or a logical formula; it is translated as it stands, since the OCR gives nothing further to go on."]],"dohna-s09-048":["For even if one does not say everything that is true, everything that one does say must nevertheless be true. The contrary [*Widerspiel*] is not merely something negative, but actually something positive, which in mathematics would bear the sign minus. For example, a physician may not merely fail to make good [*nicht ersetzt*] the patient's lack of health, but may wholly ruin it for him. From this we see, indeed, that among all the imperfections of human existence it is the imperfection of the contrary that we must guard against most, because it has the dreadful property that it is not merely of no use but does positive harm. For if one has learnt something false, it costs unspeakable trouble to put oneself back again into the state in which one was when one knew nothing of it, in order thus to come to know the truth.",["The sign | before and after *nicht* in the German is carried over in the sense only as the text reads; the German 'dem Kranken nicht allein den Mangel der Gesundheit | nicht | ersetzt' has a doubled *nicht*, which I have translated once, as the sense requires (probably a deleted or inserted word marked by the apparatus).","*Widerspiel* rendered 'the contrary'; Kant means positive error as opposed to mere lack of truth.","'Zerrüttet' rendered 'ruin'."]],"dohna-s09-049":["[Tenth lecture]",["OCR 'lOte' read as '10te'."]],"dohna-s09-050":["On the Extent [*Weitläufigkeit*] of Learned Knowledge",["*Weitläufigkeit* rendered 'extent'; the next passage shows it is the extensive magnitude of knowledge."]],"dohna-s09-051":["[Horizon is the sum-total of those modes of knowledge that belong to the purpose of a human being.]",["*Erkenntnisse* rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s09-052":["Extensive knowledge rests on multiplicity, intensive knowledge on degree. The latter is a magnitude of unity. The former the author entitles the extent of learned knowledge.",["'The author' is Meier, whose text the lecture follows."]],"dohna-s09-053":["On the horizon of human knowledge. The horizon [the circle that bounds all objects] is the congruence (agreement) of the limits of any knowledge with the limits of human perfection. This perfection is bounded, and if it extended itself beyond these limits it would fall into chimeras. Nothing can be invisible to us above the horizon; hence this manner of speaking is false. Only what lies below our horizon can we not know. We can divide our horizon:",["'Mir' in the OCR is read as 'wir' (we). The sentence 'Nur das, was unter unserm Horizont ist, können wir nicht wissen' is translated as it stands, though 'unter' and 'über' may have been confused in the transcript.","The brace '{Übereinstimmung)' is given as parentheses."]],"dohna-s09-054":["[Only this belongs to our field. One divides: things that are above, outside, and below our horizon. Outside it: what we need not know; below it: what we ought not to know. This already refers to purpose, to the practical.]",["OCR 'N\\ir' read as 'Nur'. The sentence 'Only this belongs to our field' is evidently continued from the previous passage; what 'dieser' refers to is unclear in isolation.","The text sets 'above' with 'outside' and 'below' somewhat loosely; translated as it stands."]],"dohna-s09-055":["1. Logical: the determination of our logical horizon [— relation to the faculty of knowledge demands distinctness of abstraction. This would harm the imagination. Aesthetics therefore requires only clarity].",["*Erkenntnisvermögen* rendered 'faculty of knowledge'; *Einbildungskraft* 'imagination'."]],"dohna-s09-056":["2. Aesthetic: according to agreement with our taste.",[]],"dohna-s09-057":["3. Practical: according to the suitability of our knowledge to our purposes.",[]],"dohna-s09-058":["The logical horizon we can divide:",[]],"dohna-s09-059":["1. Into the historical, and this can be a) objective [e.g. natural science, history], b) subjective.",["*Naturkunde* rendered 'natural science' (natural history is also possible)."]],"dohna-s09-060":["2. Into the rational [objectively rational, so far as it can be seen *a priori*].",[]],"dohna-s09-061":["The historical is incomparably more extensive than the rational [which is either 1. that of pure reason or metaphysics, or 2. that founded on experience, or physiology]. In mathematics the latter, in philosophy the former, is the most extensive. The field of mathematics is unsurveyable. Critique is the determination of the (rational) horizon of human knowledge. [There is a universal horizon of the modes of knowledge of the human race. The horizon of human knowledge so far as it concerns experience is called physics. Physics has its limits too, in that we cannot see by reason whence this or that object comes.]",["'(Rational->Horizonts' is garbled in the OCR; read as '(Rational-)Horizonts'. 'Erkenntnisses' (genitive singular in the bracketed text) is treated as a slip for *Erkenntnisse*.","The statement about which is most extensive in mathematics and in philosophy is given as the text has it; the order 'latter/former' may reflect the transcriber's own confusion.","*Kritik* rendered 'Critique'."]],"dohna-s09-062":["To determine the rational horizon of human knowledge is one of the noblest and most difficult occupations of the human mind. It is here that metaphysicians are accustomed to lose themselves. Only metaphysics, or its (foundation, the) Critique of Pure Reason, can show where the limits of reason begin and where all its power ceases. One may occupy oneself with the universal horizon of humanity and treat of that of individual human beings. Certain modes of knowledge, some maintain with entire injustice, we can indeed know, but they would be of no use to us. [Many modes of knowledge and sciences would not have reached their present stage if one had asked at the outset about their utility. This often shows itself only when they have attained perfection.] *Quaerit delirus et non respondet Homerus.* The human race must learn all the modes of knowledge useful to us [in order thereby to cultivate its understanding. — No knowledge of any kind can ever be wholly contrary to purpose (in general)]. For individual human beings, to be sure, much may perhaps be dispensable. Men divide the branches of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] into various fields. For each there are devotees, according as they have inclinations for this or that matter.",["The marks | mit | and | sie | in the German are apparatus signs and are not reproduced beyond the sense.","*Kenntnisse* rendered 'branches of knowledge' to distinguish it from *Erkenntnisse*; *Vermögen* 'power'.","The Latin tag is left in Latin and italicised; it is not translated in the passage."]],"dohna-s10-001":["[Eleventh lecture, the tenth of May]",["OCR 'Ute' read as 'llte' (eleventh), 'lOten' as '10ten'."]],"dohna-s10-002":["[One can further divide the horizon of human knowledge into:",["The bracket is left open, as in the source; it closes in the next passage."]],"dohna-s10-003":["1. The horizon of sound reason,",[]],"dohna-s10-004":["2. That of the sciences.]",[]],"dohna-s10-005":["We can say, in a certain sense, that the beginning of the world is where the art of writing arose. There are no branches of knowledge that we ought not to know, for in general it is our duty to know everything that we can know. But there are indeed branches of knowledge that we can dispense with, and which we therefore may not know [those that lie outside our horizon according to the above definition] (as, for example, the physician can dispense with Hebrew, and therefore may not know it).",["'Dürfen' in 'nicht wissen dürfen' is rendered 'may not', as in the German, though it is odd against the preceding sentence; it is translated as it stands.","*Kenntnisse* rendered 'branches of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s10-006":["The manner in which branches of knowledge are taught at universities is called factory-like, because here there are many who work in common upon one purpose.",["*fabrikenmäßig* rendered 'factory-like'."]],"dohna-s10-007":["Magnitude of knowledge. What matters here is not the quantity but the degree. [*Non multa, sed multum.* The external extensive magnitude of knowledge indicates its general diffusion among many human beings. — This is the present state of enlightenment — the degree of culture — which, according to the talents of the subject, makes a human being fit for his purposes as a human being and as a citizen.]",["The construction of the last bracketed sentence is loose; I have kept it as a dash-joined series rather than smoothing it further."]],"dohna-s10-008":["Practical rules with regard to the horizon of human knowledge. One must not determine the horizon of one's knowledge too early, for one does not know for what one will later have a special talent. Moreover, inclination may change. [First learn a great deal, in order then to see for what one has most liking — yet not desultorily, from one thing to another. — All study according to rules cultivates the understanding; hence grammar is useful even to the future artist. One usually learns only when it is too late to act in accordance with one's talents, inclinations and circumstances, or even to know them.] But once one has adopted a determinate horizon of knowledge, one must not lightly alter it. One must not measure the horizon of others by one's own. Above all, one must not forthwith pronounce useless what seems to us, in a certain respect, dispensable. If one had always wished to grasp the utility with one's hands, one would not have risen high in any science. Can there be things of which one can say, we do not wish to know them? — a praiseworthy ignorance? Ignorance is never praiseworthy. [Thus there is indeed an *ignoratio inculpabilis* (blameless), but never an *ignorantia laudabilis*.] Not ignorance, but the knowledge of one's ignorance, is praiseworthy [and it requires much knowledge]. Whoever comes so far takes the step from the field of science to the field of wisdom. Those who are puffed up with self-conceit commonly call others idiots. [Thus one names men who, though they have much knowledge [*Kenntnisse*], yet have no science, ignorant, as also him who has not even that. In general he is called ignorant who is unknowing in regard to the science which he professes. — The idiot is to the polymath what the layman is to the cleric, or what *literatus* is: one who can read and write — a great deal in those days!] Polyhistory is historical much-knowing; rational much-knowing would be entitled polymathy [to which philosophy and mathematics belong]; and (both taken together) all-knowing (if it could be conceived) would be entitled pansophy.",["'Noch mein' kann' is read as 'Noch mehr kann' → 'Moreover ... may'; 'desxütorisch' read as 'desultorisch' (desultorily); 'f' after 'Verhältnissen' is a stray mark, omitted; 'ignoratio inculpabüis' and 'laudabihs' corrected to *inculpabilis* and *laudabilis*.","The bracketed sentence on 'Ignorant' and 'Idiot' is loose and probably garbled in the transcript; 'Idiot ist das gegen Vielwisser' is rendered as 'the idiot is to the polymath' by judgment, and the clause on *Literatus* is left as it stands.","*heißen* rendered 'is entitled'; *Kenntnis* 'knowledge', *Wissenschaft* 'science'."]],"dohna-s10-009":["The polyhistor must, first, be a humanist, that is, he must be a connoisseur of the ancients and of the fine arts (poetry, rhetoric).",["The passage breaks off at the first of the enumerated requirements."]],"dohna-s10-010":["[The *humaniora* serve chiefly for acquaintance with taste and for participation in the sensations [*Empfindungen*] of others. This latter, namely the capacity [*Vermögen*] to communicate one's sensations (items of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] and feelings) to others, constitutes precisely what is characteristic of the humanist. — A man is a humanist by the fact that his knowledge [*Erkenntnis*] is science [*Wissenschaft*] and learning. — ]",["'Kenntnisse t' in the OCR: the stray 't' is dropped as an OCR slip.","Kenntnis/Kenntnisse rendered 'acquaintance'/'items of knowledge' to keep them distinct from Erkenntnis = 'knowledge'.","The final sentence is restated slightly ('A man is a humanist by the fact that...') to make the pronoun 'es' clear."]],"dohna-s10-011":["The counterpart of the humanist is the *belletrist*. He, however, does not pursue, as the humanist does, what is beautiful in its own right [*das selbständige Schöne*], but only what is changeable, according to the diversity of taste, which follows fashion.",["'Pendant' rendered 'counterpart'.","'selbständige' rendered 'in its own right', i.e. self-subsistent, in contrast with the changeable."]],"dohna-s10-012":["[The belletrist is only the humanist's ape — a mere dilettante. One can resolve only upon the one or the other. In order not to become a pedant, the belletrist pursues merely gallant studies [*Studia*]. The gallant manner is nothing other than popularity with taste. Scholastic perfection (conformity to the rules (to the object)) always remains the first requisite of knowledge, just as in language grammatical correctness is demanded first, and elegance afterwards. Popularity (conformity to the subject) must be a condescension of a mode of knowledge, in so far as it already possesses scholastic perfection. Whoever holds this | scholastic perfection | to be the only thing (in social intercourse) is entitled a pedant: he can communicate himself only to one who has been through the schools. Gallantry is just as much a fault.]",["The parenthesis '(Angemessenheit zu den Regeln (zum Objekt)' lacks a closing bracket in the German; I close it.","'Erkenntnis' rendered 'knowledge'; 'eines Erkenntnisses' as 'a mode of knowledge'.","The signs | ... | are the Academy editors' apparatus and are retained as they stand.","'Wer sie ... für die einzige hält': 'sie' is resolved as scholastic perfection (the editors' insertion), though the sentence's grammar is loose.","'heißt Pedant' rendered 'is entitled a pedant'."]],"dohna-s10-013":["The polyhistor must be",["Fragment; the enumeration continues in the following passage ('2tens'). The first item is not in this passage."]],"dohna-s10-014":["secondly, a linguist, a connoisseur of the ancient languages, because what matters here is lasting models that stand in their own right [*selbständige bleibende Muster*].",["Supplied 'secondly' for '2tens'.","'selbständige' rendered 'that stand in their own right' as in s10-011."]],"dohna-s10-015":["Third, the *litteratorist*, the connoisseur of books (Magliabecchi, a famous bookkeeper [*Buchführer*] at Florence, had a prodigious memory). Philology is the sum total of all the instruments of learning; the humanist is the connoisseur of the formation of taste. Humanity [*Humanität*] is always at the same time popular. Only dead languages can become models of taste, not living ones, for these change far too often, and words whose meaning was noble have acquired a low meaning. To have an enduring taste, one must study the ancients. If the ancients were to be lost, we should have to fear the incursion of barbarism. In Hindustan there is also a language formerly in use there and now likewise dead [whose origin is unknown] (Sanskrit), which is very perfect, but is spoken only by a few Brahmins. The belletrist is called the dilettante; he finds pleasure and taste in the fine arts.",["'Buchführer' is the transcript's word (probably for bookseller/librarian; Magliabecchi was a Florentine librarian); rendered literally, flagged as doubtful.","'Bücherkenner' follows 'Litteratorist' without punctuation; taken as apposition.","'Hindostan' modernised to 'Hindustan'; 'Braminen' to 'Brahmins'."]],"dohna-s10-016":["We can divide the talent of man, according as it is inclined:",["'indem es geneigt ist' rendered 'according as it is inclined'."]],"dohna-s10-017":["1. to science [*Wissenschaft*], or",[]],"dohna-s10-018":["2. to art [*Kunst*] [all culture rests on these two].",[]],"dohna-s10-019":["This | art | can now be divided:",["'Diese' stands for 'die Kunst' per the editors' insertion between the signs."]],"dohna-s10-020":["(a) into free arts, *artes liberales* or *ingenuae* [free art is the skill that pleases immediately],",[]],"dohna-s10-021":["(b) into mercenary arts, *artes mercenales serviles*, which can please only as a means to some self-interested end [mercenary arts always please only mediately, for other purposes, and so do all the sciences. Almost always they please only on account of their usefulness, etc. Mathematics, to be sure, pleases immediately, but in that respect one might reckon it also among the free arts. A mercenary art can be commissioned; a free art cannot — we cannot make its products according to rules, as we can those of the former.]",["'Lohnkünste' rendered 'mercenary arts'.","'ihre Produkte können | wir | nicht...': the editors' insertion 'wir' is retained in the translation as 'we', with the bars omitted for readability; the original shows | wir |.","'jene' resolved as the mercenary arts."]],"dohna-s10-022":["This division of talent is very delicate. The beautiful is that which pleases through itself. In that case one does not ask at all about utility. If one already asks about utility in an art, then it is a bread-art [*Brotkunst*].",["'Brotkunst' rendered literally: an art practised for a livelihood."]],"dohna-s10-023":["As regards the sciences, they can be divided into:",[]],"dohna-s10-024":["1. historical, and",[]],"dohna-s10-025":["2. sciences of reason [*Vernunftwissenschaften*].",[]],"dohna-s10-026":["The sum total of the historical sciences is learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*]. The sum total of the sciences of reason has no special name, for its parts, philosophy and mathematics, differ far too greatly. The sum total of all the sciences is entitled polyhistory, of which we have already spoken above.",["'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'."]],"dohna-s10-027":["The author now speaks of the pedant. He is (1) a school-pedant, (2) a pedant of courtesy. For example, someone speaks like a book, always under the constraint of rules. [One finds the like at court, for example among chamberlains.]",["'Der Autor' is Meier, whose text Kant is lecturing on.","Restated the sentence 'Z. B. es spricht jemand...' with 'For example, someone speaks...'."]],"dohna-s10-028":["On Pedantry",[]],"dohna-s10-029":["[p. 24, § 65.]",["Reference to Meier's *Auszug*."]],"dohna-s10-030":["By a pedant we do not understand an ignorant person (hence the author's definition is incorrect). Yet he is laughed at. Whence does this come? The word 'pedant' comes from the Italian and properly means *magister pedanius*. He was merely a schoolman, not at all fitted for the world, since he did not come to the table of the master of the house, but was for the most part among the servants.",["'pedanius' is the transcript's form; the usual etymology gives *paedagogus*/*pedante*. Kept as it stands.","'Dieser' resolved as the *magister*."]],"dohna-s10-031":["[Pedantry is punctiliousness [*Peinlichkeit*] in form. No nation inclines so much to it as the German. Pedantry occurs in all faculties, above all in the military and among jurists. — The charlatan is the ostentatious displayer of a paltry knowledge [*Erkenntnis*].]",["'Peinlichkeit' rendered 'punctiliousness' here and 'pedantic exactness' elsewhere would be inconsistent; I keep 'punctiliousness' for Peinlichkeit throughout and 'punctuality' for Pünktlichkeit."]],"dohna-s10-032":["Pedantry is useless exactness (punctiliousness) in formalities. Exactness adapted to its end (punctuality) in formalities is thoroughness [*Gründlichkeit*]. Thus the two have some resemblance. In many cases there is much that is arbitrary in formalities. They are useful for scholastic purposes, but not for popular ones, that is, in social intercourse. Punctiliousness and punctuality are therefore well to be distinguished.",["Peinlichkeit = punctiliousness; Pünktlichkeit = punctuality (my choice)."]],"dohna-s10-033":["That the transition from punctuality to punctiliousness is very easy is certainly true. It is impossible to learn everything by formalities, for example, how to write a letter. Hence one sees the great difference between the wooden letter of the schoolboy and the fluent one of his sister [although the latter may not be orthographical at all]. The Muses, then, nevertheless need the company of the Graces, and these consist in this, that the school-dust, the scholastic element, is wiped from knowledge, and that it becomes popular and universally intelligible. [Yet for that very reason the ground of knowledge must be in conformity with the schools.]",["OCR 'Avegge wischt' corrected to 'wegwischt' ('wipes away'); 'that the school-dust... is wiped from knowledge' is a smoothing of the German construction, which reads 'dass der Schulstaub... von der Erkenntnis wegwischt', apparently lacking a subject.","'schulgerecht' rendered 'in conformity with the schools'."]],"dohna-s10-034":["Pedantry is peculiar not to the learned estate alone, but to all estates. A *Pinsel* (simpleton) is one who can draw nothing from himself but can merely imitate, and who, when he wishes to bring forward something of his knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*], cannot depart at all from the formulas of the schools. [*D. umbraticus* — the designation of a pedant. — Gallantry signifies an embellishment of that which in itself is not a beautiful object.] Charlatanry is boasting and ostentation. Belletrism belongs to the fashionable study, which changes so very often. The more a science is extended not (in) content but in space, the shallower it becomes.",["'D. umbraticus' is an abbreviation (probably *doctor umbraticus*, 'closet scholar'); left as it stands, expansion doubtful.","'Baume' in the OCR is read as 'Raume' (space), and the parenthetical '(nicht)' is awkward: the sense is 'not in content but in extent', which I translate; the bracketed 'not' in the German seems an editorial or OCR irregularity. Doubtful.","'Pinsel' kept alongside 'simpleton'."]],"dohna-s11-001":["On the Magnitude of Learned Knowledge",["'Erkenntnis' rendered 'knowledge'."]],"dohna-s11-002":["Extensiveness [*Weitläufigkeit*] is extensive magnitude, which consists in plurality, in the manifold [knowing many things]. Intensive magnitude concerns degree [knowing much about one thing. — *Non multa, sed multum*. Magnitude of range weakens the degree — produces shallowness. On the whole it is of more use if a few have profound knowledge in the science — treating it with thoroughness — than if it is generally diffused.]",["'Weitläufigkeit' rendered 'extensiveness'.","'tiefe Erkenntnis' rendered 'profound knowledge'; the German lacks an article before it."]],"dohna-s11-003":["Mathematics, for example, is extensive magnitude. One must restrict the plurality of modes of knowledge in order to produce a unity.",["'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","The sense of the example is obscure; translated as it stands."]],"dohna-s11-004":["The author always speaks of a learned knowledge. But this expression is improper. Knowledge can be called scientific if it is scientific [*wissenschaftlich*] and systematic. For this a chief principle is required, just as, for example, in history one assumes civil constitution as the chief principle. History, then, can also be regarded as a science if it is brought into a system, which one derives from a chief principle.",["'scientifisch' and 'wissenschaftlich' both rendered 'scientific'; the German distinguishes them, the English cannot.","'was man ableitet' resolved as 'which one derives': the relative refers to the system."]],"dohna-s11-005":["The whole doctrine of elements is nothing further than common knowledge [*gemeine Erkenntnis*], and the whole of logic ought properly to be treated in such a way that the common man can understand it without any other knowledge. In respect of objects a mode of knowledge may be extensively great: in respect of objects, that is, of their multiplicity and manifold application, as in mathematics. Popularity, too, concerns not how much <(quantum)>, but how many kinds <*quot*> we know. This serves for using knowledge in society. In an age enlightenment may be extensively great, if it extends to all, and yet intensively small, since no one has very much of it, but all have only a little. Such people are so-called *bureaux d'esprit*, like Madame Geoffrin, who was famous on that account. It is just as when money is beaten very flat: it does indeed occupy a large space, but is thin everywhere and is easily damaged. The extensive magnitude of enlightenment can also be entitled the subjective, since it extends to all subjects; the intensive magnitude is the objective, which concerns the degree.",["Restated the third and fourth sentences slightly for English flow; the sense is unchanged.","The angle brackets around *quantum* and *quot* are garbled in the OCR; I closed the first bracket and otherwise kept them as the Academy apparatus.","*Erkenntnis* is rendered 'knowledge', or 'mode of knowledge' where an individual item is meant; 'Elementarlehre' is rendered 'doctrine of elements'; 'Objekte' is rendered 'objects'; 'heißen' is rendered 'is entitled'.","The OCR reads 'em' for 'ein'; I corrected it silently."]],"dohna-s11-006":["The author now speaks again: Of the fruitfulness and importance of a mode of knowledge",["The OCR has a stray space before the colon, which I removed. *Einer Erkenntnis* is feminine, so 'a mode of knowledge' is a choice."]],"dohna-s11-007":["*Fecunditas et dignitas*. Both concern what is intensively great. The former rests on the multitude (of the consequences), the latter on the [importance of the consequences]. We also have modes of knowledge that are at once fruitful and important. [Everything is logically important that cultivates the understanding. Hence mathematics is so in quite an eminent degree, on account of its clearness, distinctness and certainty. — Distinctness: where the marks [*Merkmale*] of the concept are clear. If the marks of the marks are so, Wolff entitled it completeness [*Vollständigkeit*]; yet the partial concepts may still always have others under them, and here therefore there is | no | limit short of the simple representations.]",["The square brackets and the signs | are carried over as they stand. The nested brackets around *Merkmale* and *Vollständigkeit* are mine.","*Merkmal* is rendered 'mark', Kant's usual term in the logic lectures. *Teilbegriffe* is rendered 'partial concepts'.","The sentence beginning 'Wenn die Merkmale von den Merkmalen' is elliptical; I supplied only the 'so' (clear) that the sense requires."]],"dohna-s11-008":["We now come to a matter that already belongs to logic proper, namely",["The sentence breaks off before the heading in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-009":["Of the truth of learned knowledge",[]],"dohna-s11-010":["Logic begins with clear concepts, but gives no rules as to how they are to be made clear. For logic presupposes consciousness of representations. Thus logic properly teaches only of the distinctness of concepts.",["The OCR reads 'Idaren', corrected silently to 'klaren'."]],"dohna-s11-011":["We have now spoken of the quantity and quality of knowledge, and we now come to the",["The sentence breaks off; the next passage supplies the heading (III. Relation)."]],"dohna-s11-012":["III. Relation of the representations in our knowledge. The agreement of representations with their object is entitled truth. — In this chapter we want to have a criterion of truth which holds in all modes of knowledge without distinction. [The sceptics said: All judgments are made at random — [*diallelos*?], always the grounded resting on the ground, and this again on the grounded, thus in a circle.]",["The Greek word is badly garbled in the OCR ('SiaÄXr|Ar)'). It is probably *diallēlos* (circular reasoning), which fits the 'circle'. I give it only as a doubtful reading, in brackets.","'Auf Geratewohl' is rendered 'at random'; 'im Zirkel' is rendered 'in a circle'."]],"dohna-s11-013":["[There is, however, a universal formal criterion of truth: the agreement of it with itself. A material one, agreement with the object, cannot be universally possible; for it would have to hold (if it were universal) even when I abstract from all matter, and the agreement of my knowledge of the object with my knowledge of the object would be <a> tautological criterion. The sceptics therefore overthrew the latter; the former they could not attack.]",["Divided the long sentence at the dash for English clarity.","'Derselben' is ambiguous: it could be truth or knowledge. I took it as 'knowledge', hence 'agreement of it with itself'. The sense is the same either way.","'Dies' (the one overthrown) is the material criterion, and 'jenes' is the formal criterion; I made this explicit as 'the latter' and 'the former'.","The OCR 'WTahrheit' was corrected silently; '<ein)' is restored as '<a>'."]],"dohna-s11-014":["The universal criterion of truth will properly always be only formal, abstracting from all content and difference of knowledge. It will therefore never tell me whether my knowledge agrees with the object, but only whether my knowledge agrees with itself [*mit mir selbst*, 'with myself']. The universal criterion of truth will not be an *organon*, but only the *conditio sine qua non*, the indispensable condition of truth. This is that in thinking we are at one with ourselves. For how could a mode of knowledge that <is at variance with itself agree with the object?> The criterion must consist in the rule by which alone the understanding agrees with itself in thinking. It is not sufficiently material, but it is nevertheless indispensably necessary. A material criterion of truth cannot possibly be universal and hold for all objects. One may well have material criteria of truth, but they cannot be universal. They would be those which we have from the constitution of the object in respect of the senses.",["The OCR shows braces and a parenthesis ('{sich selbst uneins ist, ... stimmen ?)'). I took these to be the apparatus's angle brackets and rendered them as such; the reading is uncertain.","A stray 'f.' after 'allgemein sein' is probably an OCR remnant of a footnote or apparatus mark; I omitted it.","*Mit mir selbst* is kept as 'with itself', with the German noted, since the shift from 'mir' to 'sich' is Kant's own in the transcript.","'Beschaffenheit' is rendered 'constitution'."]],"dohna-s11-015":["[Hour]",["*Stunde* is the transcriber's marker for the start of a new lecture hour."]],"dohna-s11-016":["Through intuition the object is given, and through concepts it is thought. The universal criterion of truth is merely negative. The sceptics may be divided into Pyrrhonists and Academics, the latter of whom carried it furthest. The principle of contradiction is the first formal criterion of truth. For truth it is not enough that a judgment be possible; truth might be entitled logical actuality. If one says: *posito* a triangle has 6 sides, this is a problematic judgment; if one says: a hexagon has 6 sides, it is a proposition [*Satz*].",["'Wirklichkeit' is rendered 'actuality'; 'Satz' is rendered 'proposition' and marked, since it is here opposed to a merely problematic judgment, i.e. an assertoric one."]],"dohna-s11-017":["The first criterion of truth (after the principle of contradiction)",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-018":["is the principle of problematic judgments: possibility. The",["The passage breaks off at 'The', which continues with the next item."]],"dohna-s11-019":["2. is the principle of assertoric judgments, in which [is] logical actuality [— truth].",["'[is]' is supplied; the source has no verb. '[— Wahrheit]' is an editorial bracket in the original."]],"dohna-s11-020":["The 2nd principle is the principle of sufficient reason.",["*Satz des zureichenden Grundes* is rendered 'principle of sufficient reason'."]],"dohna-s11-021":["[Cause is that which lies at the ground of all appearances.]",["'Erscheinungen' is rendered 'appearances'. The text is bracketed as in the source."]],"dohna-s11-022":["3. Every mode of knowledge has some consequence, and if even one consequence is false, the knowledge too is false. If all the consequences are true, the knowledge is true; but who can know all the consequences? That requires omniscience. One can therefore infer from the consequences to the grounds. From the falsity of the consequences one can infer only to the falsity of the ground, but from this [one can infer] to the truth of the opposite. [ — 106]",["'[one can infer]' is supplied to complete the elliptical last clause. 'Dieser' refers to the falsity of the ground.","'[ — 106]' is probably a reference to Meier's § 106; carried over as it stands."]],"dohna-s11-023":["[ad § 109.] A false judgment is always an error. In this case, however, one must prove that it conflicts with the criterion of truth. For error it is required that we hold a false judgment to be true. We are, unfortunately, filled with errors, but in order to forestall them we must first know whence they have arisen.",["'§ 109' refers to Meier's *Auszug*. The first sentence is transmitted as it stands; the transcript may be compressed, since error properly requires holding the false to be true, as the third sentence says."]],"dohna-s11-024":["[Truth and error cannot possibly be contained in intuitions, but only in judgments. Hence there are more errors in an academy of sciences than in a village full of peasants, because there more judging goes on; whoever never judges will never err.]",["'Unmöglich enthalten — sondern in den Urteilen' is smoothed to 'cannot possibly be contained ... but only in judgments' (the German is irregular; nothing of the sense is lost).","Divided the second sentence at the dash only by punctuation, not by content."]],"dohna-s11-025":["Neither truth nor error rests in the concepts of our understanding, but only in its (judgments). Judgment is an act of the understanding. Truth, however, according to its formal criterion, is the agreement of a judgment with the laws of the understanding. Error, however, is a deviation of the judgment from the laws of the understanding. — [If, then, I isolate the understanding, so that I take away sensibility, it could not err (for no force can produce an effect except according to certain laws). Only when I take an influence of sensibility for an *actus* of the understanding is this error. All our errors are deflections [*Lenkungen*] of judgments into the diagonal, into the transverse [*in die Quere*] (the word perhaps comes from the English *square*, quadrat) — ].",["The parentheses around 'judgments' are in the source and are kept.","'Kraft' is rendered 'force'; 'Sinnlichkeit' is rendered 'sensibility'; *actus* is kept in Latin, italicised.","The remark on the derivation of *Quere* from English 'square' is the transcriber's (or Kant's) conjecture, so I left it as 'perhaps'. The trailing dash and bracket are carried over as they stand; the passage may break off here."]],"dohna-s11-026":["How is it possible that a power [*Kraft*] departs from its own laws? — The limits of the human understanding are not the ground of errors. They are indeed grounds of a great deficiency in our knowledge (namely ignorance, which we recognise only after much rational science), but not of contradiction. Now since error is nothing negative, and the understanding by itself alone cannot err, it must be something positive: sensibility. The *oppositum* of the understanding, however, does not judge at all, and we have no other source. We see, then, that error comes about through the connection of understanding and sensibility [by the latter we mean the subjective element in our representations]. In so far as this ground lies in sensibility, we call it illusion [*Schein*]. This is usually explained thus: the subjective, which is falsely taken for something objective. [The ground of all error is a subjective ground of our judgment, which we regard as objective. A man readily believes what he wishes. Wish is a subjective | ground, | but for belief one must have an objective one.]",["OCR 'Sehern' read as 'Schein' (illusion); '<' and '{' before parentheses read as '('.","'Kraft' rendered 'power', with the German given; the context is the understanding as a faculty.","'Vernunft-Wissenschaft' rendered 'rational science'.","Restated 'Da es nun nichts Negatives ist' by naming the subject, 'error', which the German leaves implicit. The brackets and | marks are carried over as in the source."]],"dohna-s11-027":["Sensibility also has its own laws. Combined in one judgment, they can well agree with one another. [Error arises] solely in the combination of two heterogeneous determining grounds. The matter of errors is therefore neither true nor false.",["The second and third sentences are fragmentary in the transcript. 'Error arises' is supplied in square brackets to complete the sense; the German has only 'Lediglich in der Verbindung …'.","'sie' is taken to refer to understanding and sensibility, with their respective laws."]],"dohna-s11-028":["[*Porismata*: All errors are culpable, for all judgments are voluntary [*willkürlich*], and only through judgments are errors possible.",["*Porismata* (corollaries) kept in Latin. The opening bracket is left unclosed, as in the source; the bracket closes in the next passage."]],"dohna-s11-029":["No error is a wholly false judgment. The judgment may be correct in form.]",[]],"dohna-s12-001":["[Hour, Friday, 18 May]",[]],"dohna-s12-002":["Every error must be explained. We have internal marks of truth, which are grounded in our understanding, and secondly external marks. To the former belong the principle of contradiction (the logical criterion of possibility, for problematic judgments), and the second principle, that a judgment, as a proposition, must always be grounded (the logical criterion of actuality, for assertoric judgments; the principle of sufficient reason). The third (the logical criterion of truth (necessity)) is this: if one cannot know the truth of a proposition from its grounds, one can yet know it from its consequences. [Here one cannot judge by a sense of truth, any more than one can judge duty by moral feeling. One can never judge through the senses, only through the understanding. —]",["Silently corrected OCR 'erstem' to 'ersten', 'Wirkhchkeit' to 'Wirklichkeit', and the '{' and '<' before parentheses to '('.","Divided the long opening into short sentences and supplied 'is this' before the third principle, whose German has no verb.","'Merkmale' rendered 'marks' and 'Kriterium' 'criterion'."]],"dohna-s12-003":["The external criteria (marks) of truth consist in the agreement of the judgments of others with our own. This is properly not a logical but a psychological criterion. It is merely by comparing one's judgment with another's that one recognises whether the determining ground of our judgment is subjective or objective: the latter is universally valid, | the former | is not. [(Many exceptions to a rule indicate its defectiveness.)]",["OCR 'kern logisches' read as 'kein logisches'.","'der letztere … der erstere' refers to the objective and the subjective ground respectively. The | marks are kept where they stand."]],"dohna-s12-004":["In logic, sensibility is called the subjective [element] of our knowledge.",["'Element' is supplied in brackets. The stray 'f' before 'Erkenntnis' is OCR noise and omitted."]],"dohna-s12-005":["[*Principium rationati*: nothing without a ground; everything must have its ground. This is the criterion from the consequences. It is negative and sure. If the consequences are false, so certainly is the ground. One cannot infer conversely in this way: if the consequences are true, etc. For there can be several grounds. *Principium rationis*: every proposition is itself a ground and has its consequences.]",["'Rationati' is the reading of the OCR text and is kept as printed. It is presumably the traditional *principium rationati* (principle of the grounded), correlative to *principium rationis*.","'nihil sine ratione' is left in the Latin of the source, as it stands in the German context, and not italicised separately."]],"dohna-s12-006":["[It is an impulse of nature, a calling, to communicate one's modes of knowledge to others. Hence freedom of communication is a right of humanity (only when it could be wholly contrary to the interest of the state might a prohibition on the part of the authorities take place.)",["'Erkenntnisse' (plural) rendered 'modes of knowledge'. The opening bracket is left unclosed, as in the source; it closes in the next passage."]],"dohna-s12-007":["Possession of the power of judgment [*Urteilskraft*] is nature's greatest gift. A lack of it can be found even where the understanding is otherwise good.]",[]],"dohna-s12-008":["In a certain way it can be said of all modes of knowledge that one understands them properly only when one can make them oneself, that is, not merely copy them but derive them from oneself.",[]],"dohna-s12-009":["No judgment would be found that was wholly false. This cannot occur so long as our judgments are derived from the understanding.",["'Dies kann solange nicht stattfinden': 'Dies' is taken to be the existence of a wholly false judgment."]],"dohna-s12-010":["One cannot properly convince anyone of the opposite of his opinion unless one tells him how he came to it, from his particular point of view, so that his judgment is shown to be not objective but merely subjective. Thus, according to our subjective judgment, our final end is happiness; that pleases us, and that we want. But if we ask what we ought to do, and look to the objective, then morality and duty must take this place. By the method just indicated one gains in critical power of understanding. So it is said that a great book contains great errors, and this is very natural.",["'Verstandesvermögen' rendered 'power of understanding'; the OCR split 'Verstandes vermögen' was joined.","'Glückseligkeit' rendered 'happiness'.","The first sentence was lightly recast, with 'so that' supplied to link 'daß also sein Urteil …' to what precedes."]],"dohna-s12-011":["There now follows a distinction between",["Breaks off; the two members follow in the next passages."]],"dohna-s12-012":["1. partial truth (tolerably true, e.g. in measurements) [since, that is, taking what is partially true as wholly true does not conflict with the purpose, although this involves a certain inexactness. —] and",["OCR 'näm-Hch' read as 'nämlich'.","The passage is a fragment: the governing phrase is in the previous passage, and 'partial' is taken as modifying 'Wahrheit' from item 2."]],"dohna-s12-013":["2. total truth.",[]],"dohna-s12-014":["Someone can tell the truth, but not the whole truth. But one must take special care that it never runs counter to the ends of knowledge.",["'sie' is taken to refer to the truth (partial truth) as told."]],"dohna-s12-015":["Now the author speaks of the",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s12-016":["precise and coarse knowledge. The latter does not yet indicate that it contains something false, but only that it is not wholly true. Every mode of knowledge is either *cognitio vaga* or *cognitio determinata*. Thus, for example, *medium tenuere beati*: what is good, and what is too much and too little, such men do not know. This is a *regula vaga*, which determines nothing at all, a merely tautological wisdom. 'Be wise, make yourself perfect!' Well, in what does that consist? One takes it one way, another another. Determinate rules too can be *regulae late* and *stricte determinatae*. Many definitions are of the first kind. A mode of knowledge is entitled exact when no latitude for error is contained in it. A knowledge is entitled precise [*genau*] in respect of the object, and subtle in respect of the subject, for which much attention is needed. He who makes the small, unsuitably to the purpose, into the use of his knowledge is entitled a micrologist. Where something is practical, subtlety cannot be censured.",["Begins in mid-construction ('[of the] precise and coarse knowledge'), continuing the previous passage; 'Erkenntnis' is singular here, so 'knowledge'.","'genau' rendered 'precise' and 'exakt' 'exact', to keep the two apart; *medium tenuere beati* is a Latin tag, left untranslated.","OCR '»Subjekts' read as 'Subjekts'. 'Spielraum' rendered 'latitude'."]],"dohna-s12-017":["[Hour, 21st May]",[]],"dohna-s12-018":["Casuistry consists in a multitude of useless subtleties, in which cases have been devised that never occur in common life. There were ages in which subtleties and micrology counted for an astonishing amount.",[]],"dohna-s12-019":["There is a certain pettiness of mind [*Kleingeisterei*] which one does not always notice. The people who have it can never apply a part of knowledge to the whole system of it. This is a great touchstone, from which one can at once infer the spirit of the man. There are differences between head and spirit [*Kopf* and *Geist*]. Head is the faculty of the understanding, but spirit the faculty of principles.",["'Vermögen' rendered 'faculty' here. 'Geist' is rendered 'spirit', which is a choice."]],"dohna-s12-020":["[That is aesthetically true which agrees with universal semblance [*Schein*], e.g. the rising and setting of the sun; it is therefore very different from logical truth. From error one does not pass straight to truth, but only through the consciousness of ignorance.]",["'Schein' rendered 'semblance' here, in the sense of how things appear generally, in contrast to 'illusion' in dohna-s11-026."]],"dohna-s12-021":["A knowledge exact in respect of the object (*exacta*) is opposed to the crude (*rudi*). *Cognitio vaga*: 'do too much to no thing, too little to none', that is saying almost nothing. Those are called Latitudinarians who believe that the lawgiver commands too strictly, and that between his laws and their transgression there is still a margin. Here, to be sure, there is nothing firm, and we must always take pains that our knowledge be strict and precisely determined. This is the advantage of geometry.",["'Kenntnis' rendered 'knowledge' here, as the text has it, and not 'Erkenntnis'.","'keinem Dinge zu viel, keinem zu wenig tun zu' is somewhat garbled in the OCR (the final 'zu' is doubtful); translated as it can be made out."]],"dohna-s12-022":["Then there is also a *cognitio crassa*, 'wholesale' [*über Pausch und Bogen*], an estimate of the whole without particular judgment of the individual [parts]. [Crass knowledge not only has no exactitude [*exactitudo*], but it also corrupts men, in that it gives occasion to errors.]",["'[parts]' is supplied by me; the German has only 'des Einzelnen' ('of the individual').","'über Pausch und Bogen' is an idiom for 'in the lump'; the German is given."]],"dohna-s12-023":["The word 'truth' can be used substantively and adjectivally, for instance when we think truth in general, or the truth of a proposition. A mode of knowledge that is determined in respect of its object is entitled truth. This rational knowledge [*Rationalerkenntnis*] is always apodeictic, because we are conscious not merely of its truth but also of its necessity. Apodeictic knowledge is either through concepts or through the construction of concepts. In the first case it is dogmatic, in the second mathematical. An apodeictically certain mode of knowledge from concepts is entitled a *dogma*; an apodeictically certain mode of knowledge from the construction of concepts, a *mathema*. —",["'Substantive und adjektive' read as adverbs ('substantively and adjectivally'); the OCR/transcript form is loose.","*Erkenntnis* is rendered 'knowledge', and 'mode of knowledge' where an individual item is meant (e.g. 'ein Erkenntnis').","'Wahrheit' in the second sentence: the transcript's statement is compressed and possibly garbled; I translate it as it stands.","*Dogma* and *mathema* are kept in Latin/Greek form, italicised; 'apodiktisch' rendered 'apodeictic'."]],"dohna-s12-024":["[An apodeictic proposition: Everything is contingent — (has a cause) — 2 − 4 = 4 [sic]. The latter is intuition, the former only concept. Both are objectively equally certain, but subjectively there is a great difference. The mathematician can rest in his work, since intuition remains to him; this is not so with the philosopher, and hence his work is far more wearying.]",["'2-4 = 4' is as printed (probably a slip or OCR error in the source, e.g. for '2+2 = 4'); I have not corrected it.","'Dies ... dort' resolved as 'the latter ... the former' on the sense: the arithmetical example is intuition, the metaphysical proposition is concept.","The editors' square brackets are retained."]],"dohna-s12-025":["The propositions of morals and metaphysics are all *dogmata*. —",["*Dogmata* kept as Latin plural."]],"dohna-s12-026":["Because physics is only the application of those modes of knowledge, it has apodeictic certainty. A system is a whole (of dogmatic truths) from principles. [A system is distinguished from an aggregate in that the latter is the whole to which the parts are antecedent.]",["OCR '{dogmatischer Wahrheiten)' read as '(dogmatischer Wahrheiten)'.","'dem die Teile vorhergehen': the parts precede the whole in an aggregate, whereas in a system the whole precedes the parts; the German is compressed."]],"dohna-s12-027":["The principle of history is time — chronology.",[]],"dohna-s13-001":["[Lecture hour, 22nd May]",["'Stunde' rendered 'lecture hour'."]],"dohna-s13-002":["Of error in itself one cannot say that it is unavoidable, but one may well say this of ignorance. For we have no need to judge about everything, and if we do so, it should be done only problematically. [There is no *error invincibilis* — though there are errors that are practically hard to avoid. Mark: incompatibility with the judgment of others.] An unavoidable error always presupposes the necessity of judging. [For if the judgment were not necessary, one could easily avoid the error by not judging at all. —]",["OCR 'kernen error' corrected to 'keinen error'.","'Merkmal' here is 'mark' (the sign by which such error is recognised); the bracketed remark is terse and I have kept it so."]],"dohna-s13-003":["He who accepts a manifestly absurd judgment acts in a tasteless [*abgeschmackt*] manner. [What even the *sensus communis* already recognises as such — which does not even have appearance on its side. — (Logically considered no error is dangerous — here we do not look to the matter.) — If once one declares someone's judgments | to be | absurd, and thereby denies him understanding, no *discours* helps to bring him away from them. Whoever engages in it, therefore, himself acts absurdly.]",["*abgeschmackt* is rendered 'tasteless' in the first sentence and 'absurd' for the bracketed remark, where it qualifies judgments; it spans both senses.","The apparatus signs | are retained where they stand.","*discours* kept as in the source."]],"dohna-s13-004":["In respect of what is manifestly false no error is possible, but only in such judgments as those in which the falsity lies hidden. Mathematics has this peculiarity alone, that one perceives the falsity of a proposition in it instantly. To remove the hidden ground of error is entitled to unravel the illusion [*den Schein entwickeln*]. If someone asserts something whose falsity he clearly sees and yet does not give it up, the blame falls more on his will than on his understanding. A gross error is one whose avoidance is easy; it is not the same as the tasteless [*abgeschmackt*] error. An error is dangerous when we are concerned not merely to extend our knowledge but, for instance, always ask first after utility [which we then perhaps cannot yet see. This is what the shallowest minds usually do]. One who, when he is to show the falsity of a mode of knowledge, shows its disadvantage, is called a *Consequentiarius*, because he always infers from the consequences to the ground [— he begins with the practical].",["The sentence 'Ein gefährlicher Irrtum, wenn ...' is incomplete and obscure in the transcript; I have supplied only 'is' and translated it as it stands, and the sense is doubtful.","'heißt ... den Schein entwickeln' rendered 'is entitled to unravel the illusion', following the method's 'is entitled'; 'to remove ... is entitled' is stiff but literal.","*Consequentiarius* kept in Latin."]],"dohna-s13-005":["The author now speaks, in the fifth section,",["The sentence runs on into the next passage, which gives the title."]],"dohna-s13-006":["Of the clarity of knowledge",[]],"dohna-s13-007":["Clarity is consciousness <not> only of the representations as a whole, but also of their partial representations. A mark is a partial representation, in so far as it is a ground of knowledge of the whole representation. A ground of knowledge is twofold:",["The angle brackets around 'nicht' are the editors' and are kept; the text as printed seems to make the definition negative, so the reading is doubtful.","*Merkmal* rendered 'mark'; *Erkenntnisgrund* 'ground of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s13-008":["1. a ground for knowing a thing in itself, or the inner ground of knowledge;",[]],"dohna-s13-009":["2. a ground for knowing a thing from other things alongside it, or the outer ground. —",[]],"dohna-s13-010":["In comparison with others we know either",[]],"dohna-s13-011":["1. through the *nota* [*nota*, a partial representation that underlies the concept as a mark] *identitatis* [mark, token] and",["*nota identitatis* kept in Latin."]],"dohna-s13-012":["2. through the *nota diversitatis* [sign of distinction, e.g. between man and animal].",[]],"dohna-s13-013":["Partial representations as grounds of knowledge can be partial concepts and partial intuitions. The latter do not occur in logic.",[]],"dohna-s13-014":["A concept is the representation of that which contains several representations within itself. The author makes a distinction between mediate and immediate representations. All combinations are either combinations of co-ordination or of subordination. The former produce a whole which is entitled an aggregate. They arise through the combination of the manifold. In so far as the manifold is subordinated to one another, it is entitled a series. Here there is a *terminus a priori* and a *terminus a posteriori*; in logic only the *a parte ante*, not the latter, the *a parte post*. [A mediate mark is a *nota notae*, a mark of a mark. If one finally comes in the series of mutually subordinated representations to one that is indivisible, this is entitled the *terminus a parte ante*. This can be divided, e.g. *quid*; the *a parte post* cannot be proved, only illustrated.]",["'Sofern es einander subordiniert ist': 'it' is the manifold; I kept this, though the German is loose.","'a terminus a priori': the transcript writes 'terminum' in the accusative; given in nominative form.","The bracketed remark that the *terminus a parte ante* 'can be divided' sits oddly after 'indivisible'; the transcript may be corrupt, and I translate it as it stands."]],"dohna-s13-015":["Marks are here divided:",[]],"dohna-s13-016":["1. into affirmative and",[]],"dohna-s13-017":["2. into negative. But this belongs properly more to judgment than to marks. [Negative marks by which I designate as error something into which, without that, no one would have fallen, are superfluous; so too affirmative marks that say nothing new or unknown — tautological.] The expression 'negative marks' points to the matter, and that does not belong to logic. All negations presuppose affirmations. Thus among savages there is no poverty, for this would occur only in comparison with the well-to-do. Since they do not know the positive, they form no representation of the negative. So too no one born blind can form a representation of darkness, because he does not know light.",["OCR 'Wüden' corrected to 'Wilden' (savages); 'kern' to 'kein'."]],"dohna-s13-018":["A mark is important if it is a ground of knowledge of great consequences. [Marks in comparison with others (according to diversity or identity) are not so important as those by derivation, the inner marks. Importance is: 1. logical, e.g. three terms in the syllogism; 2. practical. — Thus logical and grammatical incorrectness can be practically unimportant (in a certain respect).]",["'3 termini im Syllogismus' is elliptical; presumably a syllogism with more than three terms is meant as logical unimportance of the number — I translate as it stands."]],"dohna-s13-019":["If it serves to distinguish a ground of knowledge which is itself great in degree, then it is entitled (where the mark is determined in respect of degree) intensive; if the mark has reference to quantity, multiplicity [in general to extension], extensive. A mark is sufficient if it contains the ground of all distinguishing marks [suffices for the derivation], insufficient if it contains the ground of only some distinguishing signs. But the insufficient ground is not to be rejected. For it always contains a part of the ground, as do conjectures [without which we never arrive at certainty].",["'heißt dies ... intensiv': supplied 'it is entitled' as the method's rendering of *heißen*.","'Bestimmte' in 'dem Grade nach bestimmt' rendered 'determined'."]],"dohna-s13-020":["Marks are further divided:",[]],"dohna-s13-021":["1. into absolutely necessary, unchangeable, and",[]],"dohna-s13-022":["2. into contingent.",[]],"dohna-s13-023":["Those are necessary without which the thing absolutely cannot be thought. One obtains them through the mere analysis of the concept. [Essence is the sum-total of the supreme marks of a thing, *complexus notarum conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*. First, completeness [*completudo*] is required; secondly, that the marks be not derived from others but primitive — and inseparable. The logical essence is the inner possibility.]",["The Latin definition is kept as printed; 'conceptum' is as in the transcript (the classical form would be *conceptum*, accusative singular, so it is not an OCR slip but probably the student's error), and *completudo* is likewise as printed.","'Wesen' rendered 'essence'; 'Zergliederung' 'analysis'."]],"dohna-s13-024":["In logic there is never any question of the marks of the thing; in that case logic would be concerned with matter. It deals only with the marks of the concept. What is physically necessary may be only contingent logically. For example, it is physically necessary that all bodies fall, but this lies only in the thing, and is logically only contingent. The *notae logicae necessariae* [necessary logical marks] belong to the *esse* of the concept. These are *ad esse conceptus necessariae, pertinent ad essentiam* [necessary to the being of the concept; they pertain to the essence]. [Man is mortal, can err: *extraessentiale*, that is, what does not belong essentially to it.] These marks cannot be removed without removing the thing itself. — *Melius esse logicum* [the logical being is the better], as with the *beatitudo juridica* [juridical blessedness], when it is said *beati possidentes* [blessed are those who possess].",["OCR '■' before 'was nicht wesentlich' is a stray mark; omitted. The dash before it is kept.","The sentence 'Sie würde sich in dem Fall mit Materie beschäftigen' is rendered with 'logic' as subject; the 'Sache/Materie' contrast is the point.","English glosses of the Latin tags are my supplied additions, in square brackets."]],"dohna-s13-025":["The necessary marks of things belong to the thing itself [or *ad esse*]:",["OCR 'f' before 'esse' is a stray mark (perhaps a cross-reference sign); omitted. 'od.' read as 'oder'."]],"dohna-s13-026":["1. As *constitutiva*, either as component parts (*essentialia*) [essential parts] — belonging to the concept | originarie | [*originarie*: extension].",["OCR garbled: 'Bestandstücke «(essentialia)' read as 'Bestandstücke (essentialia)'. The signs | are carried over as they stand. 'originarie' (originally, primitively) left in Latin."]],"dohna-s13-027":["2. As *rationata*, or as consequences (*attributa*), which belong to the concept *derivative* [derivatively]; for example, the concept of a triangle — the consequences are whether the angles are right [angles] or the like.",["OCR 'alsrationata' read as 'als rationata'; '<attributa)' read as '(attributa)'; 'f' before 'oder' is a stray mark and is omitted. The ending 'ob die Winkel recht ... oder usw.' is itself fragmentary; 'right [angles]' is supplied."]],"dohna-s13-028":["In corporeal things divisibility is a mark that *pertinet ad essentiam* [pertains to the essence], but not as a *constitutivum*, only as a *rationatum*. [From the mark that the essence is corporeal, divisibility can be derived.]",[]],"dohna-s13-029":["The former are also entitled *essentialia*, essential parts [the sum total of all *essentialia* is entitled the essence]; the latter, *attributa* (properties) [these are 1. *communia*; 2. *propria*].",["'Wesen' rendered 'essence' (cf. *essentia*)."]],"dohna-s13-030":["The contingent marks are either:",[]],"dohna-s13-031":["1. *notae internae contingentes* — *modi*, for example learning | or |",["'Gelehrsamkeit' rendered 'learning'. The signs | are carried over as in the source."]],"dohna-s13-032":["2. *notae externae contingentes* — that is, relations.",[]],"dohna-s13-033":["The tabular division of the marks (predicates) would therefore be:",[]],"dohna-s13-034":["1. *Ad essentiam pertinentia* [things pertaining to the essence], without which the thing cannot be thought.",[]],"dohna-s13-035":["a) *ut rationata sive attributa* [as *rationata*, or *attributa*];",["OCR 'sive attributa ;' standardised; Latin glossed in brackets by me."]],"dohna-s13-036":["b) *ut constitutiva sive essentialia* [as *constitutiva*, or *essentialia*].",[]],"dohna-s13-037":["[One can often derive one concept from another, [and] convert them reciprocally. This gives the surest mark.]",["'reciprocieren' is a bare infinitive after 'kann'; I supply 'and' to join it. 'Convert reciprocally' is the logical sense (*reciprocari*)."]],"dohna-s13-038":["2. *Extraessentialia* [without which the concept | can be thought |], for example a triangle — here the angles are undetermined.",["The | signs fall in the middle of the bracket in the source and are kept in place."]],"dohna-s13-039":["a) *Externa* — relation, the external determination, whether he is father or son [the external determinations of the marks are always contingent];",["'Bestimmung' = determination. 'er' has no clear antecedent (a man in the example); left as in the German."]],"dohna-s13-040":["b) *Interna*: the internal determinations of a thing, in so far as they are not necessary, are entitled *modi*, for example learned or unlearned.",[]],"dohna-s13-041":["When we speak of the essence of things, we speak of the logical essence (which consists in the concept), not of the real essence (nature), with which metaphysics deals. [The sum total of all those internal determinations which contain the first ground of the existence of a thing is the natural essence. — This is very hard to grasp; the logical essence is very easy, for it lies merely in the concept. —]",["OCR 'hegt' corrected to 'liegt'. 'Realwesen' = real essence; 'Naturwesen' = natural essence."]],"dohna-s13-042":["*Essentia est complexus notarum conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium* [Essence is the sum total of the marks which primitively constitute some concept]. The logical essence is the sum total of those marks which are sufficient to derive what belongs to the essence. The logical essence is easy to know. For here one has nothing more to do than to analyse the concepts. The real essence we can never fully comprehend; for example, we can never experience all the marks of water, however far physics may advance. Some logical concepts are nevertheless hard to define, for example the concept of virtue. There one is still far from having found all the marks. The following are named: a skill in lawful actions which are freely performed; but this is not yet enough [it is moral strength in following them, in struggle against hindrances]. Body can be defined completely: a filling of space shows only matter, but the description of its figure according to the three dimensions indicates body.",["The sentence 'das, was zum Wesen gehört, abzuleiten' is kept close to the German; it is somewhat circular as written. 'Fertigkeit' = skill. 'Tugend' = virtue.","OCR 'heraus.Man' split into two sentences; 'definieren ,' spacing corrected."]],"dohna-s13-043":["The author now speaks",["Breaks off here; the next passage continues with 'Von dunkeln Vorstellungen'. Left uncompleted."]],"dohna-s13-044":["Of obscure representations",["'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'dunkel' = obscure."]],"dohna-s13-045":["Suppose the representation to be distinct, that is, its partial representations clear — for example right: here constraint is present; equity is distinguished from it; this is very hard to see. We can make obscure representations clear by means of examples, but this clarification is not logical, only aesthetic.",["'Recht' in the example is 'right' (Recht), with 'Zwang' (constraint) and 'Billigkeit' (equity). 'deutlich' = distinct."]],"dohna-s13-046":["[Obscurity is subjective when the ground lies in the subject, objective when it lies in the object. — An obscure representation is one of which one is not conscious; this seems to be a contradiction. One cannot [grasp] it immediately, but one can nevertheless | grasp | it mediately by inference.]",["The text is awkward: 'man kann es nicht unmittelbar, aber doch mittelbar durch Schluß | erfassen |' — the verb stands between | signs, apparently an editorial insertion, and I have supplied '[grasp]' in the first clause to complete the construction.","'Die deren' is read as 'one of which'."]],"dohna-s13-047":["A discourse is often called obscure because it is logically distinct, but aesthetically has not clarity enough — no suitable examples can be given [for example body: an extended impenetrable figure]. Virtue can be distinguished from holiness; for in the latter there are no temptations to evil at all [for example the angels]. This has otherwise been forgotten. In our distinct knowledge we can still think a perfection, *completudo* (fullness of detail — full distinctness) [in a concept where there are coordinate marks, as depth is through subordinate marks — if I have here even a single one, it can be *nota superior* — [marks] can be traced back to simple concepts]. The greatest distinctness through subordinate marks is entitled the depth of knowledge. Both together constitute thoroughness.",["Bracketed sentence in the source is syntactically broken ('sich zurückführen lassen' lacks a subject); I supply '[marks]' and leave the irregularity. 'Gründlichkeit' = thoroughness. OCR 'aiis' read as 'aus'. 'Completudo' OCR '{' and ')' mismatch normalised. 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection; 'Erkenntnis' = knowledge."]],"dohna-s14-001":["[... hour]",["OCR 'nte' is the end of an ordinal ('[…]nte Stunde'); the number is lost."]],"dohna-s14-002":["Profound distinctness is always harder to understand, because it is more abstract. To attain full distinctness one must always seek out the marks of the marks, until one finally arrives at the highest marks, at the simple concepts. With this we may compare vivid knowledge, or rather mode of representation (consciousness is vivid in it). [The more this representation produces changes in the subject, the more vivid is this science | by examples |.] This aesthetically greater clarity may be called vividness. The logically greater clarity is distinctness.",["OCR 'f' in the bracket is a stray mark; I read it as an editorial sign and keep a | sign in its place with 'by examples' placed between. 'Wissenschaft' is odd here (perhaps 'Erkenntnis' intended); translated as 'science' as written. 'Vorstellungsart' = mode of representation."]],"dohna-s14-003":["[Comprehensibility and clarity are to be distinguished. By the resolution of the marks the concept becomes harder to grasp — on account of subtlety. All making distinct rests on the development of the partial representations (in so far as these are not clear of themselves).]",["'Faßlichkeit' = comprehensibility. OCR 'Subtilität.Alle' split into separate sentences."]],"dohna-s14-004":["All our representations can have a twofold relation: first to the subject, and then they are entitled sensations; second to the object, [and then they are entitled] knowledge.",["The second 'heißen sie' is elided in the German; supplied in brackets."]],"dohna-s14-005":["Modes of knowledge can become vivid:",["'Erkenntnisse' plural = 'modes of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s14-006":["1. when they are referred to the material, to sensibility (sensation) (subjective);",["OCR '{' normalised to a parenthesis. 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Empfindung' = sensation."]],"dohna-s14-007":["2. when they are referred to the formal, to intuition (objective).",[]],"dohna-s14-008":["[A representation which enlivens must present the object as present.",["The bracket opens here and closes in s14-010."]],"dohna-s14-009":["There is a difference between 1. making a mode of knowledge distinct (here nothing is added, one only raises it to distinctness; this is | analytic |,",["'ein Erkenntnis deutlich machen' rendered 'making a mode of knowledge distinct' to keep the contrast with the next item; the singular 'a knowledge' is not idiomatic."]],"dohna-s14-010":["2. making a distinct mode of knowledge — where previously there was none, this is | synthetic |.]",["The contrast is between *a cognition made distinct* and *a distinct cognition made*."]],"dohna-s14-011":["In logical distinctness the number of marks does not matter at all (S. P. 42 ...), but in aesthetic distinctness it is precisely their multiplicity that matters (for example the description of spring). The origin of distinctness is twofold, analytic and synthetic. The mathematician has the latter manner.",["'S. P. 42 . . .f)' is garbled in OCR (a reference, perhaps to a page of Meier's text, with an opening bracket lost); rendered as it can be made out. 'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness."]],"dohna-s14-012":["The author now speaks",["Breaks off; the next passage gives the topic."]],"dohna-s14-013":["Of the comprehensible and the incomprehensible",["'Begreiflich' = comprehensible."]],"dohna-s14-014":["Feelings can never produce a mode of knowledge. [The word representation must not be explained at all. For by this very act one would already have to presuppose the concept of it.]",["'ein Erkenntnis' rendered 'a mode of knowledge' for consistency."]],"dohna-s14-015":["One begins, first, with representing. The second step is then to perceive something — *percipere* — to represent something to oneself with consciousness; the third, to be acquainted with something <(*noscere*), that is, to know it in such a way that one is acquainted with it through comparison with others in respect of their identity and diversity. To understand something (*intelligere*) is to represent something to oneself through the understanding, through concepts [so that one can give an account of it and state its marks]. To know a thing from reason [that is, *a priori* through the understanding, even if it were not given], from universal principles, in accordance with its grounds, is entitled having insight (*einsehen*) [*einsehen*, *perspicere*]. Thus . . . to have insight *a priori* is to know not only that it is so (as, for example, the dissolving of salt by water), but that it must be so (for example, an eclipse of the sun (mathematically) [even if we had never seen one]). The last stage is comprehending, that is, having sufficient insight into something (for a certain purpose). To comprehend absolutely (in every respect), *comprehendere*, (is impossible for us) [not the moral rules]. One can comprehend perfectly, for example, various hydraulic phenomena of water, and have insight into this *a priori* [as soon as we assume fluidity as *hypothesis* — having insight requires throughout consciousness of the truth]. But as to what it is that is fluid, one can (not) have insight into that *a priori*. —",["'Einteiligere)' is garbled OCR; I read it as '(*intelligere*)', the Latin counterpart of 'verstehen' in the series *percipere*, *noscere*, *intelligere*, *perspicere*, *comprehendere*. Doubtful reading.","The text gives 'einsehen' and then 'einsehen perspicere'. I translate 'having insight', keep the Latin *perspicere*, and leave the German in the bracket.","'Erkennen' is rendered 'to know' and 'kennen' 'to be acquainted with', so that the stages stay distinct. 'Absicht' is 'purpose' in the first place and 'respect' in 'in aller Absicht'.","'(nicht)' in the last sentence stands in parentheses in the source, so it may be an editorial or scribal insertion. I keep it as '(not)'. Without it the sentence would say that one can have insight, which does not fit the sense.","The sentence 'Also . . .' contains an ellipsis in the source, which I keep. The angle bracket before '(*noscere*)' is carried over as it stands. Its closing partner is missing, so the apparatus is incomplete here.","Kemp Smith's practice of dividing long sentences was not needed, since the transcript is already in short clauses."]],"dohna-s14-016":["The example contributes to understanding only aesthetically. [Geist, Geischt (still in Switzerland), Gescht, vapour (Dunst).] We often believe we have insight into something that at bottom we can only explain to ourselves — understand as regards its possibility.",["The German words Geist, Geischt, Gescht and Dunst are left as they stand, since the point of the remark is their linguistic kinship; 'Dunst' is glossed 'vapour'. The spelling 'Geischt' is as in the OCR and may be uncertain."]],"dohna-s14-017":["One always understands best what one can make oneself.",[]],"dohna-s14-018":["The distinct concept becomes complete when all marks are contained in it [exhaustively distinct, so far as it contains all coordinate marks.] [Totality with regard to the subordinate marks the author calls completeness (*Vollständigkeit*).] One must not make one's concept superabundant, for one makes it hard for oneself if one takes in superfluous | marks |. The concept from which all dispensable marks are left out is entitled precise. — The concept that contains neither too much nor too little is entitled adequate [since one knows not only the marks themselves but also the marks of the marks; but the concept does not fit adequately until the series has been brought entirely to its end.]",["'Merkmale' is rendered 'marks' throughout, the usual English term for Kant's Latin *notae*.","'Complet' and 'Vollständigkeit' are both rendered by 'complete' and 'completeness', and the German is shown in the second case. 'Ausführlich deutlich' is rendered 'exhaustively distinct'. This is a choice.","'The author' is Meier, whose textbook Kant is following. The pipes | around 'marks' are the Academy's apparatus and are retained.","The last bracketed clause was divided at the semicolon to make clear that the adequate concept requires the completed series."]],"dohna-s14-019":["The first degree of subordination is a mark of a mark. The adequate concept rests upon coordination.",["The sense, as in the previous passage, is doubtful. One would expect the adequate concept to rest on subordination, the series of marks of marks. I translate the text as it stands and do not emend it."]],"dohna-s14-020":["[If the sum of the marks does not contain too much — precise; if it does not contain too little — adequate. — Distinctness of understanding is that through concepts. The distinctness of the power of judgment is that of examples through intuition. Distinctness of reason is that through mediate marks (just as distinctness of understanding is through immediate marks). All concepts that are not precise: indeterminate, vague.]",["'Urteilskraft' is rendered 'power of judgment'. Kant's wording is elliptical and I have not supplied the missing verbs.","'Deutlichkeit' is 'distinctness', following the same choice for 'deutlich' as 'distinct' in the previous passages."]],"dohna-s14-021":["A bright head, which always knows what it wants.",["'Heller Kopf' is rendered literally as 'bright head'. 'Clear-headed person' would be an idiomatic alternative."]],"dohna-s15-001":["Sixth Section. On the Certainty of Knowledge",["'Erkenntnis' is rendered 'knowledge', as the method requires."]],"dohna-s15-002":["Here we are speaking not of truth but of holding to be true (*Fürwahrhalten*). [Objectively all propositions are certainly true or certainly false.] This is judgment in its relation and reference to the subject. We want to know here not the grounds of truth but only those of holding to be true. The stages are: 1. opining, 2. believing, 3. knowing. These three concepts may be called the modalities of the *sensus veri et falsi*.",["'Fürwahrhalten' is rendered 'holding to be true', the standard English choice, with the German given at first occurrence. 'Meinen', 'glauben' and 'wissen' are 'opining', 'believing' and 'knowing'.","'Dies ist das Urteil' is read as referring to the holding to be true, so the English says 'this is judgment' in its relation to the subject."]],"dohna-s15-003":["Opining is a holding to be true that is, with consciousness, insufficient both subjectively and objectively. An insufficient holding to be true is one that does not suffice to exclude the opposite. He who merely opines is still open to counter-grounds.",["'Mit Bewußtsein' is read as governing 'unzureichend' ('insufficient with consciousness'), that is, consciously insufficient."]],"dohna-s15-004":["But when one believes, this is no longer so. [It is an assertoric holding to be true, which we nevertheless do not know with consciousness of necessity (so that we must still admit that modifications are possible).] Belief is a holding to be true that is indeed subjectively <sufficient,) yet with consciousness objectively insufficient. Thus believing is distinguished from knowing in that it is not capable of proof. Believing is a private holding to be true, sufficiently certain only for me. Hence I cannot yet say that I know it.",["'Wenn (man) aber glaubt': the parenthesis around 'man' is as in the source and is kept. 'Dies ist nicht mehr' is elliptical. I read it as 'it is no longer opining' and rendered it 'no longer so'.","The angle bracket and the mismatched closing parenthesis in '<sufficient,)' are OCR/apparatus remnants, retained as they stand.","'Abänderungen' is rendered 'modifications'. 'Beweisfähig' is rendered 'capable of proof'."]],"dohna-s15-005":["Knowing is a holding to be true sufficient both subjectively and objectively. [I hold the opposite to be impossible. A holding to be true that is, with consciousness, insufficient both objectively and subjectively is opining. The concept of belief is best grasped in a practical respect (although this influence on the will does not belong in logic).] It can be empirical, through experience, and apodictic, *a priori*. The holding to be true of an apodictic proposition can be apodictically certain.",["'Hinreichend' and 'zureichend' are both rendered 'sufficient'."]],"dohna-s15-006":["What I opine, the judgment I utter only problematically; hence holding to be true is 1. problematic, namely when it is so accepted that it is still to be proved. It is called problematic because it serves only to find, among several grounds, the sufficient one.",["A stray 'f' after 'erweisen' (an OCR mark, probably a footnote or apparatus sign) has been dropped.","'Heißt' is rendered 'is called' rather than 'is entitled', which would be stiff here. Elsewhere 'heißt' and 'genannt werden' follow the method and are rendered 'is entitled'."]],"dohna-s15-007":["Now holding to be true is 2. assertoric in believing. For I say that it is sufficient for me (subjectively), but whether it is also objectively sufficient I do not determine. Very many representations of a future state are mere products of the imagination, and many ideas that please us greatly (for example, of male and female light) are only ideas in the subject.",["I take 'ausmachen' in the sense of 'settle' and render it 'determine'.","'Einbildungskraft' is rendered 'imagination'. The sentence has no connective between its two halves in the German, and 'and' is supplied. This is a smoothing of the transcript's note-like syntax."]],"dohna-s15-008":["Holding to be true is 3. apodictic in knowing, [*intuitio* in mathematics. Conviction. Persuasion is the semblance of conviction, uncertain whether subjective or objective.]",["'Überzeugung' is rendered 'conviction' and 'Persuasion' is left as 'persuasion'. The Latin *intuitio* is kept in italics."]],"dohna-s15-009":["The propositions of holding to be true can be empirical, and the holding to be true apodictic. Holding to be true is connected either with the consciousness of subjective necessity or with that of the contingent. In the latter case, if it is subjectively and objectively insufficient, it is opining; but if it is subjectively sufficient and objectively insufficient — believing. Hypotheses are opinions. In mathematics there is neither opining nor believing, but only knowing — or nothing — and in merely speculative metaphysics it is the same.",["'Die Sätze des Fürwahrhaltens' is awkward. I translate it literally and do not emend it, though 'the propositions held to be true' may be meant.","'Des Zufälligen' is rendered 'the contingent', which is a judgment of what the contrast with 'subjektive Notwendigkeit' requires."]],"dohna-s15-010":["[Something is a matter of opining if it can never become an object of knowing, neither objectively nor subjectively (for example, whether there are inhabitants of the moon). — Concerning the possibility of the generation of their like by animals we shall never have more than opinions. — Matters of belief, where there is for us only a subjectively sufficient ground of holding to be true. — Everything that we assume from purposes is for us only subjectively sufficient — (that is, I must assume that this is so, otherwise I do not get on with my reason here — this I admittedly cannot assume as valid objectively, yet it is sufficient for me.)",["The square bracket opened at the start is not closed here and presumably closes in the next passage. I leave it open. Stray 'f' marks after 'Wo' and 'ist' (OCR) have been dropped.","The last parenthesis is closed in the German although the dash before it opens no matching mark. I follow the text as it stands.","'Zwecke' is rendered 'purposes', and 'Sache des Meinens' 'a matter of opining'."]],"dohna-s15-011":["Where knowing is possible, this is already a matter of knowing, for example geography. — Only objects of rational belief (*Vernunftglauben*) can be called matters of belief.]",["A stray 'f' after 'Wo' (OCR) has been dropped. The closing square bracket belongs to the bracket opened in the preceding passage."]],"dohna-s15-012":["What are objects of opining, believing and knowing? Are objectively historical propositions objects of opining, of believing, or even of knowing? They are objects of knowing. For there are cases where we can use the experience of others as our own. Are mathematical propositions objects of opining, and so on? All of them, because they are *a priori*, are objects of knowing. (Concepts are entitled transcendental when we have them *a priori*.) Can one not also accept propositions *a priori* on belief? Yes. Mathematics too is of this kind. [One believes mathematicians because it is not possible that they can err, since they would at once come upon false consequences. In philosophy, however, there is no belief.",["The square bracket is not closed in this passage and presumably closes at the end of the next. I leave it open.","'Objektiv historische Sätze' is translated literally, as it stands. The parenthesis on 'transcendental' is as in the source, and the definition looks like the student's own gloss.","'Auf Glauben annehmen' is rendered 'accept on belief'. It could also be taken as 'on trust'."]],"dohna-s15-013":["A believer (*gläubig*) is one who is capable of a rational belief. The morally practical rests solely on reason. What, then, either is itself duty or stands in connate connection with it — this is the object of belief (of practical holding to be true) — he who does not [believe] this, etc., is unbelieving; he who believes everything else is credulous.]",["'Wer dies nicht usw.' is left incomplete in the source. The word 'believe' in square brackets is my supply and shows what 'usw.' seems to stand for.","'Connater' is OCR for 'connate' (*connatus*), 'innate, intimately joined'. I render it 'connate'. 'Leichtgläubig' is 'credulous'. The closing square bracket belongs to the bracket opened in the preceding passage."]],"dohna-s15-014":["A matter of opinion (*Meinen*) is that for the opposite of which one has no grounds. There are objects of opinion that can become objects of belief and of knowledge [*Wissen*]; for example, in physics, what Newton opines about the origin of the planets, and so on. There is a belief which, in a practical (not in a speculative) relation, is esteemed equal to knowledge; for example, certain propositions of the ancient philosophers: probabilism. The certainty that belongs to the cognition of the command or prohibition of an action is moral certainty. What is certain according to the rules of prudence cannot be entitled morally certain, but only pragmatically certain. The concept of moral certainty is misused when one often has nothing more [for example, in wagers] than a practically sufficient ground of truth; it must not be theoretically sufficient. [Logical certainty (apodictic) is 1. philosophical, 2. mathematical.] Moral certainty relates to conscience. So, for example, with the doctrines of Catholicism, which some accept without consulting conscience. If one does something at the risk of erring, one is never morally certain. With the word 'belief' one always thinks of a voluntary assent; with the word 'opinion', of a decided assent. A matter of belief is what is to be held true and of which there is no knowledge. It is regarded as a holding-true that depends on my own pleasure. Rational belief is entitled, simply, *fides*. It is a complicated concept. [For 'believing' (*gläubig*) we properly have no Latin word: *fidelis* means 'faithful'.] There are certain transcendent judgments, which lie beyond the limits of our knowledge and which one can only believe. Whoever is not convinced even by moral grounds is an unbeliever (*Ungläubiger*). An unbeliever in the theological sense is one who does not see, and will not accept, the supersensible, even if it is morally really necessary, because it cannot be proved theoretically by reason (it is uncertain). One who refuses to accept even what is historically certain, merely because the certainty is not apodictic, is entitled *ungläubisch* [incredulous]. Certainty is the objective sufficiency of holding-true. In belief it is subjective. There I can only say: I am certain. But in the case of certainty one says: it is certain, and this is universally valid.",["'Wissen' in square brackets added to mark the German; 'Kenntnis des Gebots' rendered 'cognition of the command' (Kenntnis, not Erkenntnis).","Parentheses in '(gehört)' and '(nennen)' are editorial supplements in the OCR; I have translated them without brackets as the verbs they complete.","Meinen/Glauben/Wissen: the sentence on 'Beifall' reads oddly (opinion as 'decided' assent); transcribed as it stands, possibly a slip in the student's notes.","'Mein Beheben' is read as 'Belieben' (my pleasure), an OCR slip. 'hegen' read as 'liegen'.","'Ungläubiger' and 'ungläubisch' distinguished by the gloss 'incredulous'; the sense of ungläubisch is uncertain.","Several long sentences kept close to the text; the abrupt style is the transcript's."]],"dohna-s15-015":["A rational mode of knowledge [*ein vernünftiges Erkenntnis*] is different from knowledge from reason [*Vernunfterkenntnis*]; the former must be the property of all modes of knowledge. In the distinction of mathematical certainty, what matters is not the degree but the quantity.",["Sense of the first sentence is obscure; translated literally. 'Quantität' in the last sentence may be a slip for 'Qualität' or 'Art', but I keep the text."]],"dohna-s15-016":["All certainty is either",["Breaks off; continued in the next passages."]],"dohna-s15-017":["1. empirical, that which arises from experience, | or |",["The signs | are carried over as in the text."]],"dohna-s15-018":["2. rational, that whose origin is *a priori*. It is again considered in a twofold respect:",[]],"dohna-s15-019":["objectively, a) logical, with regard to the theoretical faculty of knowledge;",[]],"dohna-s15-020":["subjectively, b) practical, with regard to moral use.",[]],"dohna-s15-021":["Rational logical certainty is entitled, *generaliter*, apodictic, for it is known from grounds *a priori*. [We have conviction of a thing when we recognize it as true with the consciousness that our judgment is objective.] One cannot say that mathematical certainty is greater than philosophical. The difference lies only in this, that mathematical certainty is intuitive, whereas philosophical certainty is only discursive. Philosophical certainty can indeed attain the same degree, but never the property of becoming intuitive. One is accustomed to say of philosophical propositions that they are as certain as 2 times 2 is 4; but this is not accurate, for the latter is mathematical, not philosophical, certainty. [The degree of the two certainties is equal, but they are of different kinds.]",["'hegt' read as 'liegt'. '(nie)' and '<würde)' reproduce editorial marks; the OCR brackets are normalised to the sense.","'2 mal 2 4' read as '2 times 2 is 4'."]],"dohna-s15-022":["[Hour, Monday]",["Editorial note marking the lecture hour."]],"dohna-s15-023":["To assume (*annehmen*) is a contingent assent which has sufficient ground with regard to a certain purpose. 'Settled' (*ausgemacht*) presupposes a dispute, since the matter was uncertain. Of mathematical propositions one cannot say that they are settled; for who can dispute about them?",[]],"dohna-s15-024":["[One sometimes says that something is indisputable. But this means only as much as undisputed: no one has ever been found who has refuted it.]",["'hatte' (for hätte) rendered as a subjunctive."]],"dohna-s15-025":["No mode of knowledge other than the mathematical can be evidently demonstrated. For nothing else can bring about an intuition *a priori* (*intuitum a priori*).",["'Augenscheinlich' ... 'dargelegt' rendered 'evidently demonstrated'; a choice. 'intuitum' is Latin in the text (strictly the form is odd) and kept."]],"dohna-s15-026":["[A mode of knowledge is entitled thorough (*gründlich*) in so far as it is secured against doubt by logically sufficient grounds.] A thorough mode of knowledge must be derived from secure principles. Thorough history can be acquired only through chronology.",[]],"dohna-s15-027":["*Bündig* (conclusive) means as much as consistent, that everything stands in *nexu*, in connection. The conclusive is the highest in the use of our knowledge: to be consistent. [Consistent means following rightly, when the connection of the consequence with the ground accords with the logical laws. Conclusive: this proposition is connected with the preceding ones in the series of a system. The more consistent a man is in dangerous maxims, the more harmful he is.]",["'Bündig' glossed in English as 'conclusive'; a choice, since no single English word covers it."]],"dohna-s15-028":["When the mathematician demonstrates, he compels everyone who understands him to assent. Strictly, then, we cannot say here that we give him our assent. [The expression 'to give assent' does not seem to lie within our will. Can we withhold our assent from a mathematical proof? Holding-true belongs to the understanding, but investigation to choice (*Willkür*).]",[]],"dohna-s15-029":["In the *suspensio judicii* there is something of freedom. One can also give one's assent without deciding with the judgment, still waiting for further grounds, where one still assumes the opposite as possible. I am free when, with a practical purpose, I accept a proposition on account of the good that can follow from it. One can therefore say: there are things in matters of religion, for example our eternal continuance, which we accept precisely from this good, since this life is still far from adequate to the idea of the highest good.",["OCR 'judicn' read as 'judicii'; 'Avartet' read as 'wartet'."]],"dohna-s15-030":["The *suspensio judicii* consists in this, that when on both sides all grounds are equally insufficient, none is preponderant. It can be 1. *ob indifferentiam*, 2. *ob aequilibrium*. To keep oneself voluntarily in *suspensione judicii* [it is the mean between holding true and rejecting; only a problematic judgment remains] bespeaks a very great head, and is extremely difficult because inclination at once mingles in the judgment of the understanding. To be and remain undecided *ob aequilibrium* bespeaks weak heads.",["'ob indifferentiam' is the OCR reading; 'aequihbrium' corrected to 'aequilibrium'."]],"dohna-s15-031":["*Argumentum ad crumenam*, because he who has no full purse cannot join in the wager. [Whoever will not join in the wager is taken to be uncertain of his case.]",["'kernen' read as 'keinen'. 'davor angenommen' read as 'dafür angenommen'."]],"dohna-s15-032":["*Argumentum ad verecundiam*, because a great man has asserted it, and one does not wish to claim to know better. [A bad argument.]",[]],"dohna-s15-033":["The best argument is probably always the one *ad tutum*, namely in the case where one accepts what is safest. It is cunning, but does not always aim at the true.",[]],"dohna-s15-034":["As regards the *suspensio judicii*, it is:",[]],"dohna-s15-035":["1. *critica* or *indagatoria*, a postponement of judgment until closer investigation [until a ground for holding true presents itself];",[]],"dohna-s15-036":["2. *sceptica*, which is the renunciation of an assertoric judgment. [A complete renunciation of all certainty. The principle of always postponing one's judgment is called ἐποχή (*epoché*). *Status indifferentiae*.]",["The OCR 'ettoxt)' is garbled; I read it as the Greek ἐποχή, which is a conjecture."]],"dohna-s15-037":["We now come to the doctrine",["Continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-038":["Of Prejudices",["Heading."]],"dohna-s15-039":["A preliminary judgment [*Judicium praevium*] has this only in common with prejudice, that it too is passed before the matter has been investigated. [*Praejudicium* is not different from [?]; the two are one and the same. The latter is properly an appeal to a judgment, hence {through} experience; this, however, contributes to culture. A *judicium praevium* is a judgment that precedes reflection [Überlegung]. Without reflection one cannot judge at all, though one can well do so without investigation. What is immediately certain also permits none. —] A *judicium reflectens* is one in which a judgment is at once posed as a problem, in order to investigate the truth. For seeking, too, one must have a special principle, and to seek this out belongs to the power of judgment [Urteilskraft]. Judgment cannot be taught. For if one wished to give rules to the person, he would already be using the power of judgment in order to subsume under those rules. A *judicium praevium* therefore precedes the investigation; but with reflection it must always go hand in hand.",["Text after 'von' in the bracketed sentence is garbled in the OCR (a stray 'f'); I mark the gap with [?] and do not fill it.","Überlegung rendered 'reflection' to match *judicium reflectens*. The shift from 'man' to 'ihm' (him) in the sentence on rules is kept as 'the person'.","'(durch)' in the source is shown as {through}, following the braces of the edition's apparatus as read here."]],"dohna-s15-040":["[Preliminary judgments arise from grounds of which one is consciously aware that they are insufficient. — Skill in preliminary judgments is a kind of prudence. This chapter has so far been neglected in logic. Every inventor must judge provisionally.]",["'vorläufig' is rendered 'preliminary' in the noun phrase and 'provisionally' for the adverb."]],"dohna-s15-041":["I reflect upon [überlege] something when I compare it with the laws of the understanding. There are propositions which, prior to all investigation, are already *iudicia determinantia* (determining judgments), e.g. between two points only one straight line is possible. These are also entitled *judicia indemonstrabilia*, that is, judgments without proof, immediately certain, not unprovable. We shall speak of them further on.",["OCR 'mdemonstrabilia' corrected to *indemonstrabilia*.","I restated the sentence in two. The gloss 'beweislose … nicht unbeweisbare' is kept as it stands."]],"dohna-s15-042":["[The propensity to make objectively universal principles out of subjectively universal causes of judging is prejudice. —]",[]],"dohna-s15-043":["*Judicia reflectentia* are those which introduce the investigation, and which show: 1. whether a thing requires investigation, 2. how I ought to investigate it. Prejudice is a maxim of judging objectively from subjective grounds. A prejudice is never a single proposition, e.g. 'The apple does not fall far from the tree'. This is not yet a prejudice, but only a consequence from another. For no prejudice is a proposition; it is only a maxim for drawing propositions from it. A subjective fundamental proposition [Grundsatz] is entitled a maxim; an objective one is entitled a principle. A rule which the subject makes a principle for itself is entitled a maxim. Thus many men make a rule which is objectively false into a maxim subjectively. The maxim is then grounded on an illusion [Schein], which we have already defined above in this way. Prejudice, therefore, is a maxim of holding true rules which are grounded on errors. The formulas are either 1. *Denksprüche* [sayings for thought], | or | 2. *Sinnsprüche*, sentences [Sentenzen], | or | 3. *Lehrsprüche* [doctrinal sayings], or | 4. proverbs [Sprichwörter], *proverbia*. [A saying [Spruch] is a proposition bound to a customary expression, so that this makes it easier to remember. — *Lehrsprüche* or *canones*. — Sentences are aesthetic formulas. — *Canones* are classical. — *Dicta* are sacred formulas.] The national spirit of a people is best recognised from its proverbs. —",["Restated the run of short sentences on maxim and principle without change of sense; 'heißt' is rendered 'is entitled'.","OCR 'Sehern' read as 'Fehlern' (errors); 'ans' read as 'aus'; the stray '1' before 'Sprichwörter' is dropped as an OCR slip, and the numbering 1–4 is kept.","The German names of the four kinds of formula are left in brackets because their English renderings are only approximate."]],"dohna-s15-044":["The prejudice we named first, 'Like father, like son', or the like, is properly grounded on this: that one has assumed as universally valid the maxim that there is much similarity between men and animals. And this prejudice is grounded on the subjective ground that what all the world says is true.",["The transcript's link between 'Vater/Sohn' and 'Menschen und Tieren' is odd, but I translate it as it stands. The 'first' prejudice named refers to material not in this passage."]],"dohna-s15-045":["Sentences might be called aesthetic formulas, just as proverbs are *formulas vulgares*. Sentences are usually formulas for wisdom; *canones* are formulas for science. Classical formulas are called, in the juridical sense, *Brocardica*. [They have authority and declare all investigation superfluous. Proverbs are called *loci communes*, because everyone speaks so, and so this is supposed to be true. Proverbs are the concentrated (common) wisdom of the people. Hence those who always want to speak in proverbs are vulgar.] *Dicta* are sayings κατ' ἐξοχήν [par excellence].",["OCR 'koct5 e£oxr|v' read as κατ' ἐξοχήν; 'geAvöhnlich' read as 'gewöhnlich'. The gloss 'par excellence' is my supplied word, shown in brackets."]],"dohna-s15-046":["In all merely subjective grounds we are always passive, e.g. in inclination, in habit, in the propensity to imitation. The propensity to prejudice is a propensity to mechanism in the use of reason. There are sentences which already announce themselves as prejudices.",[]],"dohna-s15-047":["Prejudices may in general be divided",["The sentence runs on into the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-048":["1. into prejudice *praejudicium hominis*;",[]],"dohna-s15-049":["2. into that [prejudice] of the occasion of our (judgments), a) *loci* or b) *temporis*.",["'prejudice' is supplied in brackets to complete 'das'."]],"dohna-s15-050":["The *praejudicium hominis* is again twofold:",[]],"dohna-s15-051":["1. *praejudicium vel personae* (in favour of a person);",[]],"dohna-s15-052":["2. *praejudicium multitudinis* (in favour of the multitude). It is peculiar to womankind [Frauenzimmer], and also suits them quite well. [What all the world says is true.] One who has the *praejudicium multitudinis* distrusts his own reason. [The prejudice of authority [Ansehen], e.g. of antiquity, which has its ground, for instance, in gratitude.] | [Prejudices of authority, e.g. in favour of great men, often do great harm to the sciences, in that by their greatness they deter others from emulation. Everyone who might wish to choose this field for cultivation despairs of reaching this ideal, or ever of surpassing it. — We sometimes admire out of contempt, in that, given the slight conception we had of someone, what he has done always seemed a great deal. So Mercier admires the Germans, and so one is always admiring women who produce something scholarly.",["OCR 'Avar' read as 'war'; 'Frauen-7.immer' read as 'Frauenzimmer'. The stray 'j' before the bracket is read as the sign |. The final bracket is left unclosed, as in the source.","'Frauenzimmer' is rendered 'womankind', in keeping with the period's idiom; the sentence is the lecturer's own.","The last sentence, 'bewundert man Frauen-…immer', is rendered by judgment as 'women who produce something scholarly' [Frauenzimmer]."]],"dohna-s15-053":["There is a difference between *bewundern* and *sich verwundern* [admiring and being astonished]. We admire the ancients not on account of the degree of their knowledge, but because, relatively to the few aids they had, they got so far. — Learning [Gelehrsamkeit]. Acquaintance with the ancients. Of the esteem which it deserves we always let much fall upon the ancients themselves. — The prejudice for the ancients has many causes. One does not know the new, and, out of laziness, will not know it. In matters of fine art the ancients are really still models; in other subjects much progress has been made since. In the aesthetic, in matters of sensation [Empfindung], the ancients could get further than we, on account of their civil condition. Speeches to a cultivated (refined) people, with regard to the constitution of the state, had to attain a high degree of perfection <— energy —>. — Here is a foundation. The opinion, therefore, does not rest on mere prejudice. If these works were lost, we should not get them back, because we cannot come into that condition. Only a dead language can become a model of presentation; living ones too often undergo changes.]",["The distinction 'Bewundern/Verwundern' is left in German since English cannot reproduce it. The closing '!' after the bracket is dropped as an OCR slip.","'Empfindung' is kept as 'sensation' per the fixed term, though 'feeling' might be more natural in context.","I divided the long run of sentences; the brackets [ ] and < > are carried over where they stand, and the opening bracket is closed at the end."]],"dohna-s15-054":["The *praejudicium multitudinis* too is twofold:",[]],"dohna-s15-055":["1. *praejudicium eruditorum* [the prejudice in favour of the learned]; thus the common man trusts the learned.",[]],"dohna-s15-056":["2. *praejudicium sensus communis*. Common understanding [gemeiner Verstand] is a kind of sense (*praejudicium empirismi*) which in a manner has an intuition. [Often the learned too have a special regard for it.] In medicine, however, the contrary holds, because they believe that it is dangerous to resort to learning at haphazard [auf gut Glück]. [They believe, namely, that learned physicians do not devise anything at all out of experience.]",["Stray 'f' marks in the OCR (after 'bedienen' and 'aus') are dropped as apparatus or OCR noise.","The sense of 'sich auf gut Glück der Gelehrsamkeit zu bedienen' is a little uncertain; 'they' is taken to be those who hold the opinion in medicine."]],"dohna-s15-057":["The author now deals",["Continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-058":["With logical egoism",["'Egoisterei' is rendered 'egoism', as in the heading's own next passage."]],"dohna-s15-059":["[Logical egoism is a selfish prejudice.] This is not mere self-conceit, but a kind of logical principle, which holds dispensable the criterion of truth, namely comparing one's opinions with those of other men. [The mathematician can never risk this. — *Vitium subreptionis*, from subjective grounds. —] The principle of indifference, etc., towards the judgments of others, as compared with my own, is the principle of logical egoism. It is unfair to condemn people for [the demand] that they should keep all their judgments to themselves. For they must communicate them, if they are not to lose the strong criterion of truth, which consists in comparing their judgments with the judgments of others.",["The sentence 'Es ist unbillig … behalten sollen' is awkward and may be garbled; I translate it literally and supply '[the demand]'.","OCR 'üire' read as 'ihre'; I restated the last sentence for clarity, with 'which consists in' added."]],"dohna-s15-060":["The author further speaks of prejudice in favour of another person. This is exactly the opposite of the one previously mentioned. For we sometimes exalt a great man in order to conceal our own weakness from our contemporaries; it is a means of comparison and of removing the difference. An idol of this kind, raised up to admiration, such as Aristotle, has done extraordinary harm to mankind and has kept it constantly in bonds. For no one believed that this man could ever be surpassed, and so people did nothing but imitate. Thus the greatness of one man did harm to posterity [see also p. 57], which had not the courage to surpass him.",["OCR 'ander n' silently read as 'anderen'.","'Menschheit' rendered 'mankind'. The bracketed '[vid. auch p. 57]' is carried over as the editors' insertion, with 'vid.' given as 'see'."]],"dohna-s15-061":["We now come specifically to the prejudice of antiquity, *praejudicium antiquitatis*. We have the prejudice that when the ancients lived, nature had not yet fallen into such decline. There are also other causes. High esteem for antiquity is found chiefly among those who have studied the ancients, [and] without such study one can make no claim whatever to the name of a scholar; among others it arises from mere imitation. [A provisional judgment accepted as decisive is entitled a prejudice.] We also admire the ancients because they had already got so far in the childhood of the human race. It is, further, in accordance with gratitude that we praise them for their great merits and forget and overlook their weaknesses. For we ourselves may one day become ancients, so to speak, if we leave behind such products as they did, and so on.",["The editors' <ohne> is rendered in the sentence as 'without'; the angle brackets are dropped because the clause was restated, and 'and' is supplied in square brackets to join the two clauses.","A stray 'f' after 'mit' in the OCR is dropped as an apparatus mark; the sense 'Alte sozusagen mit f werden' is taken as 'Alte sozusagen werden'.","The sentence 'Wir können ja selbst...' was smoothed; 'ja' is rendered 'For'."]],"dohna-s15-062":["The prejudice of novelty arises from inclination. For the first person to communicate something of this kind finds in it a merit. The true provisional judgment would be this: that writings which have maintained themselves through so long a time merely on account of their inner worth and excellence must nevertheless possess something that is lasting and enduring.",["OCR 'etAvas' read as 'etwas'.","'Neuigkeit' rendered 'novelty'."]],"dohna-s15-063":["[When we have attained certain modes of knowledge with effort, and these, in connection with others, in a certain combination into a whole constitute a system, we have a predilection for them. This flows from the nature of man; we infer to truth from coherence. An assumed ground may well serve to explain many consequences without being the true ground.]",["The stray 'f' after 'wir' is dropped as an OCR/apparatus mark.","'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge' per the method."]],"dohna-s15-064":["The prejudice of the accepted proposition, *praejudicium systematis*. Much is commonly written against this. A system permits a provisional favourable judgment to its advantage. The fitness of a proposition for the whole system proves the truth <of it>.",["The editors' angle brackets around 'desselben' are retained. 'Lehrsatz' is rendered 'proposition' in the second sentence, 'Satz' also 'proposition'; the heading is as in the German."]],"dohna-s15-065":["There are many volunteers [*volontairs*] in the sciences who (so to speak) will not stand under any command of the school. We ought not to declaim too much against systems. Some French writers have done so. Next there is a prejudice of <lazy> confidence, of facility.",["'volontairs' kept as in the source with the English beside it.","Angle brackets around 'faulen' kept as the editors' insertion; 'Leichtigkeit' rendered 'facility'."]],"dohna-s15-066":["The universal validity of a rule can be judged only from rules *in abstracto*. For example, one wants to have a principle concerning the concept of the lie. About this one must judge *in abstracto*. For if one looked to single cases, some lies might be useful, e.g. the proposal of Themistocles [and] Aristides. But every breach of faith is disgraceful.",["OCR 'in6i abstracto' read as 'in abstracto'; 'Avollte' as 'wollte'.","'et' supplied for the dash between Themistocles and Aristides as '[and]'; the allusion (Plutarch's story of Themistocles' proposal to burn the Greek fleet, judged by Aristides) is left unexplained in the text."]],"dohna-s15-067":["The prejudice of shallowness. It consists in one's not going into the depths of knowledge, namely, to subordinate marks [*Merkmale*]. [For a good provisional judgment genius is absolutely required.]",["'Erkenntnis' here rendered 'knowledge'; 'Merkmale' rendered 'marks', the German given because the term is technical."]],"dohna-s15-068":["Thereupon the author speaks of the opposite prejudices, namely, when one has distrust of certain things. [Thus a prejudice against the old can be conceived, and equally one against the new. Distrust of all human knowledge is entitled misology.] It may also relate to oneself. It is then destructive to a man if it frightens his courage away from all and every kind of work; but if it is merely that in every undertaking we distrust our own powers, then that is the most excellent situation of the human mind.",["A stray 'f' after 'zurückschreckt ;' and 'so ist' is dropped as an OCR/apparatus mark.","'Gemüt' rendered 'mind'; 'Kräften' rendered 'powers'; 'Misologie' kept as in the source."]],"dohna-s15-069":["We now come to the doctrine",["OCR 'Avir' read as 'wir'. The passage breaks off, as a heading follows in the next passage."]],"dohna-s15-070":["Of the Probable",["'Wahrscheinliche' rendered 'the Probable'."]],"dohna-s15-071":["[A chapter in which logic can never get far.] When I say that something is probable (even if it should be only subjectively so), this relates to quality. But if I ask how great the probability is, I consider it according to degree. It is the relation of the grounds for the truth to the grounds of the contrary (*verisimilitudo*). The relation, however, of the grounds to sufficient truth is probability [*Probabilität*]. If I have only one ground for holding true and none to prove the contrary, this is already *verisimilitudo*. [The *verisimile* is the apparent, the *probabile* the acceptable. The judgment about *verisimilitudo* rests on a comparison of the grounds for the truth with the grounds of the contrary. In the case of probability the grounds for holding true amount to more than half; they preponderate.] The sufficient ground is the sum of all the cases that can possibly occur. Only in mathematics can the grounds for holding true be specified determinately.",["OCR 'Avorin' read as 'worin'; 'Wahrscheinlich -keit' rejoined.","'Wahrscheinlichkeit' is 'probability' in the general sense; 'Probabilität' is kept distinct, with the German given. 'Fürwahrhalten' rendered 'holding true'.","The sentence 'Das Verhältnis aber der Gründe für die zureichende Wahrheit' is doubtful in the transcript (perhaps 'zur zureichenden'), and is translated as it stands.","'Urteile' in 'Vergleichung der Urteile für die Wahrheit' looks like a slip for 'Gründe'; translated as 'grounds' on the argument's requirement. Flag: the German reads 'Urteile'."]],"dohna-s16-001":["Hour, Monday < f >",["'Stunde' is the lecture heading; the stray '< f >' is an OCR/apparatus residue and is left as printed, being too garbled to interpret."]],"dohna-s16-002":["[All probability we find by *numerando*, through counting up, or by *ponderando*. For the first way homogeneity is required, e.g. the throw of dice, where three sixes are just as easy as three eights. I am to throw 8 with two dice. Here 5 cases are possible. The probability can be counted off, and is = 5/36 of certainty. But when something is to be guessed, e.g. a testimony, the grounds are to be weighed, e.g. a man of good upbringing, and so on. Where the grounds are numbered I can say: it is probable; where they are weighed, only: it is probable to me. Likewise one can never say: this is morally certain, but only: I am morally certain, i.e. with me it rests on practical grounds; otherwise one must not use the word 'moral' at all; otherwise it is better to say: certainty from physical grounds, theoretical.]",["OCR 'g56' read as '5/36'; this is the correct value for 8 with two dice and is a silent correction, though the printed figure is garbled. 'Wurf der Würfel... 3 Achten' is carried as printed (a die has no eight), probably a slip in the transcript; flag.","The editors' '<ich bin)' is given as 'I am' with the mismatched bracket normalised.","'ponderando' and 'numerando' kept in Latin and italicised."]],"dohna-s16-003":["A proposition can be entitled probable only in so far as it lies on the way to certainty and, when augmented by homogeneous grounds, produces certainty. [So too with] judgments that go to the supersensible (otherwise also to the transcendent [*Überschwengliche*], in contrast with the immanent, which is grasped with the senses and remains in the representation). Hence in metaphysics, and in general in all things *a priori*, there is no probability in the theoretical sphere. Here we can only know, or it is nothing. For all our conjectures cannot on this path be brought to certainty, even when several different grounds are present. For this indicates nothing further than that there are many insufficient grounds, which can never furnish a universally valid proof; and it is always a proof of uncertainty when, instead of naming one sufficient ground, one offers several which are not so. Probability is an approximation to certainty, and several grounds, if they hold, ought to be combined together into one proof. In the like cases, where we are on the way to certainty, there is lacking only a *complementum*.",["The first sentence's parenthesis was a loose construction; '[So too with]' is supplied to join it, and the OCR 'hegt' is read as 'liegt'.","'Vorstellung' rendered 'representation'; 'Überschwengliche' given as 'transcendent' per the German 'Transcendente' beside it.","The sentence beginning 'Es fehlt in den gleichen Fällen' is obscure in the transcript; rendered as it stands. 'Vermutungen' rendered 'conjectures'."]],"dohna-s16-004":["[ad § 176 p. 55] The author still speaks of doubts. We obtain a doubt (*dubitatio*) subjectively when our holding-to-be-true is not determined (stands *in aequilibrio*). In the *suspensio judicii* the grounds must still leave a doubt. Calling in question (*dubium*), as an objective ground of holding-to-be-true, is the judgment which I pass upon truth, or a relation of the judgment to the subject, so far as the subject holds something to be true. — Calling in question is an objective ground for holding the opposite to be true, however small that ground may be. [The state of holding-to-be-true with consciousness of its insufficiency is entitled the state of doubt.] An objection is a *dubium* of which I am conscious, that is, so far as it is stated in concepts of the understanding; so far as it is represented obscurely, it is a scruple. [From this scruple a determinate doubt may arise.] In some cases a scruple can be raised to the clearness of an objection. There are frequent objections against judgments of the understanding which lack distinctness [they have been answered, but are not yet really answered]; hence the treatment of a scruple requires that it be developed. One must make plain what it is that the other person stumbles over. [When this becomes clear, it contributes greatly to the perfecting of holding-to-be-true.] Often the knot is cut instead of being untied. For example, in the dispute over the assertion that the world is the best, the opponents allege that there is so much evil in it, especially for man, that the world could not possibly be called good; and people are accustomed, without exposing the falsity of this, to <show> that the world, [as the] work of an all-wise being, cannot be bad. [Leibniz answers the objection thus: there is, once for all, an author whom I cannot think of otherwise than as the best and wisest being.] *Difficultatem differre, non auferre* — [that is so] when difficulties, even though removed by grounds of knowledge, are subject to like difficulties. [E.g. the Fall through the devil. (Dice — 36 F.)] Another expression: *obnubilare, non diluere* — to obscure the truth still more.",["Angle brackets and the words in them follow the source: \"<(zu) zeigen\" is rendered \"<show>\"; the sentence is left unclosed as in the German, which is irregular. \"[as the]\" is supplied for \"(das Werk)\".","Restated: the long sentence on the knot and the world's evil is kept in one piece, but the dependent clauses are smoothed; \"man pflegt\" rendered \"people are accustomed\".","\"[that is so]\" is supplied to carry the elliptical *Difficultatem differre, non auferre, wenn …*; the sense is doubtful and the transcript may be abbreviated.","\"Bezweifelung (dubium)\" rendered \"calling in question\"; \"Einwurf\" = objection; \"Skrupel\" = scruple; \"Erkenntnisgründe\" = grounds of knowledge.","\"Würfel — 36 F.\" is obscure (perhaps a dice example, with a page or figure reference); rendered literally as \"Dice\" and left unexplained.","\"Fürwahrhalten\" rendered \"holding-to-be-true\", a choice; \"Zulänglichkeit\" elsewhere = sufficiency."]],"dohna-s16-005":["[Lecture hour, Tuesday]",["A heading or marginal note; rendered as it stands."]],"dohna-s16-006":["We now come to the state of doubt — a state of the mind, so far as it is conscious that it is undetermined with regard to holding-to-be-true. Dogmatism and scepticism are opposed to one another. [The sceptical method is that of postponing one's assent, while remaining at once open to the counter-grounds.] Universal scepticism is the prejudice of assuming all knowledge whatever to be uncertain. Dogmatism is the prejudice of assuming the [complete] certainty of knowledge without holding the critique of our faculty of reason to be necessary [ — detrimental to reason]. [Dogmatic scepticism, which declares all certainty impossible.]",["\"Kritik\" rendered \"critique\"; \"Vernunftvermögen\" = faculty of reason.","Round brackets around \"(völlige)\" in the source are rendered as square brackets for consistency; the dash clause in brackets is kept as in the transcript.","\"da man gleich offen für die Gegengründe ist\" rendered \"while remaining at once open to\" – a smoothing of an irregular clause."]],"dohna-s16-007":["[There is no such thing as *logice dijudicata* — here there are no fully established truths. We must at least always answer the possible doubts against them.",["The opening bracket is left unclosed, as in the source (the passage continues in the next).","*Logice dijudicata*: kept in Latin; the sense is roughly \"decided in a logical manner\"; the transcript is terse and the sense doubtful."]],"dohna-s16-008":["Criticism is the principle of not holding judgments *a priori* to be true until we have compared them with the laws of our faculty of reason. Only in modes of knowledge *a priori* through concepts is this method applicable and necessary.]",["\"Kritizism\" rendered \"criticism\"; \"Erkenntnissen\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\".","The closing bracket matches the opening bracket in the preceding passage."]],"dohna-s16-009":["Mathematics and natural science do not need a critique of our reason. There we have no need to investigate where the limits of these modes of knowledge lie, because everything can be established through intuition. — The prejudice of dogmatism rests on the success of many modes of knowledge *a priori* without critique, through reason. Here reason succeeds admirably, and so people believe that it will succeed in other cases also. One cannot blame Plato for coming, through reflection upon mathematics, to visionary ideas — if only the Platonists had not afterwards carried it too far. Our concepts are, as already mentioned above, mathematical and philosophical; the former confirm themselves of their own accord through intuition, which cannot take place at all with the latter. To elucidate [*erläutern*] means merely to analyse one's concepts, but to extend [*erweitern*] when one goes beyond the ordinary concepts. If one wants to extend one's rational knowledge through mere concepts, one is already, where no further critique takes place, a dogmatist. [Hypothesis: inference from the sufficiency of a ground for given consequences to that ground's being the right one — this assumption being made in order to be able to explain those consequences from it; *ad p.* 68.]",["An initial stray \"j\" in the OCR is dropped.","\"Vernunfterkenntnis\" rendered \"rational knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" in the plural as \"modes of knowledge\".","\"Erläutern heißt … aber erweitern\" is elliptical; \"[*erläutern*]\" and \"[*erweitern*]\" supplied for clarity, and the construction is smoothed.","\"schwärmerische Ideen\" rendered \"visionary ideas\"."]],"dohna-s16-010":["Very often the counter-proposition has as many grounds as the proposition itself. An example — Proposition: The world has a beginning; counter-proposition: The world cannot have a beginning. What, then, would the being that created it have been doing throughout the whole of eternity? Metaphysics is the extension of our knowledge through concepts *a priori*; through the construction of these [concepts], mathematics. Our reason in its pure use is dialectical. —",["The sentence on metaphysics and mathematics is elliptical in the German; \"[concepts]\" is supplied to resolve \"derselben\", and the sense of \"durch Konstruktion derselben — die Mathematik\" is restated as a clause.","\"Gegensatz\" rendered \"counter-proposition\"; \"Satz\" = proposition."]],"dohna-s16-011":["The strictest sceptic, who is no longer critical at all, can rightly be entitled a dogmatic sceptic, although this seems contradictory. They are easily caught. They want to demonstrate that no truth can be demonstrated. They refute the arguments of the dogmatists and yet assume certainty; in the end they say: everything in the world is uncertain, even this, that everything in the world is uncertain. They called it a *cathartic* [*Kathartikon*], or purge, which carries itself off along with the rest. The sceptics usually had much dialectical acuteness, as can be seen from their writings. [Persiflage — Plato's pupils Speusippus, Arcesilaus, Carneades, sceptics — partial (Pyrrho) — nothing (the academic doubt).]",["The final bracket is badly garbled in the OCR (\"Piatos\" read as Platos; \"partial Pyrrho)\" and \"{der akademische Zweifel)\"); the rendering is conjectural and the connection of the items is uncertain.","\"Persiflage\" is kept as read; doubtful reading.","Shifts from singular \"Skeptiker\" to plural \"sie\" are kept in English plural."]],"dohna-s16-012":["The mathematician must never investigate why his propositions are *a priori*. Criticism is the mean between dogmatism and scepticism, the principle of a rightful confidence in one's use of reason. The transcendent is that which can be established through no experience or sensible evidence. For the very same reason one can never come upon an error, except in the case where one can oneself prove the opposite. In these cases, then, critique is absolutely necessary, but not in mathematics, because here there are actual facts. — Scepticism is, then, certainly to be rejected; but the sceptical method, of constantly testing and investigating a matter further, has very many advantages — the doubt of postponement — *indagare*. — [This is the doubt which has the most useful consequences for the investigation of truth.] — The academic doubt was dogmatic. They wished, namely, that nothing should be certain. The Pyrrhonian doubt seems rather to have been a doubt of postponement. At least it was certainly not so general as the academic.",["The OCR \"kerne\" is corrected to \"keine\"; \"verwerf lieh\" to \"verwerflich\".","\"Man kann aus eben dem Grunde nie auf einen Irrtum stoßen …\" is obscure in the transcript; rendered closely, and the sense is doubtful.","Vertical bars in \"gewesen | zu | sein\" (apparatus) are not carried, as instructed; the words are translated in order.","\"Zweifel des Aufschubs\" rendered \"doubt of postponement\"; *indagare* kept in Latin."]],"dohna-s16-013":["[*ad* § 181 p. 57] Hypothesis — presupposition. It is a judgment inferring, from the sufficiency of a judgment [i.e. ground] for given consequences, the actuality of the ground. Every hypothesis is in the end mere opinion — from the sufficiency of a ground for a given consequence one cannot yet infer the truth of the proposition. For other grounds might also be sufficient for the [consequences]. [The sufficiency of the ground from certain given consequences yields as yet no perfect inference to its truth.]",["\"aus der Zulänglichkeit eines Urteils\" is probably a slip for \"Grundes\" (as in the following sentences); \"[i.e. ground]\" is supplied and the reading is flagged as doubtful.","\"(Folgen)\" in the source rendered \"[consequences]\" as a supplied/uncertain reading.","\"Zulänglichkeit\" rendered \"sufficiency\"; \"zureichend\" = sufficient."]],"dohna-s16-014":["We need hypotheses and cannot dispense with them at all in natural science. [Hypothesis is more than opinion, an actual presupposition.] But what is remarkable is that metaphysics too has hypotheses — not indeed in the theoretical respect, but yet in the practical.",["\"was besonders ist\" rendered \"what is remarkable\"."]],"dohna-s16-015":["1. The ground which I assume *per hypothesin* must be certain as to its possibility [otherwise one deals wholly in empty fictions], e.g. the central fire. [Cucumbers in the honey-pot. Coming claims to have discovered Isis.]",["\"Coming will die Isis gefunden haben\" is obscure; \"Coming\" may be a garbled name (OCR or transcript). Rendered literally; doubtful.","\"Gurken im Honigtopf\" is rendered literally; the allusion is not clear.","The vertical bars around \"1.\" are apparatus and are not carried."]],"dohna-s16-016":["2. The consequence from the assumed ground must be given; it must therefore be actual — the cause need only be possible.",["\"diese\" taken to refer to the consequence (\"Folge\"), as the argument requires."]],"dohna-s16-017":["3. The connection [*Konsequenz*] of the consequences with their cause must likewise be quite certain. How else can one infer from it?",["\"Konsequenz (der Folgen)\" is rendered \"connection … of the consequences\"; the sense of *Konsequenz* here (logical sequence) is a choice.","\"daraus\" is taken as referring to the assumed ground/cause."]],"dohna-s16-018":["The fourth requisite [is] the unity of the assumed ground, together with its sufficiency in respect of the consequences. [All the consequences must flow from this one assumed ground. If this does not succeed, one calls in the aid of a new | hypothesis |, a *hypothesis subsidiaria* — (thus one seeks . . .) E.g. certain medicines cure certain diseases. The sufficiency of a ground for all the consequences, however, remains uncertain, and so therefore does every hypothesis.]",["The passage is bracketed as in the Academy text; the brackets and the | signs are carried over unchanged.","\"f man . . .f\" in the OCR is garbled apparatus; I translate only \"so sucht man . . .\" and mark the break with an ellipsis.","\"Requisitum\" is a Latin term left as in the German context; \"Hypothese\" is rendered \"hypothesis\".","The sentence \"z. B. gewisse Arzneien heilen gewisse Krankheiten\" is obscure as an illustration; I have rendered it literally and not explained it."]],"dohna-s16-019":["A hypothesis, so long as it remains a hypothesis, never attains complete certainty, that is, no apodeictic certainty (where the opposite would be impossible); an empirical certainty it may well attain. We are speaking here only of hypotheses of theoretical knowledge. There are, however, also morally practical hypotheses, which are necessary in that they constantly occur in accordance with certain laws. But a hypothesis in a theoretical respect is never necessary. In a moral respect, however, [it is otherwise]: e.g. that duty is objectively, and happiness subjectively, necessary, and so on, is certain.",["\"cl. h.\" in the OCR corrected to \"d. h.\".","The last two sentences are fragmentary in the German (\"In moralischer Absicht aber, z. B. die Pflicht objektiv und Glückseligkeit subjektiv notwendig sind usw. gewiß\"); I have supplied \"[it is otherwise]\" and restructured the sentence to make it readable.","\"indem sie beständig nach gewissen Gesetzen geschehen\" is obscure (what \"happens\"?); rendered literally, with \"occur\" for geschehen."]],"dohna-s16-020":["[On § 184, p. 58] On persuasion and conviction, [*persuasio male significat* . . .]",["OCR \"signif icat . . . . j]\" corrected to \"significat . . .\"; the passage breaks off in the source.","\"[ad §184 P. 58]\" is the editors' reference to the page of Meier's Auszug; the Latin *persuasio male significat* is left untranslated since the text breaks off."]],"dohna-s16-021":["The distinction between persuasion and conviction is a precarious one. Persuasion is properly the holding-to-be-true on grounds, without investigating whether these are objective or subjective; that is, the holding true of a proposition without being able to distinguish whether it occurs on subjective or on objective grounds. Even an upright man, when he acts as judge, is unable to distinguish the subjective grounds of his judgment from the objective ones, if he is favourably disposed towards the cause of his friend.",["*Fürwahrhalten* rendered \"holding-to-be-true\" (Kemp Smith's usual \"holding for true\" is also possible).","\"Persuasion\" renders both *Überredung* and Latin *Persuasion*; \"conviction\" renders *Überzeugung*."]],"dohna-s16-022":["We have already spoken above of science. [A mode of knowledge is systematic if the connection which it has to a whole is necessary. A system is a whole in so far as the consciousness of its totality is possible only in accordance with principles. — ] It | namely, science | is properly the sum-total of modes of knowledge in so far as they are combined in a system. It is not required that they be rational. But art, too, must be carefully distinguished from science. Art *in sensu stricto* is that skill in producing something which, even when one knows it, one still cannot make. Mechanical art would be that which one can already do if one only knows it, or handicraft art. Art, however, as distinguished from common skill, is that which was defined above *in sensu stricto*.",["\"Sie | seil, die Wissenschaft |\": OCR \"seil\" corrected to \"scilicet\" (*seil.*), rendered \"namely\"; the editors' insertion is kept in | |.","*Erkenntnis* rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where the plural sense is intended.","\"handwerksmäßige Kunst\" rendered \"handicraft art\"; the wording of the sentence on mechanical art is awkward in the German and has been kept close."]],"dohna-s17-001":["[Lesson, Monday]",["Heading only; \"Stunde\" is the transcriber's marking of a new lecture hour."]],"dohna-s17-002":["Art is the system of heterogeneous skills. Besides mathematical or intuitive certainty and philosophical or discursive certainty, there is yet another certainty which is not objective. Its ground is properly subjective — it is, namely, a practically valid certainty, which one must call not exactly moral but rather pragmatic, when one accepts something not so much according to rules of morality as according to rules of prudence. A technical rule is a rule of art, a pragmatic rule is a rule of prudence, a moral rule is a rule of morality, of virtue — duty. Under practical certainty moral certainty is also included. —",["*Klugheit* rendered \"prudence\", *Sittlichkeit* \"morality\"."]],"dohna-s17-003":["The author speaks here of a mathematical certainty of the first and second rank. But there is here no distinction other than that between mathematical and discursive certainty. [Merely absolute necessity is still far from being mathematical certainty — 2 − 2 = 4. For although what is true cannot be still more true, there nevertheless always remains a difference in the degree of certainty, because in the one case proof is by concepts only, in the other, however, by intuition.]",["OCR \"kernen\" corrected to \"keinen\".","The formula \"2-2=4\" is as printed (presumably a slip for 2+2=4, in the transcript or the OCR); I have reproduced it with a minus sign rendered as printed and not corrected it.","\"dort . . . hier\" resolved as \"in the one case . . . in the other\"; the reference is presumably to philosophical (concepts) versus mathematical (intuition) proof."]],"dohna-s17-004":["*Hypothesis* signifies in most cases a merely arbitrary position. *Thesis* [is] a firm proposition.",["\"[is]\" supplied; the German has no verb."]],"dohna-s17-005":["The author now comes to the section",[]],"dohna-s17-006":["On Proofs",[]],"dohna-s17-007":["The *constitutiva* of every proof are:",["*Constitutiva* is left in Latin: the constituent elements."]],"dohna-s17-008":["1. The conducting of the proof, *probatio*,",[]],"dohna-s17-009":["2. that which is to be proved, *probandum*. It is the matter [*Materiale*] of the proof.",[]],"dohna-s17-010":["3. The relation between the two, that a correct consequence follows from it: e.g. everything extended is divisible, all bodies are extended, therefore all bodies are divisible, and thereafter the further inferences, and so on.",["OCR \"fernem\" corrected to \"fernern\" (*ferneren*), \"further\".","\"daraus\" is ambiguous; rendered \"from it\", i.e. from the relation of the two."]],"dohna-s17-011":["In the first place, that which is wholly false, and that which is immediately certain, may be entitled unprovable. The latter propositions are indemonstrable, the mediate ones demonstrable.",["\"Unerweislich\" rendered \"unprovable\"; \"genannt werden\" rendered \"may be entitled\".","\"erstlich\" has no \"secondly\" in the passage; the text may be incomplete."]],"dohna-s18-001":["[Lesson]",["Heading only."]],"dohna-s18-002":["To prove is to hold something to be true mediately, by reason of a ground. We can divide all proofs into direct [or ostensive] and indirect or apagogic. The latter are those which one derives from the falsity of the opposite. Apagogic proofs have almost more *intuitus* in them than the direct, because the contradiction is always striking. [Apodeictic proofs — with consciousness of necessity — *demonstratio*, a proof combined with intuition.]",["\"(herleitet)\" is a parenthetical in the OCR text, apparently supplying the verb; I have kept it unmarked.","*intuitus* left in Latin as technical term."]],"dohna-s18-003":["Much can be proved apagogically *ex concessis*, namely when the other party has already conceded something. These are the *argumenta ad hominem*. There are many such proofs in mathematics. They are always excellent.",["*concediert* rendered \"conceded\"."]],"dohna-s18-004":["Now let us see how they suit philosophy. If an assumed concept itself contains a contradiction, then the predicate may conflict with the concept, and the opposite of the predicate as well. [Apagogic proofs are very precarious in philosophy.] One can prove entirely different propositions at once apagogically. This occurs very often in philosophy. Someone may refute his opponent apagogically, and be refuted by him just as forcibly, so that he too is reduced *ad absurdum*. The human will is free. This proposition is indispensable in morals. For if men could not act otherwise than they do act, then all laws would be in vain. The opinion opposed to this proposition is entitled fatalism, namely, that all men act only according to certain laws determined by nature. Priestley and Price had a long dispute about this. Each brought the other *ad absurdum*, and yet nothing was settled. [Neither proves his proposition directly, but always only the falsity of the opposite. In philosophy this may easily deceive; for one may himself equally err, even though one proves that the other errs — both may very easily be in the wrong.] The given multitude of all parts is finite or infinite, that is, all the parts of a body taken together; and this we can never do, for every body is infinitely divisible, and one would never be finished. Hence a taking-together of all the parts is a *contradictio in adjecto*. —",["The long last sentences are somewhat disconnected in the transcript; I have kept them in order and divided them into shorter sentences.","\"Die Meinung des Gegenteils\" rendered \"the opinion opposed to\".","The final passage on the multitude of parts is elliptical (apparently an antinomy example); \"Zusammennehmung\" rendered \"taking-together\"."]],"dohna-s18-005":["The result of all this is that in philosophy one cannot make use of apagogic proofs, because here one cannot exhibit them in intuition.",["OCR \"närnlich\" corrected to \"nämlich\"; rendered by the \"because\" clause."]],"dohna-s18-006":["An aesthetic demonstration would be that which is entitled such when it is effected by a *bon mot* [but is not always true].",["The syntax (\"die genannt werden\") is awkward; the sense is that a demonstration by bon mot is called aesthetic."]],"dohna-s18-007":["Now we come to the section:",[]],"dohna-s18-008":["On the Sources of Our Knowledge",["Heading; *Erkenntnis* rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"dohna-s18-009":["[The author has three: experience, reason and faith.] They are not threefold but only twofold: 1. rational, 2. empirical. [There is a rational faith (*Vernunftglaube*), but only in a practical respect. This is therefore no distinct source, but always only reason, which is either theoretical or practical. All experience is twofold, immediate or mediate. The latter kind is historical faith.] As to faith, historical faith too may be entitled empirical, for it is always founded on the testimony and experience of another. A rational faith has to do with things which cannot be known at all. But no theoretical knowledge is founded on rational faith; it is sufficient solely for a practical purpose. [Knowledge is] a comparison and connection of truth into one mode of knowledge. Experience is an operation of the understanding, so that mere perception is far from sufficing. Historical faith is the connection of the experiences of others with my own. [The question now is to determine what entitles me to esteem mediate experience equally with immediate, that is, on what authority rests.] The witness who is an eyewitness, *testis oculatus*, [who himself has immediate experience,] is authentic. He is beyond challenge if he can know the truth and is willing to tell it. Capacity and integrity are therefore the two requisites.",["The sentence 'Eine Vergleichung und Verbindung der Wahrheit zu einer Erkenntnis' has no verb; I supplied '[Knowledge is]' in brackets as the likely sense, but it may be a fragment of the transcript and the reading is doubtful.","'Erfahrung ist Operation des Verstandes' kept literally; 'Wahrnehmung' rendered 'perception'.","'worauf Autorität beruhe' completed with 'that is, on what authority rests' to render the indirect question; 'unverwerflich' rendered 'beyond challenge'.","'Capazität' rendered 'capacity' (the witness's competence to know); 'Integrität' 'integrity'.","Square brackets in the German (editors' marking of Kant's later additions) are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s18-010":["*Testis auritus* [the witness by hearing]. In a series of such witnesses there is often nothing but hearsay, *rumor* <*(sine capite)*; the more numerous they are, the more uncertain the hearsay. It is like a copy of a copy. [The longer the chain, the more uncertain. Tradition is where the first witness is unknown. If you believed, you would not investigate the hearsay back to its first ground. Competence cannot be determined at all. —] But the greater the number of co-ordinate witnesses, the more credible the hearsay. It is not the best means of judging the integrity of a person whose character one does not otherwise know, to ask whether he had an interest in the matter or not. Many rules of prudence also belong here, but these may not be set out in logic.",["'<' before '(sine capite)' is the editors' apparatus mark and is kept as it stands.","'[the witness by hearing]' is my gloss on *testis auritus*, supplied in brackets.","'Tüchtigkeit' rendered 'competence' (the witness's fitness, cf. 'Capazität' in the preceding passage).","'Sage' rendered 'hearsay'; the sentence 'Wenn du glaubtest …' is obscure in the German and is translated literally."]],"dohna-s18-011":["We now wish to touch upon the concept of faith, in so far as it is not a source of knowledge but a mere judgment of reason, in so far as it has no longer a theoretical but merely a practical purpose. [Unbelieving (*ungläubig*) in respect of the practical, that is, of rational faith; incredulous (*ungläubisch*) in respect of the theoretical, that is, of historical faith. There is no word in Latin for *ungläubig*. He is unbelieving who, on moral grounds (which are sufficient as incentives to morality), will not accept something, e.g. the existence of a highest being. —]",["'ungläu bisch' in the OCR is read as 'ungläubisch'; the distinction with 'ungläubig' is rendered as 'unbelieving' / 'incredulous'.","'Erkenntnisquelle' rendered 'source of knowledge'; 'Triebfedern' 'incentives'."]],"dohna-s18-012":["A hypothesis which one holds to be necessary for a practical purpose: for example, a merchant finds a ground sufficient for undertaking something. Or again, a general must of necessity judge and resolve upon something when he stands before the enemy. Here, then, the hypothesis is not arbitrary, adopted in order to explain something, but is necessary for a practical purpose. In the former case, however, the purpose would be theoretical. All practical purposes (that is, legislations of reason) are either 1. pragmatic or 2. moral, that is, either rules of prudence or laws of morality. Thus the purpose of that general is pragmatic. Faith, regarded as a hypothesis, which becomes necessary for a moral purpose, may be entitled pure rational faith. The concept of duty is wholly distinct from that of advantage. The end set by the fulfilment of all duties is the furtherance of the highest good, in so far as it can be attained here, the happiness of men. The union of morality, the worthiness to be happy, with happiness itself, lies in no man's power. If we see that our sources of knowledge do not suffice for knowing God as a moral being, we can nevertheless have a moral faith through this. [Practical faith does not extend knowledge; it is only the transition from the theoretical to the practical.]",["'Pf hellten' in the OCR is read as 'Pflichten' (duties).","The sentence 'Der Glaube, als Hypothese angesehen, wird … notwendig, kann … genannt werden' is irregular; I smoothed it into a relative clause.","'Im erstem Falle' is taken to refer to the hypothesis adopted to explain something; the antecedent is not certain.","'Gewalt' rendered 'power'; 'Glückseligkeit' 'happiness'."]],"dohna-s18-013":["Our author now speaks of the perfection of practical knowledge, a chapter which, taken specially, does not properly belong to logic.",[]],"dohna-s19-001":["On Practical Knowledge",[]],"dohna-s19-002":["One sees that what he says on this subject does not quite belong to logic. It would be well to discard the merely speculative matters in religion. A speculative mode of knowledge is one that has no application at all to the practical [reading of 'Avaspraktisch' doubtful]. Practical propositions are either imperatives, in which case they are opposed to the theoretical, or they are grounds of possible imperatives, and in that case they are opposed to the merely speculative. For example: There is a God. This is not a merely speculative but a practical proposition, for it contains grounds of possible imperatives.",["'wegheße' in the OCR is read as 'wegließe' (discard); doubtful.","'Avaspraktisch' is garbled OCR; probably 'was praktisch', i.e. 'what in practice'. I translate 'has no application at all to the practical' and flag the reading as uncertain.","'Erkenntnis' rendered 'mode of knowledge' where it takes the indefinite article."]],"dohna-s19-003":["[Practical propositions (are those which) contain grounds of knowledge (*Erkenntnisgründe*) for possible imperatives. The proposition: There is another life, is practical, because it contains grounds for possible imperatives. — Theoretical propositions too are entitled practical if they contain grounds for these. Speculative propositions are those which contain no grounds for them at all. Imperatives determine to action.]",["'(sind die, welche)' is the editors' parenthetical insertion and is retained."]],"dohna-s19-004":["We now pass to a new section:",[]],"dohna-s20-001":["On Concepts",[]],"dohna-s20-002":["All knowledge is through intuition or concept. Representation is an elementary expression which cannot be analysed further. With representation (*Repräsentation*) there can yet be connected apperception, the consciousness of the representation.",["The OCR mark '■' before 'das Bewußtsein' is omitted as apparatus."]],"dohna-s20-003":["[ad p. 14] A representation connected with consciousness is entitled perception, *perceptio*. This *perceptio* becomes *cognitio* in so far as the representation, with consciousness, is referred to an object. This representation is again twofold:",["'Bewußtsem' read as 'Bewußtsein'. 'Wahrnehmung' rendered 'perception'."]],"dohna-s20-004":["1. *intuitus*; this is not yet knowledge,",["'kern' read as 'kein'."]],"dohna-s20-005":["2. *conceptus*, which contains what is common to several objects, *nota communis*. The concept, considered by itself, is:",[]],"dohna-s20-006":["a) *conceptus empiricus*, when there is sensation in its representation [for whose existence a preceding experience is required. The matter of all empirical representation is sensation.]",[]],"dohna-s20-007":["b) *conceptus purus*; this is entitled *notio*. It may be called a pure concept of the understanding. The concept of the triangle is a *conceptus* |a| *priori*.",["The '|a|' is carried over from the source apparatus; probably marks a break around 'a priori'."]],"dohna-s20-008":["[Concept is *repraesentatio discursiva*. The act whereby we give to a concept the corresponding intuition is entitled exhibition (*Darstellung*), *exhibitio*. Knowledge is more than *conceptus*, more than *intuitus*; it is both together. We seek objective reality, which we obtain through application to intuition.]",[]],"dohna-s20-009":["An *idea* is entitled a concept of reason to which no object adequate to it can be given in intuition.",[]],"dohna-s20-010":["A representation, when it springs from the understanding, is always a concept. But the intuition of red does not yet give a concept of the understanding. The representation of something as cause can be made only through the understanding, and is therefore a *notio*. [A cause can never be experienced, for causality is a *notio*.] All intuition is only representation of that which is given to us through the senses. Where it has place, as, for example, in mathematical concepts, there are never *notiones*, pure concepts of the understanding, but certainly *conceptus a priori*. [*Conceptus purus*: 1. *intellectualis*, 2. *rationalis*. A *conceptus purus intellectualis* is entitled *notio*, e.g. virtue. A *conceptus purus* to which no corresponding intuition can be given is an *idea*, e.g. of God. Of virtue I can give examples; yet it is not a concept of experience. Ontology has to do with sheer *notiones*. Concepts are, in respect of their matter, *dati* or *factitii*. The former are entitled *notiones*, the latter empirical concepts (*conceptus arbitrarii*, arbitrary). Every concept of experience is made. Perception is only subjective, and is not yet experience. Experience is knowledge with consciousness of the relation to the object.]",["'kernen' read as 'keinen'; 'Die Ontologiehat' as 'Die Ontologie hat'.","'Erfahrungsbegriff' rendered 'concept of experience'.","The statement that notiones are *dati* and empirical concepts *factitii*, and that every empirical concept is 'made', is translated as it stands."]],"dohna-s20-011":["Two components belong to sensibility, sense and imagination. There are only logical concepts; the so-called aesthetic concepts are merely representations without concept. There are pure concepts of the understanding *a priori*, but also concepts of the understanding that are sensibly determined [ad § 255].",["'Einbildungskraft' rendered 'imagination'.","'Stücke' rendered 'components'."]],"dohna-s20-012":["Experience is entitled empirical knowledge. In an empirical representation something must be perceived (sensed with consciousness). Experience therefore presupposes empirical intuition and concept, and sensation is indispensable to it. Transcendental philosophy, which must precede metaphysics, deals with the origin of the pure concepts of the understanding. Logic does not do this; it is concerned only with actual acts. For the use of a concept abstraction is required, but the concept is not thereby made. The making of it takes place, first, in that something is regarded as a partial representation which can be common to several things, for example the red colour. Secondly, when I regard the partial representation as a *nota*, as a ground of knowledge of a thing, so that, for example, I know blood, rose and the like by means of red. The third act is abstraction: [I regard] this partial representation as a ground of knowledge, in so far as I leave out of account all the remaining partial representations. A concept is therefore a partial representation, in so far as I abstract in doing so from all the others.",["Restated the third act: the German has no verb after 'Abstraktion'; I supplied '[I regard]' in brackets to complete the construction.","'Erkenntnisgrund' rendered 'ground of knowledge'; 'Teilvorstellung' as 'partial representation'; *nota* kept in Latin.","'Letzteres geschieht 1. ... 2. ...' divided into 'first/secondly' sentences; the numerals are Kant's/the transcriber's.","'Handlungen' rendered 'acts' (logical acts of the understanding)."]],"dohna-s20-013":["[To abstract, in the philosophical sense, is a negative concept: not to attend. There are no abstract concepts. We can only abstract from something, from certain differences, from marks of things. The distinction between abstract and concrete lies not in the concept but in the *usus conceptuum* [use of concepts].",["The opening square bracket is the editors' and is left unclosed because the passage is cut off here; the bracket evidently closes in a later passage or the next one.","'nicht attendieren' kept close to the Latin-derived verb: 'not to attend'."]],"dohna-s20-014":["A concept can be contained in other concepts (be a part of them); but the others can also at the same time stand under it, it being their ground. These are merely different relations of one and the same concept, which may very well be at once part and ground of something. 'Man' is the partial representation of the negro and of the scholar (for it is always a part of the concept of the latter, and so on, that he is a man); but at the same time this concept is also a ground of knowledge under which the white man, the negro and the scholar belong.]",["The closing square bracket is the editors', closing the insertion opened in s20-013.","'Neger' rendered 'negro' as the period term in the transcript; the usage is that of the source."]],"dohna-s20-015":["By abstracting nothing is produced; on the contrary, something is left out. One must not say, 'I abstract a representation', but 'I abstract from a representation'. I can either abstract from the diversity of the things to which this concept is common, or I can attend to it in comparison with others. This is properly the use of the concept: in the first case *in abstracto*, in the second *in concreto*.",["'Verschiedenheit' rendered 'diversity'."]],"dohna-s20-016":["The faculty of using concepts *in concreto* is entitled the common understanding; the faculty of using them *in abstracto*, the speculative understanding. The abstract concept {is contained in the} concrete and itself <holds> the concrete under it. A higher concept is one that comprehends several others within itself. A *repraesentatio singularis* has an *intuitus* and indicates it immediately, but is at bottom no *conceptus*. Socrates, for example, is no *conceptus*.",["Braces and angle brackets are carried over from the Academy apparatus as printed; the OCR of '{ist in dem)' looks mismatched, so I give 'is contained in the'.","'Repraesentatio singidaris' is an OCR slip for *repraesentatio singularis*; 'intuitum' corrected to nominative *intuitus* for the English.","'gemeiner Verstand' rendered 'common understanding'."]],"dohna-s20-017":["[Lesson, Monday]",["Editorial heading marking a new lecture hour."]],"dohna-s20-018":["As soon as I make use of words, the representation is a single concept. But if the concept is expressed quite particularly, one then regards it *in abstracto*. It is then no longer entitled *conceptus universalis*, but *communis*. A *conceptus communis* can, in respect of the concepts contained in it, be called *superior* [a higher concept], and in respect of those in which it is contained, *inferior*. [The *inferior* (lower) always contains more within itself; the former, that is the *superior*, contains more under itself but less within itself. For the *conceptus superior* is quite general precisely because it contains little within itself; for example, the concept man under that of animal. Hence the latter is a *conceptus superior*; though it now contains more under itself, it nevertheless contains less within itself. We here leave out of account the property of understanding (in man). The higher the concepts, the simpler. Living corporeal beings; higher still, corporeal beings, simple. The highest: being, thing, the simplest. When we look to the content of the concept, we regard it *in concreto*; when we look to the *sphaera*, we regard it *in abstracto*. Of the first kind is the knowledge of the practical man (with mole's eyes, merely on the spot).]",["The signs | ... | in the German are editorial and carried over as printed: '|der|' dropped nothing but is not rendered, as it belongs to the apparatus.","The OCR sign '■ — ■' after 'Je höher die Begriffe' is garbled; I omit it, and 'Je höher ... desto einfacher' is translated as it stands.","'Wird aber der Begriff ganz besonders ausgedrückt, so betrachtet man es': 'es' neuter, resolved as the concept; the sense of 'besonders' is doubtful.","'Der Begriff Mensch unter dem des Tiers': the argument is muddled in the transcript (man is under animal, so animal is the superior); I translate as it stands."]],"dohna-s20-019":["Every *conceptus communis* is entitled *species* in respect (relation) of the *conceptus* under which it is contained, and *genus* in respect of those which are contained under it. [The concepts of *genus* and *species* differ merely in respect of relation.] Now what in one respect was called *genus* I can in another call *species*; for example, man is *genus* in many respects, but in relation to animal he is *species*. Can a *genus summum* be found? The *genus summum* is that which is contained under no other and is no partial concept, that is, has no further part. If there is now a *genus summum*, an object in general, can a *species infima* also be found? One must assume that there are *conceptus communes* under which no others can be subsumed. But this remains merely arbitrary, and one cannot therefore say that a *species infima* is to be found.",["'Respectu genns' is an OCR slip for *respectu generis* (or *genus*); I translated the sense only.","'Den Teil weiter hat' rendered 'has no further part'; 'keinen Teil weiter hat' is slightly loose in the German."]],"dohna-s20-020":["In the subordination of human concepts we begin from the lowest stage, the *individuum*; this is reckoned with the *conceptus singularis*. From there one proceeds to the *conceptus superior* or *communis*. For example: oaks [*Hex* in Spain, with their splendid acorns, which taste like chestnuts]; growths; plants; bodies; substances. At last one arrives *per analysin* at the concept of a thing. The *conceptus superior* is always only a part of the *inferior*, so that the *conceptus summus* contains the very least within itself, [namely] object, since one has not determined at all what; but it contains everything else under itself.",["'Avir' is an OCR slip for 'wir'; 'conceptui' (dative) rendered in the nominative for the English.","'Hex' (the holm oak, *ilex*) kept as printed; spelling of the original doubtful.","'Objekt, da man ... bestimmt hat, was' is elliptical; I supplied '[namely]'."]],"dohna-s20-021":["Our knowledge gains extraordinarily when we always go to the *conceptus superior*. The multitude of things contained under a concept is entitled the logical *sphaera* of the concept. The greater this is, the smaller is its content. [The *sphaera* is *latissime patens* the simpler and more abstract the concept is. By this one understands not the content but the circle of application, like a line which in itself has no breadth but yet encloses a large space. A concept that has no *sphaera* at all, for example that of the individual Julius Caesar, is equal to a point.]",["'Wenn' in 'dadurch, wenn' rendered 'when'.","The comparison with the line is obscure in the transcript; rendered as it stands.","Both uses of 'Inhalt' rendered 'content'."]],"dohna-s20-022":["Some concepts have one and the same *sphaera*, like the concept of the necessary and that of the unalterable. Such concepts are entitled *conceptus reciproci*, reciprocal concepts. [A concept is] *latior* if one understands still more under it. If one wished to make a consideration and comparison between metal and wood, these are *conceptus heterogenei*, and have no relation at all in respect of their quantity, for example 100 years and a German mile, [because none of these concepts which are compared with one another stands under the other.] *Conceptus reciproci* must be homogeneous. One should not properly say *conceptus universalis*. Only the application can be used *in abstracto*, and it is then *quoad usum universalis* or *particularis*, and so on. A *conceptus* is always a *repraesentatio communis*; there are no others in logic.",["'universabs' is an OCR slip for *universalis*.","'Latior | conceptus |, wenn | man | noch mehr darunter versteht' is fragmentary; I supplied '[A concept is]' and carry the editor's strokes by omission since they enclose editorial insertions.","The brackets around 'weil keiner...' are the editors'; the dash and space inside them are an OCR artefact."]],"dohna-s20-023":["There are different kinds of *differentia*. It is namely:",["'Dif ferentia' corrected to *differentia*."]],"dohna-s20-024":["1. *generica*: the difference of kinds, *generum*; for example, physicians call certain diseases *materias sui generis*.",["'Gattungen' rendered 'kinds' to avoid confusion with the Latin *genus*."]],"dohna-s20-025":["2. *specifica*: the distinction of the things which belong to one *genus*; in the *species* there must be something which was not contained in the *genus*.",[]],"dohna-s20-026":["3. *numerica*: that is, the difference of the *conceptus singulares*, in so far as they are not common to several. In the case of men we indicate it by *nomina propria*.",[]],"dohna-s21-001":["On Definitions",[]],"dohna-s21-002":["The logical perfection of the definition consists:",[]],"dohna-s21-003":["1. in distinctness; it is the highest requirement of the definition.",["'Deutlichkeit' rendered 'distinctness'."]],"dohna-s21-004":["2. in *completudo*, or in distinctness through sufficient marks.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'."]],"dohna-s21-005":["3. *praecisio*, exactness of measure; that is, the separation of everything else which is not required for sufficient distinctness.",["'Abgemessenheit' rendered 'exactness of measure'."]],"dohna-s21-006":["[Precision is a requirement of the second rank for definition. No more marks than are necessary. Definition is the highest logical perfection of a concept.]",["Square brackets are the Academy editors' marks and are kept as printed. 'Bedürfnis' is rendered 'requirement'; 'Range' as 'rank'.","'der Begriffs' in the OCR is corrected to 'des Begriffs' (genitive), rendered 'of a concept'."]],"dohna-s21-007":["*Conceptus adaequatus in minimis terminis*—*conceptus completus, praecise determinatus*. When the concept has these requirements, it is a *conceptus rei adaequatus*.",["Latin technical terms are kept in italics. They run roughly: an adequate concept in the fewest terms; a complete concept, precisely determined; an adequate concept of a thing.","'Erfordernisse' is rendered 'requirements'."]],"dohna-s21-008":["We shall now show how definitions are made.",["The OCR 'Defmitionen' is corrected to 'Definitionen'."]],"dohna-s21-009":["All concepts are of two kinds, namely:",["The OCR 'nämbch' is corrected to 'nämlich'."]],"dohna-s21-010":["1. *Conceptus dati* [given concepts], and these are either",["The English in square brackets is my gloss, supplied to complete the sense. The OCR '1 .' is read as '1.'."]],"dohna-s21-011":["(a) *empirici*—given *a posteriori*,",[]],"dohna-s21-012":["(b) *rationati*—given *a priori*. [E.g. the concept of cause, of magnitude, of substance, of effect, time and space. We cannot observe space. It is not an empirical representation, for such a representation always rests upon perception.]",["'rationati' is probably a slip for 'rationales', or a transcriber's form. I keep it as printed.","The OCR gives no full stop after 'beobachten'. I supply one in the translation.","'Wahrnehmung' is rendered 'perception'."]],"dohna-s21-013":["2. *Conceptus facticii* (made, fabricated [*gedichtete*] concepts)—they can be given.",["The OCR 'facticn (' is read as 'facticii ('. The dashes are kept as printed.","'gedichtet' is rendered 'fabricated', and the German is left in brackets because the term is a choice. The final clause 'sie können gegeben werden' is translated as it stands, though it is odd and possibly corrupt."]],"dohna-s21-014":["(a) *ex datis a posteriori*,",[]],"dohna-s21-015":["(b) *ex datis a priori*.",[]],"dohna-s21-016":["All definitions of given concepts, if these are given *a priori*, are invariably analytic; all definitions of made concepts are, without exception, synthetic.",["'allemal' is rendered 'invariably'."]],"dohna-s21-017":["[A synthetic definition is that which arises from many concepts taken together. I make for myself the concept of a square (*est rectangulum quadrilaterum aequilaterum*) through the definition. The definition therefore precedes the concept. So it is always in synthesis.",["The OCR 'Avelche' is corrected to 'welche'.","The Latin means 'it is an equilateral quadrilateral rectangle'. The bracket opens here and is not closed in this passage."]],"dohna-s21-018":["Analysis. I seek out in the concept that I already have the marks [contained in it]. This requires, first, (exposition of the concept) (e.g. body). Through exposition we obtain the elementary propositions for the definition. Here too [there are] the three requisites. Body: extension, composition, divisibility already lie in it.)",["'Merkmale' is rendered 'marks'. The words '[contained in it]' are supplied by me.","The sentence 'Auch hier die 3 Requisita' has no verb, so '[there are]' is supplied. 'Ausgedehnt, zusammengesetztes, teilbar' is a list of adjectives and is rendered as the corresponding nouns.","I restate the final clause as 'extension, composition, divisibility already lie in it'. The closing parenthesis has no matching opening one in the OCR."]],"dohna-s21-019":["Mathematical definitions can never be false, because mathematics makes its own concepts. Definitions of empirical concepts are synthetic. Strictly they can never be defined, only described.]",["The OCR 'sjmthetisch' is corrected to 'synthetisch'. 'Erfahrungsbegriffe' is rendered 'empirical concepts'.","The bracket closes here, ending the one opened in the previous passage."]],"dohna-s21-020":["An empirical concept can also be given analytically. In that case not merely the concept is given, but also the object. If, for example, one wished to define water, one would define only one's concept of it, not the object itself. It is the same with all other empirical concepts. One may well define the concepts themselves, but not the object, since we cannot find all its marks. In the case of all empirical concepts the definition of the concept is, in and by itself, entirely dispensable. (It is indeed wholly subjective.) The *conceptus dati* can usefully be defined only so far as they are given *a priori*. My definition, in respect of the object, will never become complete (*complet*) and *eo ipso* never a definition.",["'Erfahrungsbegriffe' is rendered 'empirical concepts'.","The OCR parentheses '(nur)' and '(Sie ist ja ganz subjektiv.)' are kept. 'complet' is kept in the transcript's form."]],"dohna-s21-021":["So far we have dealt with given concepts, and we now come to made concepts. Here all definitions are synthetic, for data always lie at the basis, and these are put together. All mathematical definitions are synthetic. All approximations to empirical concepts, [and] observations, are synthetic. But the synthesis is not arbitrary, so the concept is not *arbitrarie facticius* [made arbitrarily]. The mathematician can never err, he can only fall short in precision. For example Wolff, in his definition of the circle, which can be put more briefly than he put it. He did not thereby err, but merely fell short in precision.",["'hegen' in the OCR is corrected to 'liegen'.","'[and]' is supplied, and the English in brackets after *arbitrarie facticius* is my gloss. The sentence 'Z. B. Wolff…' is a fragment and is kept as such, with 'For example' carried over."]],"dohna-s21-022":["I [hour]",["'Stunde' is the editors' bracketed expansion and is kept in square brackets. It marks the start of a new lecture hour."]],"dohna-s21-023":["Definition is the declaration of the representations that I make for myself of the object. To made concepts belong also those that arise from perceptions. If a definition of these is possible, it can be only synthetic. But we can define empirical concepts only nominally [describe them. Description is distinguished from definition in that it does not have the requirements of completeness (*completudo*) and precision.]",["'Vorstellungen' is rendered 'representations'. 'Erfahrungsbegriffe' is rendered 'empirical concepts'.","The bracket is left open in the passage, as in the source."]],"dohna-s21-024":["We now pass to the concepts given *a priori*. All definitions of concepts given *a priori* are made *per analysin*. [The beginning here is always the exposition—(not the definition).] Take, for example, the concept of right. It is certainly not innate in us; to bring it forth we had to reflect upon it. In the concept of virtue there is contained first conformity to law in actions, then conformity to law in right actions, and then freedom besides. [What stands in conflict with inclinations to the contrary—in this it is distinguished from holiness.]",["The OCR '{sondern)' is read as '(sondern)' and translated 'it is certainly not innate in us; to bring it forth…'. The transcript's turn from 'Recht' to 'Tugend' is kept as it stands.","'Gesetzmäßigkeit zu Handlungen' is rendered 'conformity to law in actions'. 'rechten Handlungen' means 'right actions'.","The last bracketed sentence is elliptical. The subject is presumably virtue, but the German does not say so, so I do not add it."]],"dohna-s21-025":["In the analysis of a concept the first step—the exposition—is to become aware gradually of the manifold that is contained in my consciousness; the second is definition, when the exposition is complete. But how can we become certain whether we have exposed a concept completely? One must be very cautious in such cases, as in those of substance and cause—concepts that are purely *a priori*. [One can never be wholly conscious of completeness (*completudo*) in philosophical definitions. It is very different not to be conscious that something is lacking, and to be conscious that nothing is lacking.]",["'philosopisehen' in the OCR is corrected to 'philosophischen'.","'lste' and '2tens' are read as 'first' and 'second'. 'exponiert' is rendered 'exposed' in the sense of set out."]],"dohna-s21-026":["Remark. With given concepts one must never begin with definition, but always with exposition. With made concepts (*conc. facticiis*), for example mathematical ones, the definition must always come first. The exposition begins from elementary propositions. In respect of subsidiary concepts, if one takes the word strictly, neither definition nor demonstration can take place at all. These words belong properly only to mathematics. Nevertheless in philosophy we call it definition, so far as we presume completeness (*completudo*). It is not always necessary to define.—",["'conc. factieiis' in the OCR is corrected to 'conc. facticiis'. 'Anmerkung' is rendered 'Remark'.","'für f die Mathematik': the stray 'f' is dropped as an OCR slip. 'Nebenbegriffe' is rendered 'subsidiary concepts'; the sense is uncertain."]],"dohna-s21-027":["We now go further and wish to deal with",["The sentence breaks off here and continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-028":["The rules of definitions",["This is a heading and the continuation of the previous sentence ('Von den Regeln…')."]],"dohna-s21-029":["The following rules belong to the appraisal of definitions:",[]],"dohna-s21-030":["1. whether the alleged definition is true as a proposition,",["The OCR '1 .' is read as '1.'."]],"dohna-s21-031":["2. whether the definition is distinct as a concept.—",["'deutlich' is rendered 'distinct'."]],"dohna-s21-032":["Two things hinder the distinctness of a concept, namely",[]],"dohna-s21-033":["(a) when the marks are tautological, [that is,] I explain *idem per idem* [the same by the same],",["The words in square brackets are supplied by me as a gloss."]],"dohna-s21-034":["(b) when I explain *obscurum per aeque obscurum* [the obscure by what is equally obscure].",["The English in square brackets is my gloss."]],"dohna-s21-035":["The third criterion is whether the concept, as a distinct concept, is exhaustive (*ausführlich*) [completeness: *completudo*],",["'ausführlich' is rendered 'exhaustive'. The German is given because the rendering is a choice. The bracketed *Completudo* is the editors'."]],"dohna-s21-036":["the fourth, whether the concept that I regard as exhaustive is precise. [Precision.] How are definitions to be made?",["The bracketed '[Präcision.]' is the editors' and is kept."]],"dohna-s21-037":["1. Seek several elementary propositions (here we are speaking of analytic ones).",["The OCR '1.' is retained."]],"dohna-s21-038":["2. See to it that the predicate is not just as obscure as the concept that you wish to explain.—",[]],"dohna-s21-039":["3. Collect several such predicates, until you are certain that together they exhaust the whole concept.",["'exhaurieren' is rendered 'exhaust'."]],"dohna-s21-040":["4. See to it that no one mark already contains another and so can be dispensed with. (Through this one does not yet learn to make definitions.)",[]],"dohna-s21-041":["The most useful of all rules is that one must begin with exposition, whereby one obtains elementary propositions for a definition. In an analytic definition we always reserve to ourselves the extension of our knowledge, and perhaps also the possibility of thinking the opposite in future. If no concept lies at the basis, I cannot speak of the thing at all. This, however, can always be done once we have made the concept clear. One must guard especially against reciprocating the definition, that is, converting it. All definitions in philosophy must be regarded as *tentamina* [attempts] at philosophizing.",["'Erkenntnis' is rendered 'knowledge'.","'vielleicht auch künftig das Gegenteil zu meinen' is rendered loosely as 'perhaps also the possibility of thinking the opposite in future'. The sense is uncertain.","'Dies geht aber dann immer an' is read as 'this can always be done', with 'this' referring to speaking of the thing. 'Reciprociert' is rendered 'reciprocating'. The bracketed gloss on *tentamina* is mine."]],"dohna-s21-042":["The perfection of a definition, so far as quantity is concerned, rests on this: that the *definitio* reciprocates with the *definitum*, so that one can put the one for the other. [When the sphere (*sphaera*) of the definition is equal to the sphere of the *definitum*.] *Definitio nec sit latior nec angustior definito* [let the definition be neither wider nor narrower than the defined]. A *conceptus* is *latior* than another if it contains less, *angustior* if it has more determinations. This is, namely, the consequence of the narrowing of the concept.",["'nee' in the OCR is corrected to 'nec', and 'coneeptus' to 'conceptus'. The stray 'I' and '|' in the OCR ('was I die | Quantität') are dropped as apparatus marks.","'sphaera der Definitionen' (plural) is rendered as singular, 'of the definition'. 'Bestimmungen' is rendered 'determinations'. The bracketed gloss on the Latin tag is mine."]],"dohna-s21-043":["Quality in logic—distinctness. The logical requisite:",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-044":["*Ne definiatur obscurum per aeque obscurum.* The",["The Latin means 'let the obscure not be defined by what is equally obscure'. The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-045":["2. logical relation is the relation of the marks in the definition to the concept of a *definitum*. Every ground in general must be something other than the consequence; without this it is not a ground. The third rule is",["'Die … 2. logische Relation' spans the break between 044 and 045, so 'The second logical relation' is the sense. 'Jeder Grund generaliter' is rendered 'every ground in general'.","The passage breaks off before the third rule is stated."]],"dohna-s21-046":["3. *Non definiendum in circulo*: that is, one must not explain *idem per idem* [the same by the same]. If the identity is obvious, the definition is entitled tautological. [Thus: *Ratio est id, per quod cognoscimus, cur aliquid sit.* But *cur* means *quam ob rationem*. The explanation is therefore wholly tautological and says nothing new.] If the identity is concealed, we call this method explaining in a circle. The fourth rule:",["The OCR 'circulo f , d. h.' is garbled; I read it as a repetition of 'circulo' followed by 'd. h.' ('that is') and translated accordingly. 'f' is a stray mark and is dropped.","'heißt es tautologisch' rendered 'is entitled tautological' per the method's rule for heißen; the subject ('es') I take to be the definition, supplied in English.","The square-bracketed sentence is an editorial/transcript insertion in the Academy text and is kept in brackets."]],"dohna-s21-047":["4. Modality: necessity here is logical perfection. An empirical proposition does not contain it. Empirical concepts cannot be defined at all.",["The dashes of the German are smoothed into full stops; nothing is added."]],"dohna-s21-048":["Definitions can be divided into",["The sentence breaks off; the continuation is in the next passage."]],"dohna-s21-049":["nominal [definitions] and, in distinction from others, real definitions, which suffice for the derivation of all predicates and concepts. Through real explanations (*Sacherklärungen*) we see the possibility of things. Nominal explanations serve merely | to | distinguish a thing. A nominal definition which sufficed to distinguish the thing from all others would be fully as good as a real definition. Mathematical definitions are all real. A description differs from a definition in that it contains only what is historical. The exposition of a concept in which it remains undetermined whether it is complete or precise is a description.",["The German opens with 'Nominal- und — zum Unterschied von andern, Realerklärungen'; this irregular construction is smoothed, with '[definitions]' supplied.","'Description' is left as in the German (the English word is identical); 'complet' and 'präcis' rendered 'complete' and 'precise'.","The apparatus signs | are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s22-001":["[Lecture, Monday] On the Division of Concepts",["'Stunde, Montag' is an editorial heading in brackets and is kept in brackets."]],"dohna-s22-002":["[It consists in the distinct representation of all lower concepts, so far as they are contained under a higher one and are opposed to one another.] Logical division is entitled *Einteilung* [division], and is distinguished from the partition of the concept, or analysis. By the *divisio logica* is understood, not the partition of the concept itself (*divisio realis*), but that of its *sphaera* [sphere]. For example, the concept of man has a *sphaera*; it has no manifold within itself, but comprehends many others under itself. [A division of trees, for example, into crown, trunk and root would be *partitio*, not *divisio*.",["The bracket opened at 'Eine Einteilung' is not closed in this passage; it is closed in the next passage, and I leave it open.","'Die logische Division heißt Einteilung' rendered with 'is entitled' per the method; 'Teilung' here rendered 'partition' to mark the contrast with *partitio*.","'hat eine sphaeram' is OCR/transcript accusative; rendered as *sphaera*."]],"dohna-s22-003":["*Partitio* is different from *divisio*; the latter divides only the concept. Logical division is always dichotomy, *a* or *non a*. All living beings are mortal or not mortal. This is analytic division, according to the principle of contradiction. The principle of all logical divisions is the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria* [principle of the excluded middle between two contradictories]. [There is also] synthetic division into *a* and *b*. Polytomy presupposes real knowledge, not merely knowledge of form. One can never be certain of the completeness of one's division. Strictly, one would have to be able to survey how much is possible. But these are concrete concepts, objects of experience. Here only *partitio* is possible.]",["'a oder nona' read as 'a oder non a'; the OCR slip is silently corrected.","'Synthetische Einteilung in a und b' is elliptical; '[There is also]' is supplied.","'Billig' rendered 'strictly' (i.e. 'in fairness, properly'); a judgment, the sense being 'ideally'.","The closing bracket ends the insertion opened in the previous passage."]],"dohna-s22-004":["All division can proceed by *opposita* or by *disparata*. The first can also be made *a priori*, but empirical (experiential) division is only by *disparata*, for example the different races of men, reddish-brown, yellow, and so on. | One | by *opposita* would be if I said: all men are either white or not white.",["'Erfahrungs->' is an OCR/apparatus remnant; read as the editor's gloss 'Erfahrungs-' and rendered '(experiential)'. The angle bracket belongs to the apparatus.","The sign | and 'eine' are carried over as they stand; 'eine' is perhaps an editorial insertion."]],"dohna-s22-005":["Now we shall go through the parts that belong to the division of a concept.",[]],"dohna-s22-006":["1. The concept divided, *conceptus divisus*, whose sphere can be divided.",[]],"dohna-s22-007":["2. The *membra dividentia*, the lower concepts, which are contained under the higher. They are members of the division, which, taken together, form a complete whole. This is recognised from their being *aequat* [equal] to the sphere. A division therefore expresses this: that lower concepts, subordinated to higher, are together equal to the whole sphere of the concept.",["'aequat' is left as in the text (probably *aequales* or 'equal'); doubtful reading, rendered '[equal]'."]],"dohna-s22-008":["A given division can be considered in relation to its subdivisions, *Subdivisionen* (division of a member of the division), which go very far, down to the individual, and to its co-divisions (*Codivisionen*), for example men, with respect to <1.> learning, <2.> sex, <3., 4.> and so on. The members of the co-division are entitled *membra codividentia*.",["'Subdi Visionen ^Einteilung' is OCR-garbled; read as 'Subdivisionen (Einteilung ...'.","The angle brackets are the Academy apparatus (the numerals); kept as they stand."]],"dohna-s22-009":["We now come to the rules proper. The first is:",[]],"dohna-s22-010":["1. The *conceptus* must not be *latior* [wider] than all the *membra dividentia* taken together; the less in itself, the more under itself. [Otherwise it contains too much.]",["The bracketed remark is the transcript's own; 'zuviel' rendered 'too much'. The sense is awkward, since a concept wider than its members would have a sphere too large."]],"dohna-s22-011":["2. The *conceptus* must also not be *angustior* [narrower] than the *membra dividentia*.",[]],"dohna-s22-012":["3. Between the members of the division there must be oppositions, and these one takes together disjunctively, that is, by 'either – or'. [The *membra dividentia* must be opposed to one another, otherwise it is not logical division.]",["'Opjwsitionen' is OCR for 'Oppositionen'; corrected."]],"dohna-s22-013":["4. The concept divided must not contradict the concept of the division.",[]],"dohna-s22-014":["5. The members of the subdivision must not fall under the members of the superior division; that would be contradictory. Particular attention must be given to this. For example, to the ignorant one must oppose not the learned man, but him who possesses science.",["'Obereinteilung' rendered 'superior division'."]],"dohna-s22-015":["6. The number of the members of the division I must derive from the nature of the concept divided. All empirical division can more properly be entitled partition than *divisio logica*. [In the latter one must know that there are neither more nor fewer members of the division; in the empirical this need not be so.]",["The final '— ' before the bracket is dropped as a trailing mark."]],"dohna-s22-016":["All logical division (which abstracts from all content) can be nothing other than dichotomy (2 members), *a* – *non a*, according to the principle of contradiction. [It is the principle of all analytic judgments.] Polytomy, trichotomy, and so on: divisions that are not made according to this principle are always synthetic. [Synthetic divisions: trichotomy, polytomy generally. It is striking that trichotomy occurs so often, [for example] God: (holiness, goodness, justice). It is as it were everywhere here a supreme proposition, or a condition, <a conditioned,> a consequence: unity the condition, plurality the conditioned, totality the consequence, the combination of both together (totality: plurality as unity).]",["The OCR 'f' (stray mark, probably a sign for 'for example' or 'namely') is rendered '[for example]' in one place and dropped elsewhere; doubtful. 'Subst f Gott' is especially unclear.","'QPJ' and the like are not present here; the angle brackets are the apparatus. The bracketed 'a conditioned' is the editor's inserted reading.","The passage 'Es ist gleichsam hier überall ein oberster Satz ...' is syntactically loose in the German; I have kept it as nearly as sense allows, and the construction is doubtful."]],"dohna-s22-017":["On Judgments",["'Urteile' rendered 'judgments' (the heading)."]],"dohna-s22-018":["A representation which is general through the consciousness of it as a mark is a clear concept. The consciousness of a general representation (*repraesentatio communis*) is therefore entitled not merely concept but clear concept. The concept which becomes clear through a judgment is entitled a distinct concept, and a concept which becomes distinct through an inference of reason is entitled a concept of reason (according to Wolff, a complete one).",["*Vernunftbegriff* rendered 'concept of reason'; *Vernunftschluß* rendered 'inference of reason'."]],"dohna-s22-019":["[Judgment is the representation of the unity of given concepts, so far as one is subordinated to the other or excluded from it. —",["The opening bracket is not closed in this passage and is left open."]],"dohna-s22-020":["1. Clear concept, through consciousness of universality.",[]],"dohna-s22-021":["2. Distinct concept, not merely through *apprehensio*, but through *judicium*.",["'Judicium' is rendered in lower case in italics; the German gives it capitalised."]],"dohna-s22-022":["3. Concept of reason.",[]],"dohna-s23-001":["3 judgments:",[]],"dohna-s23-002":["1. Where one [concept stands] under the other as subject under predicate: categorical.",["'f' is a stray OCR mark and is dropped; '[concept stands]' is supplied."]],"dohna-s23-003":["2. The judgment where one concept [stands] under the other as the conditioned under the condition, as consequence under ground: hypothetical.",["'f' again dropped; '[stands]' supplied."]],"dohna-s23-004":["3. The judgment which contains a consequence that can be divided: disjunctive. QPJ.]",["'QPJ' is an OCR garble that I cannot read and leave as it stands.","The closing bracket ends the insertion that began at the previous passage-group."]],"dohna-s23-005":["To act (*Handeln*) is entitled separating the cause from the effect. If a concept is to become distinct, one must always pass a *judicium*. The mark immediately becomes the predicate of the judgment, for example: man is an animal. This distinct concept comes about only through a judgment. From this one sees that, before one goes on to distinct concepts, one must treat of judgments.",["'Handeln heißt die Ursache von der Wirkung absondern' is odd in the context, and the reading may be a transcriber's slip (perhaps *Handlung* or *Abhandeln*); I translate it as it stands."]],"dohna-s23-006":["The concept of reason arises from an inference of reason. For example: all men are living beings, all living beings are animals, *ergo* all men are animals. Thus one can infer to a *nota remota* [remote mark] only through a *nota media* [intermediate mark], through an inference of reason. Hence one should rather treat of inferences, and should take up the three operations of thinking at once, first of all. This was Aristotle's manner, very rigorous. It was Wolff who gave it up.",["'f' before 'alle Menschen' and 'eine notam remotam' is a stray mark and is dropped; 'smd' read 'sind'.","'Es war Wolff, der es abbrachte' rendered 'gave it up' ('broke it off')."]],"dohna-s23-007":["Judgment is the representation of the relation of concepts to one another, whereby a mode of knowledge becomes distinct. It is a great fault in this logic that the author speaks at once of subject and predicate [before, that is, the more necessary preliminary knowledge has been given; it is so in almost every logic]. Our author's definition of judgment fits only categorical judgments. [We have not merely one kind of judgment — ] There are also hypothetical and problematic judgments. [3 relations: 1. of inherence, 2. of dependence, 3. of the parts to one another, which together make up a whole.]",["Stray OCR marks (\"f\") and the hyphenation break in \"unter-einander\" silently removed.","\"Erkenntnis\" in the singular is rendered \"a mode of knowledge\" since \"knowledge\" cannot take the article here.","The long second sentence is kept in one piece, with the bracketed Academy insertion left in place."]],"dohna-s23-008":["All judgments can be brought under 3 kinds: 1. categorical, which contain the relation of the predicate to the subject.",["Stray OCR \"f\" removed; \"1 .\" read as \"1.\"."]],"dohna-s23-009":["2. hypothetical, which contain the relation of ground to consequence. <(If the soul is not composite, then etc.)",["The angle bracket is carried over as printed; its closing mark is absent in the OCR."]],"dohna-s23-010":["[What is not composite is not perishable. The soul is not composite; the soul is not perishable.]",["Kant's example is given as the transcript has it, with the conclusion stated without a connecting word."]],"dohna-s23-011":["3. disjunctive, which contain the relation of a divided concept to the whole sphere of the division <e.g. all men are learned or unlearned). These judgments are of quite another kind, but have this in common, that <they) can always also be expressed categorically (though this might not always succeed).",["Angle brackets kept as printed; the OCR has \"<sie)\" with a mismatched closing mark."]],"dohna-s23-012":["[There are kinds of difference: things are different numerically, specifically, *toto genere*. — Thus we proceeded from below upwards. Learned men are specifically the same, and generically also, and yet numerically different, as C. and J.]",["\"C.\" and \"J.\" are initials for persons not identified in the passage."]],"dohna-s23-013":["We now pass to the",["The passage breaks off; the heading follows in the next passage."]],"dohna-s23-014":["Categorical Judgments",[]],"dohna-s23-015":["Matter in logic is what is given. [Form in logic is the manner of combining and connecting given concepts.] In categorical judgments the subject and the predicate must be given. In all categorical judgments the negation <the sign of denial) must affect the copula (not the predicate); otherwise they are not negative judgments. [*Anima non est* <(copula) *mortalis* would be a negative judgment. *A. est immortalis* <*non mortalis*) is affirmative.] Judgments whose predicates are merely negative [e.g. not m. — that which | lies outside | the sphere | of m. |] are called infinite judgments <*judicia infinita*) [because there is no end to such things as are non-*a*, whereas *a* can be determined].",["\"m.\" is left as in the text (presumably *mortalis*); the | signs are the Academy's apparatus and are kept where they stand.","\"Bestimmung/bestimmen\" in the last clause rendered \"determined\"."]],"dohna-s23-016":["On the Condition of Unconditioned Judgments",["The title is kept as it stands, though \"Bedingung unbedingter Urteile\" may be an error of the transcript for \"bedingter\"; doubtful."]],"dohna-s23-017":["[Every judgment has the condition of truth — the ground why a predicate is attributed to it.] In every judgment there must be something in the subject which brings it about that the predicate is attributed to it, and this is entitled the condition. For example: every body, as an extended being, is divisible. Or another example: all men, as finite beings, are fallible (*faillible*). This again is entitled not a conditioned but a restricted judgment. If this latter [the restriction] drops out, the judgment becomes conditioned. The restriction is entitled the determination of the judgment (*determinatio judicii*). — Sometimes these determinations lie in the concept, sometimes they are added.",["\"im Subjektes\" is a garbled reading; translated as \"in the subject\".","\"hegen\" read as \"liegen\" (OCR slip).","\"[the restriction]\" is supplied to resolve \"dies letztere\"."]],"dohna-s23-018":["We come now to the division of judgments",["The passage breaks off at a heading; the division follows."]],"dohna-s23-019":["1. According to quality, in which respect they are either affirmative or negative (and infinite), e.g. some men are not learned — a negative judgment,",["The OCR braces around \"und unendliche\" are rendered as parentheses."]],"dohna-s23-020":["2. According to quantity all judgments are either",[]],"dohna-s23-021":["a) *universalia*, which are expressed by \"all\",",["\"universaha\" corrected to *universalia* (OCR slip)."]],"dohna-s23-022":["b) *particularia*; instead of this expression, rather plural [*pluralistische*] judgments.",["\"pluralistische\" is Kant's own term as the student records it."]],"dohna-s23-023":["Every predicate can be regarded in itself as *terminus major*. There are general judgments which are expressed not universally but only plurally, e.g. All rational beings are thinking men, some men are rational beings, some men are thinking, etc. There are 3 kinds of judgment according to quantity, namely general, particular and singular. Every *judicium singulare* admits of no exception, because it has no *sphaera*.",["The first example is given as the transcript has it; its sense is doubtful and may be a faulty record.","\"näinlich\" corrected to \"nämlich\"."]],"dohna-s23-024":["| [*Judicium generale*, judgment in general, is different from the universal (*universal*) judgment. It holds where there are only a few exceptions, by which one comes least into danger of erring with this judgment; one can therefore say it *generaliter*. Such judgments can occur only in the empirical.]",["The initial | is the Academy's sign; the stray \"!\" after the closing bracket is dropped as OCR noise.","\"allgemein (universellen)\" is distinguished from \"im allgemeinen\" (*generale*) as the sense requires."]],"dohna-s23-025":["A categorical judgment has either 2 subjects and one predicate, or 2 predicates and one subject, or only one subject and only one predicate.",[]],"dohna-s23-026":["We come",["The passage breaks off; it continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s23-027":["3. to relation; according to it we divide judgments into:",[]],"dohna-s23-028":["a) categorical. The relation of the subject to the predicate.",[]],"dohna-s23-029":["b) hypothetical. The relation of ground to consequence. [ — 2 judgments stand in the relation that the one is ground of the other. If the one, therefore, is true, so is the other. In the hypothetical judgment there can be only 2 propositions; in the disjunctive there can be several.]",["\"es\" in \"so ist es auch das andere\" is taken as the truth of the one judgment carrying over to the other, and rendered accordingly."]],"dohna-s23-030":["c) disjunctive. The relation of the members of the divided concept, of the parts of the sphere, to the whole sphere.",["\"Sphära\" rendered \"sphere\" as elsewhere."]],"dohna-s23-031":["The categorical (absolute) judgment contains only the relation of 2 concepts, whereas the | hypothetical and | disjunctive contain that of two or more judgments. [*Propositio*, or proposition (*Satz*), is entitled an assertoric judgment.]",["Stray OCR \"f\" marks removed; the | signs are kept as the Academy's apparatus."]],"dohna-s23-032":["What is the ground in a *judicium hypotheticum* is entitled the *antecedens*; the connection with the latter is entitled the copula; the inference which then follows (the conclusion from) both judgments is entitled the consequence. The matter of the hypothetical judgment consists not of 2 concepts but of two judgments, whose relation is the consequence. [A hypothetical judgment consists of two problematic judgments — this is its matter. The following of the *consequens* from the *antecedens* is entitled *consequentia*. The hypothetical judgment does nothing but draw the consequence from the two problematic propositions.]",["\"mit dem letzten\" is ambiguous; I take it to mean the consequent, as the sense of the copula requires.","The passage runs from AA 24:765 into 766."]],"dohna-s23-033":["Now an example of a *judicium disjunctivum*: All bodies are either fluid or solid. <If this world is not the best, then | God | either did not know the better, or | did not | wish to create it, or | could | not create it, etc.) [This together makes up the whole *sphaera*.]",["The | signs and angle brackets are kept as printed; the placement of the \"nicht\" marks is the editors' and is carried over as it stands."]],"dohna-s23-034":["4. Modality (determination of the concept of connection). The 3 determinations of the copula [or of the concept of connection] (especially for categorical judgments) are:",[]],"dohna-s23-035":["a) problematic judgments, which contain logical possibility,",[]],"dohna-s23-036":["b) assertoric judgments, which contain logical actuality [truth],",[]],"dohna-s23-037":["c) apodeictic judgments, which contain logical necessity.",[]],"dohna-s23-038":["[Lesson, Friday]",["\"Stunde\" rendered \"Lesson\"; it marks the start of a new lecture hour."]],"dohna-s23-039":["The author says: a *judicium* without modality is a *judicium purum*. A judgment is entitled *exponibile* because a thought or concept is expressed as simple, although it is in fact composite, and indeed composed of an affirmative and a negative [element], e.g. few men are virtuous. [This means: some men are virtuous, many men are not virtuous; thus an affirmative and a negative judgment.] [A composite judgment, where 2 judgments have one predicate.] Judgments that are not <purely> logical are those which [are aesthetic and] merely represent the subjective in relation to a faculty of knowledge. For example, when one gives a description of mountains and in doing so relates not universally valid concepts of their size and situation, but merely the sensations produced thereby in us.",["\"[element]\" is supplied. \"Empfindüngen\" corrected to \"Empfindungen\" (sensations).","\"Erkenntnisvermögen\" rendered \"faculty of knowledge\"."]],"dohna-s23-040":["[Theoretical judgments are those which determine an object as possible. Practical ones designate the actions by which to make it actual.]",["\"es wirklich zu machen\": \"es\" taken to refer to the object."]],"dohna-s23-041":["Practical propositions refer to the actions by which the proposition becomes possible.",[]],"dohna-s23-042":["[An indemonstrable judgment can nevertheless be true. — All immediately certain propositions are indemonstrable.] To demonstrate can have a twofold meaning. —",["\"ünerweisliches\" corrected to \"unerweisliches\" (OCR); rendered \"indemonstrable\"."]],"dohna-s23-043":["An empty judgment [one which contains no ground of any knowledge, such as tautological judgments] is a judgment that contains no concept, which is wholly indistinct if I explain *obscurum per aeque obscurum*. An empty judgment can be manifest or concealed. [Identical judgments are not on that account empty. — All definitions are identical with the concept defined. The identity can also be recognised by analysis.]",["\"welches ganz undeutlich ist\" is ambiguous in its antecedent; I take it to refer to the judgment, and say so by keeping the clause close to the German.","The sentence is awkward in the German and may be a loose record of the lecture."]],"dohna-s23-044":["If the identity is explicit [*identitas explicita*], the judgment is tautological, since a mark is named twice. Not everything that is incapable of proof is an identical proposition; synthetic propositions also belong here. When I have changed an implicit identity [*identitatem implicitam*] into an explicit one, I have performed the analysis.",["OCR 'explieitam' read as 'explicitam'; 'löAnalysis' read as 'Analysis' (the 'lö' is a stray).","'Unerweisliche' rendered 'incapable of proof'."]],"dohna-s23-045":["The author now speaks of judgments incapable of proof. Axioms are propositions incapable of proof (immediately certain). [They are possible only in mathematics.] Even if some of them could be discovered *per analysin*, the mathematician does not wish to proceed so. For this is not his business, since he produces everything through the construction of concepts in intuition. [Axioms are always synthetic propositions, namely such as can be exhibited in an intuition (through) *intuitum purum*. The analytic propositions are [thereby | not identical.]]",["OCR 'smd' read as 'sind', 'anatytischen' as 'analytischen'; 'Ließ' read as 'Ließe' would be expected (subjunctive), but I have followed the text as 'Even if ... could be'.","The final bracketed sentence is garbled ('[ damit | nicht identisch.]'); I translate what can be made out and keep the marks. The sense is doubtful, and the brackets do not close evenly in the source."]],"dohna-s23-046":["A postulate is a practical proposition about what ought to be done, without showing the manner of doing it, because it assumes that everyone can do what it recommends as an imperative. [For in logic what matters is never the matter, but always the form.]",["'Imperativen' is odd (dative plural); rendered 'as an imperative'."]],"dohna-s23-047":["Judgments are either intuitive (in so far as they concern an object that can be presented in intuition) or discursive (whose object [Gegenstand] is presented through a concept).",["'dessen Gegenstand' is loosely attached; I supply 'whose object' as the parallel to the first clause."]],"dohna-s23-048":["A judgment of perception is intuitive (not discursive). An empirical judgment is intuitive, but experience is already discursive. Judgments of experience are always discursive, because we always connect perceptions with them. It is a *vitium subreptionis* when what is only a judgment of perception is taken for a judgment of experience.",["The transcript's contrast between 'empirical judgment' and 'experience' is as it stands; it may be a slip of the note-taker, but I have not altered it."]],"dohna-s23-049":["A theorem is a theoretical proposition, in so far as it is capable of proof. [An inference that one draws by a syllogism: *corollarium*, *consectarium*.] A problem [Aufgabe] is a practical proposition, in which one must assume that what is commanded to be done [can be done].",["OCR 'Mas' read as 'Was'. The clause 'was zu tun geboten wird' is elliptical; the bracketed 'can be done' is supplied from the sense (cf. the postulate)."]],"dohna-s24-001":["[Hour, Monday]",[]],"dohna-s24-002":["An axiom is a proposition that comes immediately from the definition. It really presupposes intuition, or at all events immediate certainty *a priori*. The practical proposition that requires and admits of a solution is entitled *quaestio problematis* [the proposition that determines the action that is to take place]. The representation of the way in which the object is brought about is entitled *resolutio*. The proof that, if one has proceeded in this way, the result follows is *demonstratio*. A postulate admits of neither a resolution nor a demonstration. The solution of the problem is complete when everything is contained in it, when the relation is completely resolved. This is not always possible in mathematics, but one can approach it as closely as one will, for example in the measurement of the diameter of the earth. Exactness: precision presupposes that it [the solution] is complete, containing neither too little nor too much.",["'Resolution/Auflösung' and 'solution' both render Auflösung/Resolution as the sense requires; *resolutio* kept in Latin.","OCR '■' after 'vollständig' is an illegible mark; omitted. 'auf gelöset' read as 'aufgelöset'.","'[the solution]' supplied to resolve the pronoun 'es'."]],"dohna-s24-003":["In solutions in the practical sphere one is not so very exact, and is content if one has the means, whose force one prefers to assume too strong rather than too weak. [For example, levers, wheels: better too much force than too little. Test: bringing back to the given what one has analysed.]",["OCR 'sein*' read as 'sehr'."]],"dohna-s24-004":["*Qualitas occulta* is a method usual in scholastic philosophy, whereby the name of the effect of the cause was taken for insight into the cause itself. [The tautology in the determination of the cause, for example instinct, *horror vacui*, idiosyncrasy, and so on; one is often deceived by it. Nothing is the cause of itself; the cause must always be something other than the *causatum*, the effect.] *Entelechy*: the name of a cause unknown to us.",["OCR 'kvrsXsyeia' is garbled Greek; read as 'entelechy' (ἐντελέχεια) from the sense, a doubtful reading.","'Kausatum' kept as *causatum*."]],"dohna-s24-005":["We are still concerned with the naming of certain propositions in a system [and indeed in the dogmatic system]. A corollary is the proposition that can be inferred from another proposition by a single inference. Corollaries may be theoretical or practical. The author often presupposes knowledge in his readers, and makes corollaries which for beginners would still be theorems. This is always relative to capacities. Wolff translated *Corollarium* as 'Zusatz' (better, 'Folgerung') and *Scholion* as 'Anmerkung'. *Scholion* is a resting-place; by it one understands what is regarded not as a constituent of a system but as an explanation. Scholia may also contain the utility, the reality and the history of the proposition, who first stated it. Finally there occurs here the name *lemma*, a borrowed judgment [Lehnurteil] [where one takes something from another system]; it does not belong to the territory of the science: *est peregrinum aliquid* [an episode].",["OCR 'Fälligkeiten' read as 'Fähigkeiten'; 'Wolf f' as 'Wolff'.","'Lehnurteil' literally a 'borrowed' or 'loan' judgment."]],"dohna-s24-006":["Immediate inferences (*consequentiae immediatae*) are the inferences of one proposition from another, the two being distinguished from each other not in matter but only (merely) in form. They have no middle term [*medius terminus*]. Immediate inferences are such inferences as can be changed into syllogisms only by tautological subsumptions. [For example: All men are mortal, therefore some mortals are men; this is a *consequentia immediata*. Syllogism: All men are mortal, is the major; *atqui* some mortals are mortal, this is the minor, a tautological subsumption; *ergo* some mortals are men, the conclusion. Since tautological propositions are empty, one omits the minor and the conclusion follows immediately.] Tautological judgments may be regarded as empty, because they contribute nothing at all to distinctness, for example: All men are mortal, some men are men (tautological), some men are mortal.",["OCR 'Mensehen' read as 'Menschen'; the '<' before '(bloß)' is an apparatus mark, carried over as '(merely)' without it.","The example's minor is reproduced as in the text ('some mortals are mortal'); the transcript's logic is loose."]],"dohna-s24-007":["According to our author, the first *consequentia immediata* is to be entitled:",[]],"dohna-s24-008":["1. *Per judicia aequipollentia*. I infer from one judgment to another that signifies precisely the same. The ground must always be distinguished from the consequence.",[]],"dohna-s24-009":["Now we will go through the four *consequentiae immediatae*. The first is *per judicia subalternata*, in which I infer from the universal to the particular [*quoad quantitatem*].",["'quantitate' in the OCR corrected to *quantitatem*."]],"dohna-s24-010":["The second is *per judicia opposita*, in which I infer from | one of | two judgments opposed to one another to the other.",["The marks '|' are carried over as printed; the text here is perhaps disordered."]],"dohna-s24-011":["3. *Per conversionem judicii*, in which I reverse the terms of the judgment, [altering] only the relation, not the quality.",["'[altering]' supplied; the German has no verb. The statement 'only the relation, not the quality' is as the transcript has it (one would expect 'quantity' or 'relation' otherwise); not altered."]],"dohna-s24-012":["4. *Per contrapositionem*, in which, again, it is not the quantity that is changed but only the quality. What was predicate in the first proposition now becomes subject, but the quantity, the affirmative and the negative, remains. (*Metathesis*.) [In every affirmative judgment I can contrapose; one need only reverse.]",["The passage is as transcribed; 'wieder nun' is awkward and rendered 'again'."]],"dohna-s24-013":["We (will) now go through the individual consequences.",[]],"dohna-s24-014":["1. *Per judicia subalternata*: what belongs to all belongs to some; for example, all men are mortal, consequently some men are mortal.",[]],"dohna-s24-015":["2. All oppositions in logic are of three different kinds. They are:",[]],"dohna-s24-016":["1. Judgments opposed to one another *contradictorie* [contain everything that is required",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-017":["for conflict] or less than is required for conflict is contained in the judgments",["As printed the sentence is awkwardly split; the sense is that the *contradictorie opposita* contain exactly what conflict requires, while the next two kinds contain less or more."]],"dohna-s24-018":["2. *subcontrarie opposita* [hence they can both be true] and",[]],"dohna-s24-019":["3. *contrarie opposita* [contain more than belongs to it, hence both can",[]],"dohna-s24-020":["be false].",[]],"dohna-s24-021":["Now we turn to their rules.",[]],"dohna-s24-022":["The rule of the first: No proposition is opposed *contradictorie* to another, except the universal affirmative to the particular negative.",["The transcript omits the corresponding pair, universal negative and particular affirmative; not supplied."]],"dohna-s24-023":["Certain signs were adopted: the universal affirmative with *a*, the universal negative *e*, the particular affirmative *i*, the particular negative *o*. One expresses this thus:",[]],"dohna-s24-024":["*Asserit a, negat e, sed universaliter ambo* —",["Latin mnemonic kept untranslated; 'a affirms, e denies, but both universally'."]],"dohna-s24-025":["*Asserit i, negat o, sed particulariter ambo.* —",["'i affirms, o denies, but both particularly'."]],"dohna-s24-026":["If one of two contradictory judgments is true, the other is false, and conversely.",[]],"dohna-s24-027":["We come",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-028":["2. to the judgments *subcontrarie oppositae*. Particular judgments are opposed to one another *subcontrarie*. The",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-029":["3. judgments *contrarie oppositae* are those in which two universal judgments are opposed to one another, as *a* and *e*.",[]],"dohna-s24-030":["In universal judgments one says more than is necessary for opposition. For opposition nothing more is required than that *a* and *o*, *e* and *i*, are in conflict.",[]],"dohna-s24-031":["3. *Conversio judicii* consists in inferring one judgment from another by *metathesin terminorum* [in that what was previously subject is made predicate], while the quality of the judgment is retained. The *judicium conversum* is the one given; the *convertens* must be inferred from it by an immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*). Simple conversion (*conversio simplex*) is that in which the quantity remains the same. The concept of the subject is regarded as standing under the predicate, as a *locus geometricus*. [Hence these propositions cannot be converted simply, since that would bring the wider [*latiorem*] under the narrower [*angustiorem*].] Universal affirmative judgments can properly be converted only *per accidens*; particular affirmative judgments, however, can be converted simply (*simpliciter*). All negative judgments can be converted *simpliciter*; the opposition is always reciprocal. All universal affirmative [judgments], and so on. Contraposition [takes place] through *metathesin*.",["OCR slips corrected: 'conversio simplex f' (stray mark dropped), 'simphciter' read as *simpliciter*.","The bracketed sentence 'Hence these propositions...' follows the text as printed; the antecedent of 'diese Sätze' (presumably universal affirmatives) is not stated in the passage, and I have left it so.","'Alle allgemein bejahenden usw.' is a broken-off note in the transcript; translated as it stands, with '[judgments]' supplied. 'Contraposition durch metathesin' is elliptical; '[takes place]' supplied."]],"dohna-s24-032":["[NB. Tuesday, the 17th. Here Kant took a vacation of more than 4 weeks.]",["OCR '•4' read as '4'; the numeral is slightly doubtful."]],"dohna-s24-033":["[Monday, the 20th of August]",[]],"dohna-s24-034":["An inference of the understanding (*Verstandesschluß*) is the following of one judgment from another, *absque judicio intermedio* [without an intermediate judgment]. An inference of reason (*Vernunftschluß*) is the knowledge of the necessity of a proposition through the subsumption of its condition under a universal rule. This is what is entitled *judicium intermedium*.",["Verstandesschluß / Vernunftschluß rendered 'inference of the understanding / of reason'; 'Das heißt' rendered 'is what is entitled'."]],"dohna-s24-035":["[An inference of reason is the following of one inference of the understanding from another through a *judicium intermedium*. Inferences of the understanding proceed from the universal to the particular; inferences of the power of judgment (*Urteilskraft*) from the particular to the universal. The latter substitutes: *atqui*, this is the case [falling] under the universal rule. Its inferences are never strict, but only an attempt *in subsidium*, as are analogy and induction.]",["'Sie substituiert' is ambiguous; 'Sie' taken as Urteilskraft, and I have rendered it 'The latter'. The OCR space before the colon was dropped.","'[falling]' is supplied to complete 'das ist der Fall von der allgemeinen Regel'."]],"dohna-s24-036":["'Everything extended is divisible; consequently bodies are divisible' is not an immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*). In a universal inference of reason it is necessary, first, that the proposition be brought under a universal rule, and, secondly, that the condition be subsumed under the universal rule. For example: Everything extended is divisible, *atqui* all bodies are (extended), *ergo* all bodies are divisible. Every inference of reason consists of three propositions:",["'Bedingimg' corrected to 'Bedingung'. The round brackets around 'ausgedehnt' appear in the source (OCR braces) and are rendered as parentheses.","The first sentence is restated with quotation marks for clarity; the German is a bare string of clauses."]],"dohna-s24-037":["1. the universal rule, that is, the major premiss, *major propositio*,",[]],"dohna-s24-038":["2. the condition of the subsumption of a certain proposition under a universal rule, the *minor*,",[]],"dohna-s24-039":["3. the proposition which is to be understood through subsumption under a universal rule, the *conclusio*.",["'verstanden werden' is retained literally, though 'erkannt' might have been expected; it may be a slip in the transcript."]],"dohna-s24-040":["Analogy and induction are inferences of the power of judgment. In none of these inferences can one ever infer from the particular to the universal (with certainty). An inference of (induction) is that in which, from some things belonging to a certain genus, I infer, with regard to all things of the genus, that it belongs to all the rest. [To know how one is to look for the diversity, how one reflects, cannot be taught. —]",["The sentence on induction is irregular as it stands; I have kept its sense but smoothed it. 'Ein Schluß der (Induktion)' is rendered literally, with the parentheses retained.","'Bei allen Schlüssen' is probably meant as 'in all such inferences'; so rendered."]],"dohna-s24-041":["According to the inference of analogy: if two things agree in as many [determinations (*Bestimmungen*)] as I have come to know, I infer that they agree in the other determinations also. I infer, then, from some determinations which I know, that the other determinations also belong to the thing. It is the inference of a provisional judgment; one reserves the right to alter it. [The inference by analogy that animals have a soul — Why . . . then? If wasps have drunk of forbidden honey and now want to sting, they fall; but they have . . . faculty of desire (*Begehrungsvermögen*). This applies also to plants, the fly-trap leaf. They seek air and sun and water.]",["The apparatus signs | and the stray 'f' marks in the OCR are dropped or kept only as the brackets stand; the bracketed words after 'so vielen' are uncertain because the OCR is garbled.","The ellipses ('. . .') are in the transcript; the passage on wasps is evidently a fragmentary note and is not completed. 'Fliegenfangblatt' is read from the OCR 'Fliege nfangblatt'; the exact plant is not named."]],"dohna-s24-042":["No logician has yet worked out analogy and induction properly. This field is still open. A judgment which settles what is true or false is entitled determinant (*bestimmend*) . . .",["The passage breaks off with an ellipsis in the source; it is translated as it stands. *bestimmend* is the term later contrasted with *reflektierend*, but that contrast is not in this passage."]],"dohna-s24-043":["There are three kinds of inferences of reason: categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive. In the first there are only three *termini*. The ground of this lies in the fact that in all inferences of reason a rule lies at the basis, under which one subsumes. The third is the application, the *conclusio*. In every inference of reason we have matter and form. The former consists in the premisses [the premisses are called the matter of the inference of reason], the latter in the consequence. Logic has no concern at all with falsity *quoad materiam*. Even if one has inferred correctly from false premisses, the conclusion is nevertheless correct. No rational being is infallible. Man is a rational being, therefore no man is infallible. The conclusion is correct, the premisses false. For logic only the arguing, and the *error aut veritas ratiocinii quoad formam*, belong.",["OCR corrected: 'lvypothetische' to 'hypothetische', 'Hegt' to 'liegt', 'kern' to 'kein', 'ratiocinn' to *ratiocinii*.","'Die Conclusion ist richtig' is Kant's (the student's) own statement, and is translated as it stands, though it is logically odd, since the conclusion is true only by coincidence; the point is that the inference is formally valid.","'Das 3te ist die Anwendung' follows an incomplete enumeration; the intervening two *termini* are not named in the passage."]],"dohna-s24-044":["The *major propositio* is always an assertoric proposition. [The *major propositio* contains the rule, the *minor* the subsumption, the *conclusio* the consequence. An inference of reason is the consciousness of the necessity of a proposition through subsumption under a universal rule. —]",[]],"dohna-s24-045":["Each of these inferences of reason has its own special principle. Categorical inferences of reason infer according to the *dictum de omni et nullo*. Hypothetical inferences of reason [infer] according to the rule *a ratione ad rationatum valet consequentia*, or *a negatione rationati* (*posita ratione ponitur rationatum*).",["OCR 'schheßen' and 'nuUo' corrected to 'schließen' and *nullo*. '[infer]' supplied. The Latin rule is left untranslated as a technical formula."]],"dohna-s24-046":["The *dictum de omni et nullo* is the consequence of the *principium contradictionis* [the principle of contradiction is the principle of judgments, not of inferences of reason]. It runs:",[]],"dohna-s24-047":["*Quicquid valet de genere, valet etiam de omnibus sub genere aut specie* — *quicquid non valet de genere aut specie, non valet etiam de omnibus sub isto genere aut ista specie contentis.*",["Latin left as in the source."]],"dohna-s24-048":["I can infer in this way, for the concept of the whole contains the marks (*Merkmale*) of the individual. Kant expresses this:",["The statement that Kant 'expresses this' is the note-taker's; kept as is."]],"dohna-s24-049":["*Nota notae est nota rei ipsius* (the universal principle of all affirmative inferences) — *Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi* (the principle of all negative inferences).",["OCR angle brackets stand for parentheses; rendered as parentheses."]],"dohna-s24-050":["[For example: All bodies are changeable. I seek a *nota intermedia*, 'divisible', a nearer mark of all bodies. 'Changeable' is a mark of 'divisible', 'divisible' a mark of 'body'; hence 'changeable' is a mark of 'body'.",["The opening bracket is left open, as in the source; the closing bracket appears at dohna-s24-056."]],"dohna-s24-051":["divisible",["Part of a diagram; the layout is lost."]],"dohna-s24-052":["Body — changeable",["Part of a diagram."]],"dohna-s24-053":["divisible — changeable",["Part of a diagram."]],"dohna-s24-054":["Body — divisible",["Part of a diagram."]],"dohna-s24-055":["— — changeable, according to the principle of all affirmative inferences.",["Part of a diagram."]],"dohna-s24-056":["Inferences of reason therefore consist in this, that we compare the *nota notae* with the *nota rei ipsius* by means of a *nota intermedia*.]",[]],"dohna-s24-057":["In disjunctive propositions the *membra disjunctionis*, however many there may be, must be regarded as one member (according to the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*), for in contradictory judgments there can be only two propositions, *a* and *non a*.",["'Prinzipium' as printed."]],"dohna-s24-058":["We come to the doctrine",["Breaks off; continues in the next heading."]],"dohna-s24-059":["Of Categorical Inferences of Reason",[]],"dohna-s24-060":["They concern solely categorical propositions. — The *terminus major* is the predicate, the *minor* the subject, and [in addition] the *medius*. There can be only 4 conclusions. The *terminus medius* is in the *major* [*propositione*] in the place of the subject, and in the *minor* in the place of the predicate. Every *conclusio* consists of a subject and a predicate. The 4 different syllogistic figures we shall set out here.",["The OCR bracket '[ dazu |' is garbled; 'dazu' is read as 'in addition'. 'Es kann nur 4 Conclusionen geben' is as in the source (presumably the four forms A, E, I, O). The *medius* rule as given is true of the first figure only; it is reported as it stands."]],"dohna-s24-062":["[One] must represent this to oneself as a chain of 3 links.",["The beginning is lost; 'muß' lacks its subject, and '[One]' is supplied. The passage is a fragment from a gap in the text."]],"dohna-s24-063":["— Bodies are divisible.",["Fragment of an example."]],"dohna-s24-064":["The soul is not divisible.",["Fragment of an example."]],"dohna-s24-065":["corporeal.",["Fragment of an example."]],"dohna-s24-066":["The major is the rule. The minor is always affirmative. For I say",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-067":["this is contained under the condition.] In the 2nd figure one always finds a negative conclusion.",["The closing bracket matches an opening bracket lost in the gap."]],"dohna-s24-068":["in it one never infers immediately, but one must",["The beginning of the sentence is lost."]],"dohna-s24-069":["insert an immediate conclusion (*conclusio immediata*).",["Continues the previous passage; the figure concerned is not identified here."]],"dohna-s24-070":["The *major propositio* must always (in all categorical inferences of reason) be universal.",[]],"dohna-s24-071":["In the 3rd figure we can have only a particular conclusion.",[]],"dohna-s24-072":["The 4th figure never has a universal affirmative or universal",["Continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-073":["negative conclusion. The inference of the 2nd, 3rd and 4th figures",["The stray '15' is a line number and is omitted."]],"dohna-s24-074":["must in thought always be brought into the first.",[]],"dohna-s24-075":["[Only the inferences according to the first figure are categorical. The others",[]],"dohna-s24-076":["are all *ratiocinia impura*. — For the predicate of the *conclusio* must",[]],"dohna-s24-077":["be related to the *terminus medius* in the categorical inference of reason. *Ratiocinia hybrida*, where besides the premisses and the *conclusio* [there is]",["The stray '20' is a line number and is omitted. '[there is]' supplied to link with the next line."]],"dohna-s24-078":["still an immediate inference (*consequentia immediata*). —]",[]],"dohna-s24-079":["We must now go through the universal rules of all inferences of reason",["'Vernunft Schlüsse' read as one word."]],"dohna-s24-080":["[we must] go through.",["OCR 'clurchgehn' read as 'durchgehn'. The line repeats the verb of the previous passage; '[we must]' is supplied."]],"dohna-s24-081":["In every inference of reason there can be only three *termini*. If a *terminus* is taken in two meanings, the inference in fact has four *termini*. Hence the school expression: the *terminus* goes on four feet. The *terminus medius* must lie in the premisses.",["OCR \"4terniini\" read as \"4 termini\"."]],"dohna-s24-082":["Now come some errors which are easily committed:",[]],"dohna-s24-083":["1. *Ex puris negativis <(praemissis> nil sequitur* [for the minor proposition must be affirmative,]",["The angle brackets are the Academy apparatus and are carried over as printed, including the OCR-garbled \"<(\". The square bracket is Academy apparatus and is kept."]],"dohna-s24-084":["2. *Ex puris particularibus nil sequitur* [follows from the concept of an inference of reason. At least the major must be universal.]",[]],"dohna-s24-085":["3. *Conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*; that is to say: one of the premisses is entitled *pars debilior* if it is negative or particular; then the *conclusio* too is particular or negative. [ — Only the minor can be particular. — ]",[]],"dohna-s24-086":["Negation, after all, is opposition, contradiction.",[]],"dohna-s24-087":["We now come to the special rules of inferences of reason of the first figure. They are entitled",["Breaks off; the rules follow in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-088":["*Major sit universalis, minor affirmans* — this holds of all categorical inferences. Next, further, *Minor sit affirmans*, likewise for all. The minor proposition, after all, is to express that the condition applies to this or that [subject]. All inferences in the remaining figures are not *ratiocinia pura* but *hybrida*, that is, inferences which really do not consist of three propositions, but into which a fourth has been mixed *per consequentiam immediatam*. [Another *terminus* in the *ratiocinium* is called the fourth foot.] It is a mere artifice to produce a correct inference nevertheless by transposing the *terminus medius*. [The transposition, *metathesis terminorum*, is twofold: *per contrapositionem*, *per conversionem*. The *conclusio* in the three remaining figures is never universal affirmative; after the first [figure] one has all four kinds (universal <and) particular, affirmative and negative). This already shows that those [figures] go on stilts.]",["The relative clause \"die eigentlich nicht aus 3 Propositionen, sondern wo ... eine 4te eingemischt ist\" is irregular; I have smoothed it and supplied \"[subject]\" for the dative \"diesem oder jenem\".","Apparatus signs \"<u.)\" kept as printed (OCR)."]],"dohna-s24-089":["In the second figure, *Major <(sit)> universalis, conclusio negativa*.",["OCR \"iiniversalis\" corrected to \"universalis\"."]],"dohna-s24-090":["In the third figure, *Minor* (because it is subsumption) *affirmans, conclusio particularis*. [The alteration takes place in the *minor propositio*. If this was previously universal <or particular) affirmative, as it always is, it can be converted only *per accidens*; had it been particular affirmative, then, *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*, and so the conclusion too is particular.]",["\"er\" refers to the proposition (Satz), which I render \"it\". The words \"as it always is\" are doubtful in sense (\"wie immer\"), and I translate them literally."]],"dohna-s24-091":["In the fourth figure: *Conclusio vel negativa vel particularis*.",[]],"dohna-s24-092":["a a e",["Presumably a mnemonic or diagram fragment (the vowels of a mode), translated as it stands."]],"dohna-s24-094":["Reusch's *Logic*. The conclusion of the second figure is negative, because the syllogism cannot be turned into the first figure. In the third figure a *metathesis terminorum* must be carried out with the *minor propositio*. A universal affirmative proposition can be converted only *per accidens*. The inference in the first figure is the only pure one. (*Ars non habet osorem nisi ignorantem* [Art has no enemy but the ignorant].)",["The Latin gloss in square brackets is mine, supplied to complete the sense."]],"dohna-s24-095":["The *modus* of a proposition is its quality and quantity.",[]],"dohna-s24-097":["p. 110]. One spells it thus: Cel — ar — ent. In the syl-",["A fragment that breaks off mid-word (\"Sil-\", presumably Silbe/syllables). Translated as it stands; the mnemonic *Celarent* is spelled out in syllables."]],"dohna-s24-099":["The *modi* all come out at 64 different kinds",["Breaks off without a verb or full stop."]],"dohna-s24-101":["From each of these there are 16 different inferences. But 28 drop out according to the rule: *ex puris negativis nihil sequitur*. 18 drop out according to the rule: *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*. 8 drop out according to the rule that a negative conclusion cannot follow from merely affirmative [premisses]. There remain no more than 10 *modi*, of which only 8 are *modi utiles*. —",["A stray mark (\"— — '\") in the OCR is omitted. \"premisses\" is supplied in square brackets. The figures are as printed in the transcript."]],"dohna-s24-102":["The hypothetical inference of reason is that in which the major proposition is a conditioned proposition. The *modus ponens* infers from the ground to the consequence. The *modus tollens* infers from the consequence to the ground. It cannot happen that I infer *a consequente sublato ad antecedentem*",["Breaks off without a full stop. \"Grund\" is rendered \"ground\", \"Folge\" \"consequence\"."]],"dohna-s24-103":["The dilemma is a hypothetical (judgment) whose consequent is a disjunctive judgment. [E.g. if this world is not the best, then God either knew no better, or was unable or unwilling to create a better.]",["\"(Urteil)\" is bracketed as in the source. \"Konsequenz\" is rendered \"consequent\"."]],"dohna-s24-104":["A formal inference of reason, then, is an enunciation in which something is expressed *explicite*. To the *ratiocinia cryptica* belongs the enthymeme. [E.g. all animals are mortal, consequently all men also. The *terminus medius* is lacking.]",[]],"dohna-s24-105":["An inference of reason is cryptic *per transmissionem praemissarum* or *per omissionem praemissarum*.",[]],"dohna-s24-106":["The *syllogismus contractus* is when I infer through the *terminus medius*.",["OCR \"terminuni\" read as \"terminum\". The sentence is brief and obscure in the transcript."]],"dohna-s24-107":["All inferences of reason are to yield necessity in their conclusion. If two things which stand under the same genus agree in all the respects that I know, I infer that this holds of the others also. I infer from the identity of some properties to the identity of all. An inference can never produce a mode of knowledge [Erkenntnis] from empirical propositions. [Inferences from induction and analogy are only crutches of human reason.] We can infer only from universal premisses. Yet no empirical proposition gives us *universitatem simpliciter*, but only *secundum quid*, so far as we know it. The inference | when I | infer from some predicates of things to all the rest is an inference according to analogy. E.g. Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus are opaque planets. Now I infer by induction that the remaining planets, which perhaps may still be discovered, may also be opaque. *Ob paritatem rationis* is also the name given to the inference according to induction.",["The sentence \"Der Schluß, | wenn ich | von einigen Prädikaten ...\" is irregular in the German; I have smoothed it, keeping the apparatus bars. \"Erkenntnis\" here is a single instance and could stand as \"knowledge\".","The transcript calls the planet example both analogy and induction; I have kept both as written."]],"dohna-s24-108":["*Exempla illustrant, non probant* [examples illustrate, they do not prove]. Hence there is no inference by example.",["English gloss in square brackets supplied by me."]],"dohna-s24-109":["An inference of reason which is false in <matter> is entitled a fallacy (*Trugschluß*), and if it is false in <*forma*> — a paralogism. A *sophisma* is an inference false in *forma*. [The word \"sophist\" has not always had the bad meaning of quibbler (*Vernünftler*).]",["OCR brackets around \"Materie\" and \"forma\" are doubtful (probably Academy angle brackets); rendered as <...>. The text as printed seems to give *forma* for both paralogism and sophisma, as in the transcript."]],"dohna-s24-110":["Of many false inferences it is believed that they are so in *forma*, but very often they are so in *materia*. The old Megarian school, founded by Euclid of Megara (not the geometer), occupied itself a great deal with such inferences, and turned many of them neatly.",["\"wendete viele artig\" appears incomplete (perhaps \"an\" or an object is missing); translated literally."]],"dohna-s24-111":["*Achilles, Cretensis, Acervus.* [See Darjes, *Logic*.]",["\"Daries\" in the source is Darjes; the three names are the Achilles (and tortoise), the Cretan (liar) and the heap."]],"dohna-s24-112":["[If you say that you are lying, and in saying this speak the truth, then you speak the truth, [?] you lie — is false *in materia*: in that you say that you are lying. —",["The words \"meiern du lügest\" are garbled OCR (perhaps \"indem\" or \"wenn\"), and the sense is uncertain; I mark the gap with [?] and do not fill it. The bracket opens here and is not closed in this passage, as in the source."]],"dohna-s24-113":["Epimenides says: All Cretans are liars. *Ipse vero est Cretensis — ergo ipse est mendax* [he himself, however, is a Cretan; therefore he himself is a liar], so his assertion too would be a lie.]",["The English gloss of the Latin is mine. The closing bracket belongs to the bracket opened in the previous passage."]],"dohna-s24-114":["One keeps adding more: how many grains belong to the heap? In gradual increase there is no transition to another genus. [For concepts of comparative magnitude there is no specific difference at any determinate place. This serves chiefly in morals to warn against defects of the mean (of mediocrity). With things that are specifically different, one should beware of making a difference according to degree.]",["\"Defekten des medii\" is rendered \"defects of the mean\"; the sense is the moral point against treating a difference of kind as one of degree."]],"dohna-s24-115":["*Cornutus.* *Quicquid non amisisti, habes. Cornua non amisisti. Ergo habes.* [Whatever you have not lost, you have. You have not lost horns. Therefore you have them.]",["English gloss in brackets is mine."]],"dohna-s24-116":["*Calvus.* How many hairs gone make a bald head? — I can define nothing by the more or less. —",[]],"dohna-s24-117":["The *sophisma heterozeteseos*, when it concerns a disputed question, is entitled *sophisma ignorationis elenchi*. — E.g. the proof of the immortality of the soul — one proves a future life.",["OCR \"Sophisrna\" and \"elenehi\" corrected to *sophisma* and *elenchi*."]],"dohna-s24-118":["*Ratiocinia* can also, so far as they are subordinated to one another, be *composita*.",[]],"dohna-s24-119":["The *ratiocinatio episyllogistica* occurs when the conclusion of one syllogism is the premiss of a given one. In all academic disputations one infers *per prosyllogismos*.",["OCR \"Conchision\" corrected to \"Conclusio\"; a stray quotation mark is dropped."]],"dohna-s24-120":["[Sorites, a series of inferences of reason in episyllogistic order.] Some explanations are still to be added here:",[]],"dohna-s24-121":["A *saltus in probando* [leap in proof] occurs when one connects a ground with a remote consequence without an intermediate proposition. — [One may leap legitimately when the intermediate proposition is self-evident. — ]",[]],"dohna-s24-122":["A *circulus in probando* occurs when one cites in the ground of proof, as known, that which is to be proved.",["OCR \"Averden\" corrected to \"werden\"."]],"dohna-s24-123":["*Petitio principii* — to assume gratuitously. —",[]],"dohna-s24-124":["Of proofs that are faulty one says that they say too much or too little; the former is a *vitium*, the latter a *defectus*. He who proves too much proves nothing at all. Just so, he who says less than the whole truth does not lie, but he who says more than the truth certainly lies.",["\"Vitium\" is kept in Latin, with \"fault\" implied."]],"dohna-s24-125":["[Topics (*Topik*) is the science which shows how logical places are to be determined.] Topics is the art of bringing one's concepts under certain principal concepts (heads or compartments) | : *generalia capita argumentorum aut genera in quibus reperiuntur*. A particular maxim is entitled a *locus topicus*, or, since that is tautological, a *locus communis*. Such, for instance, is a canon like: 'What the young are used to, the old will do.' There is a *locus grammaticus*, e.g. etymology; a *locus logicus*, such as the *dictum de omni et nullo*, the inference *a toto ad partes*, and so forth. There is a *locus metaphysicus* and a *locus ethicus*, according as I derive something from the useful or from the honourable (*utile*, *honestum*). *Argumentum ab utili*, and so on. There is a *locus physicus*, drawn from the purposes of nature. Of the *locus*",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Von dem locus'); continued in the next passage.","'jung gewohnt, alt getan' is a proverb; rendered freely, as the sense rather than the words is what the example needs.","Restated the string 'utili oder honesto' with the nominative forms in parentheses for clarity; this is my addition.","'Örter' rendered 'places' to keep the link with *locus*."]],"dohna-s24-126":["*geometricus* the",["Fragment; the sentence began in the previous passage and continues in the next ('Von dem locus geometricus mag wohl eigentlich die Benennung hergekommen sein')."]],"dohna-s24-127":["name may properly have come, that is,",["Fragment of a sentence continued in the following passage."]],"dohna-s24-128":["when a problem can be solved in infinitely many different ways.",[]],"dohna-s24-129":["[Parallel; the *locus geometricus* of the",["Continues in the next passage."]],"dohna-s24-130":["equal triangles upon one base.]",["Taken with the preceding: the locus of the apexes of equal triangles on a common base is a line parallel to the base."]],"dohna-s24-131":["But it is impossible for philosophy to put itself on a level with mathematics in this respect.",[]],"dohna-s24-132":["In accordance with our division we pass to the last part of logic, which may be regarded as a brief appendix:",[]],"dohna-s24-133":["II. The Doctrine of Method",["*Methodenlehre*."]],"dohna-s24-134":["It is also entitled the practical part. Strictly, however, there is no practical logic, for logic is not an organon but only a canon. This appendix serves for the criticism (*Kritik*) of knowledge.",["*Kritik der Erkenntnis*: 'criticism' chosen as the context suits."]],"dohna-s24-135":["The doctrine of method is the prescription for the possibility of a system of the knowledge of understanding and of reason. It is therefore the doctrine of method [*methodus*: the way in which a mode of knowledge can acquire scientific form]. Method is the connection of thoughts. We can think it in two ways: as *methodus logica* [not merely a way of teaching but a way of thinking], which might be called manner (*Manier*), and which rests wholly on rules; the *methodus aesthetica* rests solely on taste and cannot be brought under any rules.",["'Verstandes- und Vernunfterkenntnisses' rendered 'knowledge of understanding and of reason'.","Singular *Erkenntnis* in the bracket rendered 'a mode of knowledge' to avoid the article with 'knowledge'."]],"dohna-s24-136":["The word *methodus* is not adequately translated by 'way of teaching' (*Lehrart*), for there is just as much a method as a way of thinking (*Denkart*) as there is as a way of teaching.",["The transcript has 'Denk- |wie| als Lehrart'; the apparatus sign is carried over in sense only, and the reading 'wie' is editorial; I have not reproduced it as such in the English, as the sentence is smoothed."]],"dohna-s25-001":["[Manner of presentation (*Vortrag*)",["Opening of a bracketed list continued in the next passages."]],"dohna-s25-002":["1. systematic,",[]],"dohna-s25-003":["2. fragmentary,",[]],"dohna-s25-004":["1. methodical,",[]],"dohna-s25-005":["2. tumultuary.]",["*tumultuarisch* rendered 'tumultuary' (haphazard, unordered)."]],"dohna-s25-006":["Now the different methods come up.",["'ran' is colloquial for 'heran'."]],"dohna-s25-007":["The first division is into synthetic and analytic. The latter is that in which I pass from the consequences to the grounds, the former that in which I pass from the grounds to the consequences. [A division is unsystematic if one cannot see from it why there might not be still more members of the division.] In philosophising one can proceed both synthetically and analytically. The mathematical way of teaching is the synthetic method. It is distinguished from all others in that it demonstrates through intuition. A way of thinking (*Denkungsart*) is always something moral. Yet one might say that *Denkart* would be the *methodus*. Method is",["Passage breaks off ('Die Methode ist'); continued in the next passages.","*Denkungsart* (disposition, cast of mind) is distinguished from *Denkart* (mode of thinking); the transcript is elliptical, and I have left the point as it stands.","*Anschauung* = intuition."]],"dohna-s25-008":["1. popular: this is for the common understanding, *in concreto*.",[]],"dohna-s25-009":["2. scholastic: belongs to the sciences, *in abstracto*.",[]],"dohna-s25-010":["To orient oneself means to put oneself at a certain standpoint from which one can conveniently consider things *in concreto* [OCR 'f' after *concreto* is a stray mark and is omitted].",["Stray 'f' in the OCR dropped; I have placed the remark in brackets, which is my own addition and should be removed if a clean text is wanted."]],"dohna-s25-011":["*Methodus syllogistica*. Many have even endeavoured to present mathematics in this way. [But at bottom it is merely a piece of subtlety.]",["*Spitzfindigkeit* rendered 'subtlety' in the sense of hair-splitting."]],"dohna-s25-012":["The way of teaching by tables: a kind of tree which has many main and subsidiary branches.",[]],"dohna-s25-013":["Science is a mode of knowledge which is derived from certain principles and is connected in a system.",["*Erkenntnis* = 'mode of knowledge'."]],"dohna-s25-014":["The Socratic way of teaching; the Platonic.",[]],"dohna-s25-015":["A method is either *acroamatica* [where one person alone lectures, without asking or answering] or *erotematica* [by question and answer]. The latter is either catechetical or dialogical. In the first the questioner is always the teacher, in the second the person questioned. The acroamatic method is also the Platonic.",["'Diese' refers to the erotematic method. The statement that the acroamatic method is Platonic follows the transcript as it stands, though it may be a slip for another term."]],"dohna-s25-016":["Philosophical matters ought not to be presented catechetically. Catechesis can be mechanical or *judicieuse* [through association of representations, which cultivates the understanding].",["*judicieuse* kept in the transcript's French form; *Vorstellungen* = representations; *Verstand* = understanding."]],"dohna-s25-017":["Can religion be presented catechetically? [By this it is already led into prejudices.] Strictly, this is not the best method; the dialogical would be. The common man cannot well grasp a connected exposition. Historical modes of knowledge can be presented catechetically.",["'sie' in the bracket taken to refer to religion (or the pupil); doubtful."]],"dohna-s25-018":["Socrates said: I am the midwife of the other's thoughts. He derived everything from their concepts, which they simply could not properly develop. [This kind of catechetical method is the only one which may be employed in philosophical subjects. At bottom it is erotematic catechesis, although it is called dialogical. Catechisms ought to be so arranged: first derive everything from reason, and then build upon it positive religion, for man as he now is.]",["OCR 'entwickehi' corrected to 'entwickeln'.","'ihren Begriffen': 'their' refers to the interlocutors."]],"dohna-s25-019":["One can think tumultuarily and methodically. The latter, when we wish to bring forth a mode of knowledge, is entitled meditating. But before one thinks methodically one must always also think tumultuarily, roaming about, in order to seek out everything that bears on the matter.",[]],"dohna-s25-020":["1. What do I want? [Many who wrote books did not know this at all, or only obscurely.]",[]],"dohna-s25-021":["2. What is it that matters in this?",[]],"dohna-s25-022":["These two points mark the judicious (*judicieus*) man.",[]],"dohna-s25-023":["[Homonyms, expressions that signify many things, are proofs of the poverty of a language.] Designation of knowledge: speaking, writing. But all that the author says on this does not belong to logic. Technical terms (*termini technici*) must not be coined without necessity, though they sometimes belong to scholastic rigour. One does not understand a thing until one can communicate it to others. Words with which one can connect no distinct concept (e.g. *entelechia*) are entitled *termini inanes*. A *conceptus deceptor*, a deceptive concept, usually contains two concepts which are distinct in and for themselves but not intelligible together. [| Concepts | which seem to signify something and in the end signify nothing, such as apathy and idiosyncrasy.] Further, *terminus familiaris* [a current expression]: those which occur in common life.",["OCR stray quotation mark before 'was' omitted.","The apparatus bars in the bracket are carried over as they stand."]],"dohna-s25-024":["[Logomachy, dispute through misunderstanding of words, a verbal dispute. Such a dispute cannot last long; it would long since have been discovered. So, for example, the dispute between | defenders of | freedom and defenders of natural necessity.]",["The apparatus bars are carried over."]],"dohna-s25-025":["Very often people believe that they understand one another only when they are furthest apart.",[]],"dohna-s25-026":["*Terminus ambiguus* [ambiguous], *vagus*: an expression about which one cannot quite make out what one is to think by it, e.g. 'happiness' (*Glück*); such a term can be determined. Proposition (*Satz*): a *judicium verbis prolatum*? No: only assertoric [judgments are] propositions. [A *propositio* is a *judicium categoricum*, hence a species of judgment, and *judicium* is not synonymous with *propositio*.]",["'{Urteile sind)' is the OCR's rendering of an editorial insertion; I render it in square brackets as a supplied word.","*Glück* could also be 'fortune'; 'happiness' chosen from the context of vagueness."]],"dohna-s25-027":["*Syllogismus* is entitled *ratiocinium formale*, not *verbis prolatum*. One must in any case express it in words, aloud or silently. [All that matters is the expression of the concepts (by thought or words).]",["The editorial braces '{durch Denken oder Worte)' are rendered as parentheses."]],"dohna-s25-028":["[What logic is with regard to thinking, style is with regard to the designation of thinking. The logical perfection of style is the scholastic; opposed to it is the aesthetic.]",[]],"dohna-s25-029":["The purity and elegance of style is entitled purism when it is exaggerated. Concinnity. Hybrid words: [illegible OCR 'f,'] *Longimetrie*, half Greek, half Latin. Not talkativeness; *ornatus*; (no sonority). A discourse is *sermo*, or *oratio* when it is solemn. Fluent speech (*Wohlredenheit*) suits every piece of writing. Eloquence (*Beredsamkeit*), *ars persuadendi*, is to win applause for the present moment.",["'Zwitterwörter: f, Longimetrie' is garbled in the OCR; the 'f,' is an unreadable mark and the example is translated as it stands. 'Longimetrie' is doubtful.","*Wohlredenheit* and *Beredsamkeit* distinguished as 'fluent speech' and 'eloquence'; the next passage defines them."]],"dohna-s25-030":["In learned exposition: descendence and coascendence. [Volubility (*Beredtheit*), a copiousness (*copia*) of words; *Wohlredenheit*, the art of applying this *copia*; *Beredsamkeit*, the skill of using this art to lead others according to one's purposes, and to feign what is false as true.]",["'Descendenz und Coascendenz' left as in the German sense (descending and co-ascending order of exposition)."]],"dohna-s25-031":["Sermons must be generally intelligible. A power must prevail in them that acts upon every soul. For they come together out of a common end. A. Hermann Francke says that sermons ought to be so constituted that, if a man has heard only one of them, he finds in it a complete morality. *Condescendenz* and *Coascendenz* are a great gift: to distinguish, and to choose what this hearer and what that hearer requires. Much genius is needed for this, to teach little to one who can grasp little. In a lecture-hall, as in writing a book, one must *coascendieren* [rise to the level of the hearers], and had better provide for the greater capacities, from which something may after all come.",["*Condescendenz* and *Coascendenz* are left in the transcript's Latinate forms; *coascendieren* is likewise left, with a bracketed gloss that is my own.","Restated 'Es muß darin eine Kraft herrschen, die auf jede Seele wirkt' closely; 'Seele' kept as 'soul'.","'A. Hermann Francke': the 'A.' is as printed; its meaning is uncertain (perhaps 'August')."]],"dohna-s25-032":["We have two kinds of exposition [Vortrag].",[]],"dohna-s25-033":["1. Didactic, and 2. polemical. The first is instructive (dogmatic); the second merely frees from errors.",[]],"dohna-s25-034":["We refute another when we disprove his assertion. (To answer [*antworten*] and to reply [*beantworten*] are different.) The burden of proof lies on him who says that the other is in the wrong.",["OCR 'Aviderlegen' corrected to 'widerlegen'; 'hegt ob' to 'liegt ob'; the curly brace before 'antworten' read as a parenthesis.","The distinction between *antworten* and *beantworten* cannot be reproduced in English, so the German is kept in brackets."]],"dohna-s25-035":["In every dispute we have a *thesis* and an *antithesis* — *thesis* (*status*) *controversiae*. The determination [Bestimmung] of the *quaestio* [this often presents great difficulties] is entitled *forma controversiae*. Here one often discovers a *logomachia*, a dispute about words. But a *logomachia* is never wont to last long. —",["'Quaestion' rendered as Latin *quaestio*; 'Logomachie — Wortstreit' given as *logomachia* with the German gloss.","OCR '{status)' read as '(status)'."]],"dohna-s25-036":["Argumentations are divided into",[]],"dohna-s25-037":["1. *argumentationes κατ' ἀλήθειαν*, from objective grounds,",["Greek garbled in OCR ('koct ' aAnOeiccv'); restored as κατ' ἀλήθειαν, which is the evident reading."]],"dohna-s25-038":["2. *argumentationes κατ' ἄνθρωπον*, from subjective grounds [that is, from the assertions of the subject, as e.g. in the Gospel, that spirits exist, because they believed [them] to be ghosts. —]",["Greek garbled in OCR ('Kcnr5 avöpcoiTov'); restored as κατ' ἄνθρωπον.","The Gospel example is obscure in the transcript; translated as it stands, with 'them' supplied in square brackets."]],"dohna-s25-039":["A wager is an agreement to a penalty for him who has erred. The *argumentatio ad crumenam* is quite fair. As to the *argumentum a verecundia*, the monk Abelard said: *Si omnes patres sic — ego non sic*. — An instance [Instanz] is a particular proposition that contradicts a general one. Retorsion is when someone proves conversely, namely, makes out of my disputed proposition a consequence that strikes the opponent. —",["'Mönch Abelard' as in the text. The Latin ('If all the fathers [say] so, I do not') is left untranslated in the text, since the sense is plain."]],"dohna-s25-040":["A *consequentiarius* is one who draws from the theoretical assertions of another consequences that bring danger. [A *consequentiarius* is one who draws from my propositions inferences that are prejudicial to someone's person. —]",["'Folgen' and 'Folgerungen' rendered 'consequences' and 'inferences' respectively."]],"dohna-s25-041":["An apology is a defence against a maker of consequences [*Konsequenzenmacher*]. A formal disputation — in it the *acteurs* are the respondent and the opponent. In order to make it at once easy for the respondent to grasp, the speaking is done in syllogisms. The rule chiefly contains this, that the conclusion of the opponent's argument directly contradicts the *thesis* of the respondent — *per prosyllogismos*. The *praeses*, as assistant to the respondent, seeks to settle the dispute. —",["'Um dem Respondenten es gleich faßlich zu machen' smoothed slightly; 'Assistent' kept as 'assistant'."]],"dohna-s25-042":["That is entitled pragmatic which makes prudent. [It is] the judging of effects from their causes.",["'[It is]' supplied; the German has a bare noun phrase."]],"dohna-s25-043":["A compiler [*Compilator*] is one who makes one book out of many. [It is incomparably harder to express oneself briefly and coherently, e.g. in a letter — a handbook — than at length.] An epitomiser [*Epitomator*] is one who makes a small book out of a large one. [He must be a connoisseur, in order not to violate the spirit of the work even in the abridgement.] A *plagiarius* is a learned thief, who steals another's thoughts.",["'weitläuftig' rendered 'at length'."]],"dohna-s25-044":["The author [Meier] speaks finally of the character of the scholar. A naturalist is one who acquires for himself modes of knowledge [Erkenntnisse] that constitute no system. Metaphysics and morals are the hobby-horses of such people. [One can have naturalists in all fields. —]",["'Der Autor' is Meier, the textbook author, supplied from context; the identification is in brackets and is mine.","'Erkenntnisse' = 'modes of knowledge'. OCR 'Avelcher' corrected to 'welcher'."]],"dohna-s25-045":["An *autodidactos* is one who has science [Wissenschaft] without a teacher [like Lambert], who has either drawn everything from himself or at least has been his own expositor in reading scientific books.",["'Wissenschaft' rendered 'science' in the older sense of systematic learning."]],"dohna-s25-046":["There are really no synonyms in any language. For when words were invented, one certainly wished to designate by each a particular concept, which one will also certainly always find on a closer examination of the word. E.g. *Roß* (the rider's horse), *Pferd* (the genus), *Gaul*, *Mähre* (work-horse), (*Kobbel*, from which comes *caballus*): each carries with it a particular concept.",["German horse-words are kept since English cannot reproduce the distinctions. The glosses in parentheses are as in the transcript; 'Arbeit' is rendered 'work-horse' by judgment."]],"dohna-s25-047":["We forgot the mathematical method earlier, in enumerating the methods. It is no other than the synthetic, which proceeds from the first grounds of a knowledge and ends with the last consequences. The first thing in this method is the definition, then axiom, theorem, problem, and so on.",["'eines Erkenntnisses' rendered 'of a knowledge'."]],"dohna-s25-048":["[Scholium, corollary. Wolff presented philosophy by this method, which cannot be done.]",["Wolff's name and the final clause are as in the transcript."]],"dohna-s25-049":["Note. In conclusion Kant added something further on meditating, or methodical thinking. He said, namely, that what chiefly matters in it (as already mentioned above) are the two points:",["This is the transcriber's own remark, not Kant's words."]],"dohna-s25-050":["1. to know exactly what one really wants, and afterwards",[]],"dohna-s25-051":["2. what it depends on. He then cited, for example, how much trouble it had cost him, when he was occupied with the thought of writing the *Critique of Pure Reason*, to know what he really wanted. In the end he had found that everything could be comprised in the question: Are synthetic propositions *a priori* possible? — Yes. But it depends on our being able to give them a corresponding intuition [Anschauung]. If this cannot be done, however, they do not have this character. From this one sees how greatly meditating is facilitated by this method.",["'Kritik der reinen Vernunft' given in Kemp Smith's title form. 'Eigenschaft' rendered 'character' (the property of being possible)."]],"dohna-s25-052":["[NB. This took place in the *Repetitorium* on Saturday; the lecture-course had already been closed on the Friday before. —]",["'Collegium' rendered 'lecture-course'."]],"dohna-s25-053":["End of Kant's Logic,",[]],"dohna-s25-054":["Saturday, the . . .th of September 1792.",["The day of the month is left blank in the source ('den . . .sten')."]]};
